window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"t":"1.1 What is data communication?","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-1.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.1 What is data communication? Data communication is the exchange of data between two or more devices through a transmission medium. For it to work reliably, three things matter: delivery (to the correct destination), accuracy (data arrives unaltered) and timeliness (data arrives soon enough to be useful, which is vital for voice and video). What \"data\" means here In networking, data is any information that has been turned into a form a computer can store and send: text, numbers, pictures, sound and video. Inside a computer all of it is held as bits (binary digits, 0 or 1). Data communication is the job of moving those bits from one device to another, correctly and quickly enough to be useful. The three goals explained Delivery: the data must reach the right device, and only that device. A message meant for the accounts PC must not end up on a student's laptop. Networks use addresses (MAC and IP addresses, see Unit 4 ) to make this possible. Accuracy: the data must arrive exactly as it was sent. One wrong bit in a bank transfer could change Rs. 1,000 into Rs. 9,000. Networks detect damaged data and either throw it away or ask for it again (see 1.8 ). Timeliness: data must arrive soon enough to be useful. An email that takes ten seconds is fine, but in a voice call, sound that arrives half a second late makes conversation difficult. Data that must be delivered as it is produced, such as live voice or video, is called real-time data. A fourth goal is often added: jitter (uneven delay) must be low. If video packets arrive at irregular intervals, the picture stutters even when the average delay is small (see 1.7 ). Everyday example: sending a WhatsApp message to a friend uses data communication. Your phone (sender) turns the text into bits, sends them through Wi-Fi and the Internet (medium), following agreed rules (protocols), to your friend's phone (receiver). The two ticks are a form of acknowledgement: proof of delivery. Local and remote communication Communication can be local , between devices in the same room or building (a PC printing to an office printer), or remote , between devices that are far apart (a laptop in Kandy loading a website hosted in Singapore). The same basic ideas apply to both; remote communication simply passes through more devices and links on the way. Remember Data communication is the exchange of data (bits) between devices through a transmission medium. It must deliver to the correct destination, accurately and on time. Real-time traffic such as voice and video is the most sensitive to delay and jitter."},{"t":"1.2 Components of a data communication system","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-2.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.2 Components of a data communication system Component Role Example Message The information to send An email, a video Sender Device that originates the message Your laptop Receiver Device that receives the message A web server Medium Physical path of transmission Cable, radio waves Protocol Set of rules both sides follow HTTP, TCP Without a protocol, two devices may be connected but unable to understand each other, just as two people speaking different languages cannot communicate even in the same room. The five components of data communication: a message travels from sender to receiver over a medium, following a protocol Each component in more detail Message: the data itself. It can be small (a few bytes for a chat message) or very large (several gigabytes for a film). Large messages are split into many smaller pieces called packets before sending. Sender and receiver: any device that can send or receive data: computers, phones, printers, cameras, servers, smart TVs. Most devices are both sender and receiver at different moments. When you open a web page, your laptop sends a request and then receives the page. Medium: the physical path. Guided media keep the signal inside a cable (copper or fibre). Unguided media send it through the air as radio waves. Different media have different speeds, distances and costs (see 1.6 ). Protocol: the agreed rules. A protocol defines the format of the message (what each part means), the order of messages (who speaks first), what to do when something goes wrong, and how fast to send. Why protocols matter so much A protocol is like the rules of a conversation. When you phone someone you say \"Hello\", wait for a reply, take turns to speak and say \"Goodbye\" at the end. Without such rules, both people could talk at once, or never know when the call is over. Computer protocols set rules like these, but much more precisely. A protocol defines three things: Element Question it answers Example Syntax What is the format of the data? The first 6 bytes of an Ethernet frame are the destination address Semantics What does each part mean, and what action should follow? A \"404\" in an HTTP reply means the page was not found Timing When should data be sent, and how fast? Wait for an acknowledgement before sending more Protocols are written as standards so that equipment from different manufacturers works together. Important standards organisations include the IEEE (Ethernet and Wi-Fi), the IETF (Internet protocols such as TCP, IP and HTTP), the ISO (the OSI model) and the ITU (telecommunications). Remember Five components: message, sender, receiver, medium and protocol. A protocol defines syntax, semantics and timing. Standards let devices from different vendors communicate."},{"t":"1.3 Data flow (transmission direction)","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-3.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.3 Data flow (transmission direction) Data flow (also called the transmission mode ) describes the direction in which data can travel between two connected devices, and whether both can send at the same time. Simplex: one direction only (keyboard to computer, TV broadcast). Half-duplex: both directions, but one at a time (walkie-talkie, old hubs). Full-duplex: both directions at once (telephone, modern switched Ethernet). Data flow: simplex is one way only, half-duplex takes turns, full-duplex sends both ways at once Each mode explained Simplex is like a one-way street. One device always sends and the other always receives; the receiver can never reply on the same channel. A keyboard only sends to the computer, a radio station only broadcasts to listeners, and a traditional CCTV camera only sends pictures to the recorder. The full capacity of the channel is used in one direction. Half-duplex is like a narrow bridge that only one car can cross at a time. Both devices can send, but they must take turns. A walkie-talkie user presses the button to talk, and while talking cannot hear the other person. Old Ethernet networks built with hubs worked this way: if two computers sent at the same moment, their signals collided and both had to send again. Wi-Fi is also half-duplex on each channel: a device and the access point take turns to transmit. Full-duplex is like a two-lane road with traffic in both directions at once. Both devices send and receive at the same time, so a 1 Gbps full-duplex link can carry 1 Gbps in each direction. A telephone call is full-duplex: both people can speak and hear at once. Modern switched Ethernet is full-duplex, because each cable has separate wire pairs for each direction and the switch gives every device its own link, so there are no collisions. Mode Directions At the same time? Example Simplex One Not applicable Keyboard, TV broadcast Half-duplex Two No, they take turns Walkie-talkie, Wi-Fi, hub-based Ethernet Full-duplex Two Yes Telephone, switched Ethernet Duplex mismatch: if one end of a cable is set to full-duplex and the other to half-duplex, the link still works, but very badly. The half-duplex side sees \"collisions\" whenever both sides send, and performance drops sharply. This is a classic cause of a slow but working connection (see 9.5 ). Leaving both ends on automatic negotiation usually avoids it. Remember Simplex: one way only. Half-duplex: both ways, taking turns. Full-duplex: both ways at once. Modern switched Ethernet is full-duplex and has no collisions."},{"t":"1.4 Analog and digital signals","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-4.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.4 Analog and digital signals Analog signals vary continuously (the human voice, radio waves). Digital signals use discrete levels, typically 0 and 1 (computer data). Digital signals resist noise better because a receiver only needs to decide between two levels. Modulation converts digital data into an analog carrier for transmission over radio or telephone lines; a modem (modulator-demodulator) does this. An analog signal varies smoothly; a digital signal jumps between two levels that stand for 0 and 1 Analog signals in more detail An analog signal is a smooth wave that can take any value within a range. Three properties describe a wave: Amplitude: the height (strength) of the wave. Frequency: how many complete waves pass per second, measured in hertz (Hz) . Wi-Fi uses radio waves at about 2.4 billion (2.4 GHz) or 5 billion (5 GHz) waves per second. Phase: the position of the wave in its cycle at a given moment. The problem with analog signals is noise : any interference adds itself to the wave, and the receiver cannot tell which part is the real signal and which part is noise. Each time an analog signal is amplified, the noise is amplified too. Digital signals in more detail A digital signal has only a few fixed levels, usually two: one for 0 and one for 1. Noise still affects it, but as long as the receiver can still tell \"high\" from \"low\", it recovers the exact original bits. A device called a repeater can then send out a perfectly clean copy (it regenerates the signal rather than just amplifying it). This is why digital communication can cross very long distances without any loss of quality. Modulation: carrying digital data on an analog wave Radio waves and old telephone lines can only carry analog waves, so digital data must be modulated : the bits are used to change one property of a carrier wave. Method What changes Simple description Amplitude modulation (ASK) The height of the wave A tall wave means 1, a short wave means 0 Frequency modulation (FSK) The frequency A faster wave means 1, a slower wave means 0 Phase modulation (PSK) The phase The wave is shifted to show a change of bit Modern systems such as Wi-Fi and 4G combine amplitude and phase changes ( QAM ), so each change of the wave carries several bits at once. That is how they reach such high speeds. At the receiver, demodulation turns the wave back into bits. A device that does both jobs is a modem (*mo*dulator-*dem*odulator). Your home router usually contains one for the DSL, cable or fibre line. Analogy: think of a torch flashing Morse code at night. The light itself is the carrier. Switching it on and off (changing its amplitude) carries the message. Remember Analog: continuous values, easily affected by noise. Digital: a few fixed levels, can be regenerated perfectly. Modulation puts digital data onto an analog carrier wave; a modem modulates and demodulates."},{"t":"1.5 Serial and parallel transmission","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-5.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.5 Serial and parallel transmission Parallel: several bits sent at once on separate wires. Fast over very short distances, but suffers from skew and cost. Serial: bits sent one after another on one channel. Used for almost all long-distance and modern links (USB, SATA, Ethernet). Synchronous transmission uses a shared clock and sends blocks of data; asynchronous transmission sends characters framed by start and stop bits. Serial transmission sends bits one after another on one channel; parallel sends several bits at once on separate wires Why serial won At first, parallel looks faster: eight wires carry eight bits at once. The problem is skew : over a long cable, the bits on different wires arrive at slightly different times, so the receiver mixes up bits from neighbouring bytes. The wires also interfere with each other ( crosstalk ), and a cable with many wires is thick and expensive. Engineers therefore had to keep parallel links short and slow. Serial links avoid both problems. With only one data path (or one pair per direction), there is nothing to get out of step, so the clock rate can be pushed very high. Modern serial links send billions of bits per second. That is why old parallel printer ports and parallel IDE disk cables have been replaced by USB and SATA, which are both serial. Inside a chip, where distances are tiny, parallel buses are still used. Feature Parallel Serial Bits at a time Several (often 8, 16 or 32) One per channel Cable Many wires, thick, expensive Few wires, thin, cheap Distance Very short Short to very long Problems Skew, crosstalk Needs a fast clock and careful timing Examples today Buses inside chips and memory USB, SATA, PCI Express, Ethernet Asynchronous and synchronous transmission The receiver must know exactly when each bit starts. There are two ways to keep it in step: Asynchronous: each character is sent on its own, with a start bit before it and a stop bit after it. The gap between characters can be any length. It is simple and cheap, but the extra start and stop bits waste capacity: 8 data bits need 10 bits on the line, so 20% is overhead. Used by old serial ports, and the console port you will use on a switch in 3.2.1 (9600 baud, 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit). Synchronous: data is sent in large blocks ( frames ), and the sender and receiver keep their clocks aligned, often using timing information carried inside the signal itself. There is no start and stop bit per character, so it is much more efficient. Ethernet, Wi-Fi and almost all modern high-speed links are synchronous. Remember Parallel: many bits at once, but only over short distances because of skew and crosstalk. Serial: one bit after another; used for almost every modern link. Asynchronous framing uses start and stop bits per character; synchronous sends large clocked blocks."},{"t":"1.6 Transmission media","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-6.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.6 Transmission media Medium Type Typical use Advantages Disadvantages Twisted pair (UTP/STP) Guided LAN cabling (Cat5e, Cat6) Cheap, easy Limited to 100 m, interference Coaxial cable Guided Cable TV, older LANs Better shielding Bulky Optical fibre (single-mode, multi-mode) Guided Backbones, ISPs Very high speed, long distance, immune to EMI Costly, fragile to install Radio waves / Wi-Fi Unguided WLAN, mobile Mobility Interference, security risk Microwave / Satellite Unguided Long-haul, remote areas Wide coverage High latency, weather effects Cross-sections of the three guided media: twisted pair, coaxial cable and optical fibre Guided media explained Twisted pair cable contains four pairs of copper wires, with the two wires in each pair twisted around each other. The twisting matters: interference hits both wires of a pair almost equally, and the receiver only looks at the difference between them, so most of the noise cancels out. UTP (unshielded twisted pair) is the normal cable for office and home networks. STP (shielded twisted pair) adds foil shielding for places with a lot of electrical noise, such as factories. Cables come in categories : Cat5e supports 1 Gbps, Cat6 supports 1 Gbps (and 10 Gbps over shorter runs), and Cat6a supports 10 Gbps over the full 100 m. Coaxial cable has one central copper conductor, surrounded by insulation, a braided metal shield and an outer jacket. The shield gives good protection from interference. It is still used for cable TV and cable Internet, but has been replaced by twisted pair in LANs. Optical fibre carries pulses of light through a very thin strand of glass. The light stays inside the core by bouncing off the boundary with the surrounding glass ( total internal reflection ). Multi-mode fibre has a wider core and uses cheaper light sources. It is used for distances up to a few hundred metres, for example between buildings on a campus. Single-mode fibre has a very thin core and uses lasers. It reaches tens of kilometres, and is used by ISPs and undersea cables. Because it carries light, not electricity, fibre is immune to electrical interference and lightning, is very hard to tap without being noticed, and has very low signal loss. Unguided (wireless) media Wireless signals spread through the air, so anyone in range can receive them. This gives mobility and avoids cabling costs, but it brings three issues: interference (from other networks, microwaves and Bluetooth), attenuation (walls and distance weaken the signal), and security (signals can be captured, so they must be encrypted). Radio waves (Wi-Fi, mobile networks, Bluetooth) pass through walls reasonably well. Microwave links need a clear line of sight between dishes, often on towers. They are used to join sites a few kilometres apart. Satellite links cover huge areas, including remote places. Traditional satellites orbit very high (about 36,000 km), which gives high latency (around 600 ms for a round trip). Newer low-orbit satellite services reduce this greatly. Infrared only works over a short, clear path, for example a TV remote. Choosing a medium Ask: How far must it go? How fast must it be? How much electrical noise is there? Is mobility needed? What is the budget? A typical school uses UTP from switches to PCs, fibre between buildings and between floors, and Wi-Fi for laptops and phones. Remember Twisted pair: cheap and common, up to 100 m. Fibre: fastest, longest distance, immune to interference. Wireless gives mobility but suffers from interference and needs encryption. The choice depends on distance, speed, environment, mobility and cost."},{"t":"1.7 Performance measures","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-7.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.7 Performance measures Bandwidth: maximum capacity of a link (bits per second, for example 100 Mbps). Throughput: the actual rate achieved, always at or below bandwidth. Latency (delay): time for data to travel from source to destination. Made up of propagation, transmission, queuing and processing delay. Jitter: variation in latency, harmful to voice and video. Error rate: proportion of bits received incorrectly. Bandwidth is how much data the link can carry per second; latency is how long data takes to cross it Worked example: a 10 MB file is sent over an 8 Mbps link. 10 MB = 80 Mb, so transmission time = 80 / 8 = 10 seconds (ignoring propagation delay and overhead). Bytes or bits? File sizes are measured in bytes ( B ) but link speeds in bits per second ( b ). One byte is 8 bits, so multiply a file size by 8 before dividing by the link speed. Bandwidth and throughput: the motorway analogy Think of a link as a motorway. Bandwidth is the number of lanes: the most traffic it could ever carry. Throughput is how many cars actually get through per hour. Throughput is always lower than bandwidth, because of protocol overhead (headers that are not your data), sharing the link with other users, errors that need resending, and the speed of the slowest link on the path. A 100 Mbps Internet plan might give a download throughput of 85 to 95 Mbps on a good day. The four parts of latency Delay Cause Depends on Propagation The signal travelling along the medium, at nearly the speed of light Distance Transmission Putting all the bits of a packet onto the link Packet size ÷ bandwidth Queuing Waiting in a router's buffer behind other packets How busy the network is Processing The device reading the header and deciding where to send the packet Device speed Latency is usually measured as a round-trip time (RTT) : the time for a message to go and the reply to come back. The ping command shows it in milliseconds. Typical values: a few ms inside a LAN, 20 to 60 ms to a server in the same country, and 150 to 300 ms to the other side of the world. Latency and bandwidth are different. A faster plan (more bandwidth) makes large downloads finish sooner, but it does not make a game feel more responsive. Responsiveness depends on latency, and most of that is the distance to the server. Jitter and error rate Jitter is the variation in delay. If voice packets arrive 20 ms, 90 ms, 25 ms and 70 ms apart, the sound breaks up even though the average is fine. Applications use a small buffer to even it out, at the cost of a little extra delay. The bit error rate compares bits received wrongly with bits sent. Fibre has an extremely low error rate; Wi-Fi in a crowded area has a much higher one. Errors force retransmissions, which reduces throughput. A second worked example A 2 GB video is downloaded over a 50 Mbps link. 2 GB = 2,000 MB = 16,000 Mb (multiply by 8). Time = 16,000 ÷ 50 = 320 seconds, about 5.3 minutes. (Storage sizes are sometimes counted with 1 GB = 1,024 MB; for estimates, 1,000 is close enough.) If the measured throughput is only 40 Mbps, the time becomes 400 seconds. Always work with the same units: bits with bits per second. Try your own numbers in the calculator below. Putting the numbers together When users say \"the network is slow\", measure before guessing: Throughput — how many bits per second you actually move (speed test, file copy timing). Latency — how long a round trip takes ( ping ). Jitter — how much latency varies (hurts calls more than average delay). Loss — percent of packets that never arrive (even 1–2% damages VoIP and games). Bandwidth is the capacity of the pipe; throughput is what you got. Congestion, Wi-Fi interference, server load or a duplex mismatch can make throughput far below the link bandwidth. Everyday example: a 100 Mbps link with 80% utilisation left for you in theory might deliver only 20 Mbps to one PC because Wi-Fi is shared, the AP is far away, or the server itself is slow. Remember Bandwidth = capacity; throughput = measured rate; latency = delay; jitter = uneven delay. Real-time traffic needs low latency, low jitter and low loss — not only high bandwidth."},{"t":"1.8 Error detection basics","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-8.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.8 Error detection basics Noise can flip bits. Common detection methods: parity bit , checksum and Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC) . Ethernet uses CRC in its frame trailer; if the check fails, the frame is discarded and higher layers (TCP) may retransmit. Error detection with CRC: the sender adds a check value; the receiver recalculates it and discards the frame if they differ Why errors happen Electrical noise from motors and power cables, radio interference, a weak signal at the end of a long cable, a damaged connector or a loose plug can each change a 0 into a 1 or a 1 into a 0. A change to a single bit is a single-bit error . Noise that lasts a little longer often damages several bits in a row; this is a burst error , and it is the more common kind on real networks. Parity The sender adds one extra bit so that the total number of 1s is even ( even parity ) or odd ( odd parity ). Data Number of 1s Even parity bit Sent 1011001 4 0 10110010 1110000 3 1 11100001 The receiver counts the 1s. If even parity was agreed and the count is odd, an error has happened. Parity is simple, but if two bits flip, the count is even again and the error is missed. It also cannot tell which bit is wrong. Checksum The sender adds up the data in blocks and sends the total (the checksum) with it. The receiver adds the blocks up again and compares. If the totals differ, the data is damaged. IP, TCP and UDP headers all carry checksums. They are quick to calculate, but they can miss some patterns of errors. CRC (Cyclic Redundancy Check) A CRC treats the data as one very long binary number and divides it by a fixed number agreed in the standard. The remainder of this division is the check value, and it is sent in the frame's trailer (Ethernet calls it the FCS , frame check sequence). The receiver does the same division. If its remainder differs, the frame is corrupted. CRCs detect all single-bit and double-bit errors and almost all burst errors, which is why they are used at the data link layer for every Ethernet and Wi-Fi frame. Detection and correction Approach What happens Used by Detect and discard The damaged frame is dropped; a higher layer notices that it is missing and asks for it again Ethernet with TCP Detect and request again (ARQ) The receiver asks the sender to retransmit TCP, Wi-Fi acknowledgements Forward error correction (FEC) Extra bits let the receiver repair errors without asking again Satellite links, 4G/5G, storage, streaming FEC costs extra bits on every transmission, but it avoids waiting for a resend, which matters when the delay is long (satellite) or the data is live. Remember Parity detects single-bit errors; a checksum adds up the data; a CRC is the strongest common method. Ethernet uses a CRC and discards bad frames; TCP resends the missing data. Forward error correction repairs errors without resending."},{"t":"1.9 Multiplexing and switching","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-9.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.9 Multiplexing and switching Multiplexing lets many signals share one link: FDM (by frequency), TDM (by time slot), WDM (by light wavelength on fibre). Circuit switching: a dedicated path is set up for the whole call (traditional telephone). Packet switching: data is split into packets that travel independently and are reassembled (the Internet). It uses links efficiently and tolerates failures. Time-division multiplexing: several signals share one link by taking turns in time slots Circuit switching reserves one path for the whole call; packet switching lets each packet find its own way Why multiplexing is needed Laying a separate cable for every phone call, TV channel or computer connection would be impossibly expensive. A single fibre can carry far more capacity than any one user needs, so it makes sense to share it. A multiplexer (MUX) combines several signals onto one link, and a demultiplexer (DEMUX) at the other end separates them again. The three methods explained FDM (frequency division): each signal is given its own frequency band, with small gaps between bands. All signals travel at the same time. Radio and TV stations work this way: each station has its own frequency, and your radio tunes to one of them. TDM (time division): each signal gets the whole link, but only for a short time slot , in turn: A, B, C, A, B, C... The slots are so short and repeat so quickly that every signal seems to have a continuous link. Traditional digital telephone systems use TDM. WDM (wavelength division): the fibre version of FDM. Each signal uses a different colour (wavelength) of light, so one fibre can carry dozens of channels, each running at very high speed. Undersea and ISP backbone cables rely on it. Wi-Fi and 4G/5G use advanced methods (such as OFDMA ) that combine frequency and time sharing among many users. Circuit switching in detail Before any data is sent, a complete path is set up from end to end and reserved for this one conversation. It stays reserved until the call ends, even during silences. Advantages: guaranteed capacity and constant, predictable delay, which is good for voice. Disadvantages: time is needed to set up the call; capacity is wasted during silences; and if any link on the path fails, the call drops. Packet switching in detail The data is split into packets. Each packet carries the destination address in its header, and each router on the way decides where to send it next. Packets from many users share the same links, and they may take different routes and arrive out of order; the receiver puts them back in order. Advantages: links are used efficiently, because capacity is only used when there is data to send; if a link fails, packets are sent another way; and many users can share the network. Disadvantages: delay can vary (jitter) when links are busy, and packets can be lost when buffers overflow, so protocols such as TCP must detect losses and resend. Feature Circuit switching Packet switching Path Reserved for the whole call Shared; each packet is routed separately Setup Needed before sending None Use of capacity Wasted during silences Used only when data is sent Delay Fixed Varies with load If a link fails The call drops Packets are rerouted Example Traditional telephone network The Internet, including voice calls over IP Remember Multiplexing shares one link: by frequency (FDM), by time slot (TDM) or by light wavelength (WDM). Circuit switching reserves a path; packet switching shares links among independent packets. The Internet is a packet-switched network. Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 1.10 hands-on lab and the 1.11 review questions ."},{"t":"1.10 Hands-on lab: Measure bandwidth and latency","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-lab.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.10 Hands-on lab: Measure bandwidth and latency Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. Run ping 8.8.8.8 and note the round-trip time (RTT) in milliseconds. Run a speed test and record download and upload speeds. Compare your measured throughput to the bandwidth your ISP advertises and explain any difference. Tip: use the transfer time calculator in 1.7 to work out how long a 1 GB download should take at your measured speed, then time a real download and compare."},{"t":"1.11 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-review.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.11 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. Protocol simplex half-duplex full-duplex modulation bandwidth throughput latency jitter CRC multiplexing packet switching Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. List the five components of a data communication system. Which transmission mode does modern switched Ethernet use, and why is it better than half-duplex? Why is optical fibre preferred for backbone links? A 25 MB file is sent over a 50 Mbps link. How long does it take in the ideal case? State one difference between circuit switching and packet switching. Show answers Message, sender, receiver, transmission medium, protocol. Full-duplex, so that both devices can send and receive at the same time and no collisions occur, giving higher effective throughput. Very high bandwidth over long distances with low signal loss and immunity to electromagnetic interference. 25 MB = 200 Mb; 200 / 50 = 4 seconds . Circuit switching reserves a dedicated path for the whole communication; packet switching splits data into packets that share the network and may take different routes. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 1 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 1: Data Communication Fundamentals","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01.html","x":"Unit 1 Data Communication Fundamentals How data moves between devices: the parts of a communication system, data flow, signals, transmission media, performance, error detection, multiplexing and switching. What you will learn After this unit you can: Define data communication and list its five components. Distinguish transmission modes, data flow directions and signal types. Compare transmission media. Explain bandwidth, throughput, latency and the basic ideas of multiplexing and switching. Topics in this unit 1.1 What is data communication? ✓ 1.2 Components of a data communication system 1 diagram ✓ 1.3 Data flow (transmission direction) 1 diagram ✓ 1.4 Analog and digital signals 1 diagram ✓ 1.5 Serial and parallel transmission 1 diagram ✓ 1.6 Transmission media 1 diagram ✓ 1.7 Performance measures 1 diagram · interactive lab ✓ 1.8 Error detection basics 1 diagram ✓ 1.9 Multiplexing and switching 2 diagrams ✓ 1.10 Hands-on lab: Measure bandwidth and latency ✓ 1.11 Review: key terms and self-check 5 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"2.1 What is a computer network?","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-1.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.1 What is a computer network? A computer network is a set of devices (hosts) connected by links so that they can share resources and exchange data. Main purposes: Resource sharing: files, printers, internet connection. Communication: email, messaging, voice and video. Centralised management: one place to manage users, security and backups. Reliability and scalability: redundancy and the ability to grow. Hosts, network devices and links Hosts (end devices) are where data starts and finishes: PCs, laptops, phones, printers, servers and IP cameras. Each host needs a network interface and an address. Intermediary devices pass data along the way: switches, routers, access points and firewalls (see 3.1 ). Users rarely see them, but every message goes through them. Links (media) connect everything: copper cables, fibre and radio (see 1.6 ). Each purpose explained Resource sharing. One printer can serve a whole office, one Internet connection can serve hundreds of users, and files kept on one server can be used by everyone who needs them. This saves money and avoids keeping many copies of the same file, all slightly different. Communication. Email, instant messaging, video calls and shared calendars all need a network. Today most businesses could not operate for a single day without them. Centralised management. With a network, an administrator can create user accounts, apply security settings, install updates and back up data for hundreds of computers from one place, instead of visiting each machine. Reliability and scalability. If one server fails, another can take over; if a link breaks, traffic can take another path. When the organisation grows, new devices are simply connected to the existing network. Costs and risks Networks also bring costs and risks that must be planned for: equipment and cabling, skilled staff to run them, and security threats , because every connected device is a possible way in for an attacker. A single fault in a central device can also stop many people working at once. Good design (see 2.8 ) and security (see Unit 8 ) reduce these risks. Remember A network is made of hosts, intermediary devices and links. Networks share resources, enable communication, centralise management and improve reliability. They also need investment, skills and security. Hosts, devices and links in one picture A host (PC, phone, server) runs applications. Intermediary devices (switches, routers, APs, firewalls) forward or filter traffic. Links are the cables or radio paths between them. Protocols agree how bits are framed, addressed and delivered so equipment from different vendors can interoperate. Remember Network = hosts + intermediary devices + media, speaking shared protocols. Switches forward within a LAN; routers connect different networks."},{"t":"2.2 Classification by geographic scale","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-2.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.2 Classification by geographic scale Type Full name Range Example PAN Personal Area Network A few metres Bluetooth headset and phone LAN Local Area Network Room, building, campus School computer lab MAN Metropolitan Area Network City City-wide cable or fibre network WAN Wide Area Network Country or world The Internet, bank branch links Networks classified by size: a PAN fits inside a LAN, LANs join into a MAN, and WANs span countries Each type in more detail PAN (Personal Area Network). The devices around one person, within a few metres: a phone with wireless earbuds, a smartwatch and a laptop. Usually Bluetooth or USB. LAN (Local Area Network). A network in one building or campus, owned and managed by one organisation: a school, an office or your home. LANs are fast (1 Gbps to each desk is normal), cheap per connection, and use Ethernet and Wi-Fi. Because the organisation owns all the equipment, it has full control. MAN (Metropolitan Area Network). It covers a city, for example a university with several campuses across a city, or a city-wide fibre network run by a service provider. It often joins several LANs together. WAN (Wide Area Network). It covers a country or the world. Organisations rarely own the long-distance links; they rent them from telecom companies or ISPs, or connect over the Internet using VPNs (see Unit 6 ). A bank joining its head office in Colombo to branches across Sri Lanka uses a WAN. The Internet is the largest WAN of all. Feature LAN WAN Area Building or campus Country or world Ownership The organisation itself Mostly rented from providers Speed Very high (1 to 10 Gbps) Lower and more expensive per Mbps Delay Very low (under 1 ms) Higher (tens to hundreds of ms) Main technologies Ethernet, Wi-Fi Fibre leased lines, MPLS, broadband, 4G/5G, satellite You may also meet WLAN (wireless LAN), CAN (campus area network, several buildings of one organisation) and SAN (storage area network, a fast network that connects servers to shared disk storage). They are variations of the four main types. Remember PAN, LAN, MAN and WAN are classified by the area they cover. An organisation owns and controls its LAN; WAN links are usually rented."},{"t":"2.3 Classification by role","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-3.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.3 Classification by role Peer-to-peer (P2P): every computer is both client and server. Cheap and simple, but difficult to secure and manage; suitable for fewer than about ten computers. Client-server: dedicated servers provide services (files, web, email, DNS) to clients . Offers centralised security, backup and control; requires a more skilled administrator and higher cost. This is the model on which the rest of this module is built. Peer-to-peer: every computer is both client and server. Client-server: a dedicated server provides services to clients Peer-to-peer in detail In a peer-to-peer network (often called a workgroup in Windows), every computer is equal. Each one keeps its own user accounts and decides itself what to share. To share a folder, its owner sets permissions on their own PC. Good for: a home or a very small office with a few computers and no IT staff. Problems as it grows: every user needs an account on every PC they use; there is no single place for backups; a shared file disappears when the PC holding it is switched off; and security depends on every user setting things up correctly. File-sharing apps such as BitTorrent also use a peer-to-peer design, at Internet scale. Client-server in detail In a client-server network, servers are powerful, always-on computers that provide services, and clients are the computers people use, which request those services. Central accounts: with a directory service such as Active Directory, each user has one account that works on every PC (see 5.2 ). Central data: files are stored on a file server, which is backed up every night. Central security: the administrator controls permissions, updates and policies for everyone. Scalability: adding a hundred more users means adding client PCs and perhaps more server capacity, not reconfiguring every machine. The cost is the server hardware and software, and a skilled administrator. The servers are also important single points: if the only file server fails, nobody can open their files, so important servers need redundancy and backups. Feature Peer-to-peer Client-server User accounts On each computer Central (directory service) Data storage Spread across PCs On servers Backup Hard: each PC separately Easy: central Security Weak, depends on each user Strong, centrally managed Cost Low Higher (servers, licences, staff) Suitable size About 10 computers or fewer Any size Everyday example: when you open a website, your browser is a client and the web server is the server . The same model is used by email, online banking and streaming services. Remember Peer-to-peer: every computer is equal; cheap but hard to manage and secure. Client-server: dedicated servers provide central services, security and backup."},{"t":"2.4 Network topologies","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-4.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.4 Network topologies Topology describes how devices are arranged. Physical topology is the actual cabling layout; logical topology is how data flows. The five common topologies. A switched star is used in almost every modern LAN Topology Description Advantages Disadvantages Bus All devices on one shared cable Cheap, little cable Cable fault stops everyone; collisions Ring Each device connects to two neighbours Orderly access One break can disrupt the ring Star All devices connect to a central switch Easy to manage, fault isolation Central device is a single point of failure Mesh (full or partial) Devices interconnect directly Very reliable Expensive; full mesh needs n(n-1)/2 links Tree / Hybrid Combination of the above Scalable Complex The star topology with a switch is by far the most common in modern LANs. Worked example: how many links does a full mesh of 6 devices need? 6 x 5 / 2 = 15 links . Physical and logical topology The physical topology is what you would see if you followed the cables. The logical topology is the path that data actually follows. They can be different: an old hub-based network was a physical star (every cable went to the hub), but a logical bus (the hub repeated every signal to everyone, as if they all shared one cable). Each topology in more detail Bus. All devices connect to one backbone cable, with a terminator at each end. Every device hears every transmission, so only one device can send at a time. If the backbone is cut, the whole network stops. It is now only of historical interest. Ring. Each device connects to exactly two neighbours, forming a circle, and data travels around it. Older ring networks passed a token around: only the device holding the token could send, so there were no collisions. A single break can stop the ring, so many designs use a dual ring that can send traffic the other way around. Rings are still found in some metropolitan fibre networks. Star. Each device has its own cable to a central switch. Adding or removing a device does not disturb anyone else, and a faulty cable only affects one device, which makes faults easy to find. The weakness is the centre: if the switch fails, every device connected to it is cut off. That is why important networks keep a spare switch, or use two switches. Mesh. Devices have several connections to each other, so there are several possible paths. In a full mesh every device connects directly to every other device; in a partial mesh only the important ones do. The Internet's core and the links between important routers are partial meshes, so traffic can go around a failed link. Tree / hybrid. A combination, such as several stars joined to a central backbone. Most large networks are hybrids: stars on each floor, joined by a backbone between floors and buildings. Calculating mesh links In a full mesh of n devices, each device connects to the other n − 1, and each link is shared by two devices, so the number of links is n(n − 1) ÷ 2. Each device needs n − 1 ports. Devices Links Ports per device 4 6 3 6 15 5 10 45 9 50 1,225 49 The number of links grows very quickly, which is why full mesh is only used for a small number of very important devices. Remember Physical topology is the cabling; logical topology is how data flows. Star is the standard for LANs: easy to manage, but the central switch is a single point of failure. A full mesh of n devices needs n(n − 1) ÷ 2 links."},{"t":"2.5 The Internet, intranet and extranet","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-5.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.5 The Internet, intranet and extranet Internet: the global network of networks, connected by ISPs and using TCP/IP. Intranet: a private network of an organisation, using Internet technologies, accessible only to its members. Extranet: an intranet partly opened to outside partners (suppliers, customers) under controlled access. The Internet is public, an intranet is private to the organisation, and an extranet gives selected partners controlled access The Internet in more detail The Internet is not owned by any one organisation. It is made of thousands of independent networks (ISPs, universities, companies and cloud providers) that agree to exchange traffic, all using the TCP/IP protocols (see 3.4.2 ). Large networks connect at Internet exchange points (IXPs) , and undersea fibre cables join the continents. When you visit a website, your traffic usually passes through several of these networks. The World Wide Web is not the same as the Internet. The Web is one service that runs on the Internet (web pages over HTTP and HTTPS). Email, online games, video calls and file transfer are other services that use the same Internet. Intranet An intranet uses the same technologies as the Internet (web servers, browsers, email, TCP/IP), but only the organisation's own staff can use it. Typical content: staff news, policies and forms, the HR leave system, internal phone lists and project sites. Staff reach it from inside the office, or from home through a VPN (see Unit 6 ). Because it is private, it can hold information that must not be public. Extranet An extranet opens selected parts of the intranet to trusted outsiders, after they log in. For example: a supermarket lets its suppliers see stock levels, so that they can deliver before shelves are empty; a university lets partner colleges enter student marks; a manufacturer lets dealers place orders and track deliveries. Access is controlled carefully: each partner sees only the information meant for them. Feature Internet Intranet Extranet Who can use it Anyone Members of the organisation Members plus approved partners Access control Public (some sites need a login) Login, internal network or VPN Login with partner accounts Example Public websites, email Staff portal Supplier ordering portal Remember The Internet is a global network of networks using TCP/IP; the Web is one service on it. An intranet is private; an extranet gives selected outsiders controlled access."},{"t":"2.6 Ethernet and LAN technologies","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-6.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.6 Ethernet and LAN technologies Ethernet (IEEE 802.3) is the dominant wired LAN technology. Common standards: Standard Speed Typical medium 10BASE-T 10 Mbps Cat3/Cat5 UTP 100BASE-TX (Fast Ethernet) 100 Mbps Cat5 UTP 1000BASE-T (Gigabit) 1 Gbps Cat5e/Cat6 UTP 10GBASE-T 10 Gbps Cat6a UTP Ethernet originally used CSMA/CD (listen, send, detect collisions). Modern full-duplex switched Ethernet has no collisions. Wireless LAN (IEEE 802.11, Wi-Fi): uses CSMA/CA because wireless devices cannot detect collisions while transmitting. Key concepts: SSID (network name), access point (AP) , channels (2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands, plus 6 GHz in Wi-Fi 6E), and security modes ( WPA2, WPA3 ; avoid the obsolete WEP). Which Wi-Fi band? 2.4 GHz reaches further and passes through walls better, but it is slower and crowded; 5 GHz and 6 GHz are faster with more channels, but have a shorter range. How Ethernet works today Ethernet sends data in frames . Each frame contains the destination and source MAC addresses (see 4.1 ), a type field that says what is inside (usually an IP packet), the data, and a CRC check value at the end (see 1.8 ). A normal frame carries up to 1,500 bytes of data. CSMA/CD: the old shared-cable method On old shared cables and hubs, only one device could transmit at a time. CSMA/CD (Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Detection) controlled this: Carrier sense: listen first; if someone else is sending, wait. Multiple access: if the line is quiet, any device may start sending. Collision detection: keep listening while sending. If two devices started at the same time, their signals collide; both stop, wait a random time, and try again. The random wait makes it unlikely that they collide again. With a switch and full-duplex links, every device has its own private connection to the switch, so collisions cannot happen and CSMA/CD is not needed. CSMA/CA in Wi-Fi A wireless device cannot listen while it transmits, because its own signal drowns out everything else, so it cannot detect a collision. Wi-Fi therefore tries to avoid collisions instead: it listens, waits a random back-off time even when the channel seems free, then sends, and the receiver replies with an acknowledgement . If no acknowledgement arrives, the sender assumes something went wrong and tries again. Wi-Fi generations Name Standard Bands Typical maximum rate Wi-Fi 4 802.11n 2.4 and 5 GHz Up to 600 Mbps Wi-Fi 5 802.11ac 5 GHz Several Gbps Wi-Fi 6 / 6E 802.11ax 2.4 and 5 GHz (6E adds 6 GHz) Up to about 9.6 Gbps Wi-Fi 7 802.11be 2.4, 5 and 6 GHz Higher still These are the theoretical totals for the access point. A single device usually gets much less, because the airtime is shared and walls and distance reduce the signal. Channels The 2.4 GHz band has only three channels that do not overlap (1, 6 and 11). If neighbouring access points use overlapping channels, they interfere with each other and everyone slows down. The 5 and 6 GHz bands have many more non-overlapping channels, so they suit busy buildings such as schools and offices. Wi-Fi security WEP: broken many years ago, and can be cracked in minutes. Never use it. WPA2: uses strong AES encryption. Safe if the passphrase is long and hard to guess. WPA3: the current standard. It protects against password-guessing attacks even when the passphrase is weak, and is preferred wherever all devices support it. Personal mode uses one shared passphrase; Enterprise mode gives each user their own login through a central server, which is better for organisations. Remember Ethernet sends frames between MAC addresses; modern switched full-duplex Ethernet has no collisions. Wi-Fi uses CSMA/CA with acknowledgements, because radios cannot detect collisions. Use WPA2 or WPA3, never WEP."},{"t":"2.7 Cabling","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-7.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.7 Cabling Twisted pair Ethernet cables are wired to either the T568A or T568B standard. T568A and T568B pin colours. Only the green and orange pairs swap places, so a cable with the same standard at both ends is straight-through Straight-through cable: same standard at both ends. Connects unlike devices (PC to switch). Crossover cable: T568A at one end and T568B at the other. Connects like devices (PC to PC, switch to switch), though most modern devices use Auto-MDIX and do not need it. Maximum UTP segment length is 100 metres . Why there are two standards Both standards use the same eight pins and the same RJ45 connector. The only difference is that the orange and green pairs swap places. Either standard works, but a site should pick one (T568B is the most common in commercial cabling) and use it everywhere, so that every cable is wired the same way. Pin T568A T568B 1 White-green White-orange 2 Green Orange 3 White-orange White-green 4 Blue Blue 5 White-blue White-blue 6 Orange Green 7 White-brown White-brown 8 Brown Brown Why crossover cables existed In 10 and 100 Mbps Ethernet, a PC sends on pins 1 and 2 and receives on pins 3 and 6. A switch does the opposite. With a straight-through cable between a PC and a switch, the PC's \"send\" pins meet the switch's \"receive\" pins, which is correct. Two PCs connected straight-through would both send on 1 and 2 and nobody would hear anything, so a crossover cable swaps the pairs. Gigabit Ethernet uses all four pairs in both directions, and almost all modern ports have Auto-MDIX , which detects the cable type and adjusts automatically. There is also a rollover (console) cable, which is used to connect a PC's serial or USB port to a switch or router's console port for first-time setup (see 3.2.1 ). It is not used for network traffic. Making a cable: step by step Cut the cable to length and strip about 2 to 3 cm of the outer jacket, without cutting the inner wires. Untwist the pairs and arrange them in the T568B order (or T568A). Flatten and straighten the wires, then trim them evenly to about 1.2 cm. Push them into the RJ45 plug with the clip facing away from you, until every wire reaches the end of the plug. The jacket should go inside the plug so that it is gripped. Crimp firmly with the crimping tool. Test with a cable tester: all eight lights should come on in order. The 100 m rule A UTP link from the switch to the device must not be longer than 100 metres: normally up to 90 m of fixed cable in the walls plus up to 10 m of patch cords at the two ends. Longer runs lose too much signal. For longer distances, use fibre or put a switch in between. Common mistakes: untwisting the pairs too far (more than about 1 cm) causes crosstalk; the jacket not reaching inside the plug means the wires pull out; mixing T568A and T568B by accident creates a crossover cable. Remember Same standard at both ends gives a straight-through cable; different standards give a crossover. Modern ports use Auto-MDIX, so a straight-through cable works almost everywhere. A UTP link must not be longer than 100 m."},{"t":"2.8 Basic network design and structured cabling","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-8.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.8 Basic network design and structured cabling Plan for scalability, reliability, security and cost . A common enterprise design has three layers: access (end devices connect), distribution (policy, routing between VLANs) and core (fast backbone). Small networks collapse these into one or two layers. Use structured cabling: work area, horizontal cabling, patch panel, equipment room, backbone. Three-layer enterprise design: access switches connect devices, distribution switches apply policy, the core is a fast backbone The four design goals Scalability: the network can grow without being redesigned. Leave spare switch ports, spare space in racks and cable trays, and spare IP addresses. Reliability (availability): important parts have a backup, so one failure does not stop everyone. Examples: two links from each access switch to the distribution layer, two power supplies, a UPS (uninterruptible power supply). Security: separate users into VLANs (see 3.2.4 ), protect the edge with a firewall, and restrict who can manage the devices. Cost: meet today's needs and allow for sensible growth, without buying far more than is needed. The three layers explained Access layer: where users plug in. Switches with many ports in each wiring closet, plus wireless access points. Features: VLANs, port security, Power over Ethernet (PoE) for phones, cameras and access points. Distribution layer: collects the uplinks from access switches. It routes between VLANs, applies policies (which networks can talk to which), and provides redundant links. Core layer: the high-speed backbone joining the distribution switches, the server room and the Internet connection. Its only job is to move traffic as fast and reliably as possible. A small school or office usually uses a collapsed core : one pair of switches does both the distribution and core jobs. Structured cabling Structured cabling is a standard way of organising the cables, so that changes are easy and faults are quick to find: Work area: the wall socket and patch cord at the user's desk. Horizontal cabling: the fixed cable from the wall socket to the wiring closet on the same floor (up to 90 m). Telecommunications room (wiring closet): the patch panels and access switches for that floor. Backbone (vertical) cabling: usually fibre, between floors and buildings. Equipment room: the main switches, servers and the Internet connection. Label both ends of every cable and every patch panel port, and keep the records up to date. The design process Gather requirements: the number of users and devices, the applications, wireless coverage, security needs, budget and expected growth. Survey the site: the building layout, cable routes, power, and where to put wiring closets and access points. Design: the topology, equipment list, IP addressing plan (see Unit 4 ) and VLANs. Implement: install, configure and test. Document: diagrams, IP addresses, device passwords (stored securely) and labels. Monitor and improve: watch performance and plan upgrades before problems appear. Remember Design for scalability, reliability, security and cost. Access, distribution and core layers; small networks collapse the top two. Structured cabling and good labelling make changes and troubleshooting easy."},{"t":"2.9 Network services at a glance","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-9.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.9 Network services at a glance A typical LAN offers: file and print sharing, DHCP (automatic addressing), DNS (names), web, email, authentication (directory services) and internet access via a gateway. Later units show how to configure each. Each service in a sentence Service What it does for users Where it is configured File sharing Shared folders on a server, with permissions for each group Windows File Server (SMB) or Samba on Linux ( 5.6 ) Print sharing Everyone prints to shared printers through a print server, which queues the jobs Print server role DHCP Gives every device its IP address, mask, gateway and DNS server automatically 3.3.2 and 5.6 DNS Turns names such as intranet.school.lk into IP addresses Unit 7 Web Intranet sites and web applications Apache, Nginx or IIS ( 5.6 ) Email Sending and receiving mail inside and outside the organisation Mail server or a cloud service Authentication (directory) One username and password for every service, with central control of accounts Active Directory or LDAP Internet access Shares the Internet connection, translates private addresses and filters traffic The router (NAT, 4.3.2 ) and firewall ( 8.2 ) How the services work together When a student switches on a lab PC in the morning: DHCP gives the PC an IP address, the gateway address and the DNS server address. The student logs in; the directory service checks the username and password. Their home folder opens from the file server , found by name through DNS . They open the intranet in a browser: DNS finds the web server , which sends the page. They print a document, and the print server sends it to the lab printer. They visit an outside website: the request goes through the gateway , which uses NAT and the firewall to reach the Internet. If any one of these services fails, users notice at once. That is why the following units show how to set up, secure and troubleshoot each one. Remember A LAN's main services are file and print, DHCP, DNS, web, email, authentication and Internet access. The services depend on each other: DHCP and DNS in particular are needed by almost everything else. Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 2.10 hands-on lab and the 2.11 review questions ."},{"t":"2.10 Hands-on lab: Build a small star network","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-lab.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.10 Hands-on lab: Build a small star network Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. In Packet Tracer, place one switch and four PCs; connect them with straight-through cables. Assign IP addresses 192.168.1.1 to 192.168.1.4 with mask 255.255.255.0. Ping between all PCs. Disconnect one PC cable and observe that others still work. Explain why this shows the advantage of the star topology. Sketch the physical and logical topology of the real network at your institution."},{"t":"2.11 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-review.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.11 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. Host LAN WAN peer-to-peer client-server topology star mesh Ethernet CSMA/CD CSMA/CA SSID access point straight-through crossover intranet extranet Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. Name three purposes of a computer network. Give two advantages of client-server over peer-to-peer networks. Why is a star topology preferred in modern LANs? What is its main weakness? How many links are needed for a full mesh of 10 devices? Which cable would you use to connect a switch to another switch on older equipment? Distinguish an intranet from an extranet. Show answers Resource sharing, communication, centralised management/security (also scalability and reliability). Central management and security; easier backup; better scalability and control of resources. Easy to manage and a fault on one cable affects only one device; weakness: the central switch is a single point of failure. 10 x 9 / 2 = 45 links . A crossover cable (modern switches with Auto-MDIX accept a straight-through cable). An intranet is private for an organisation's own users; an extranet extends controlled access to selected outside partners. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 2 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 2: Computer Network Fundamentals","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02.html","x":"Unit 2 Computer Network Fundamentals What a network is and how networks are classified by size and role, topologies, the Internet, intranets and extranets, Ethernet and Wi-Fi, cabling and network design. What you will learn After this unit you can: Define a computer network and describe its purposes. Classify networks by size (PAN, LAN, MAN, WAN) and by role (peer-to-peer, client-server). Compare physical and logical topologies. Explain the Internet, intranets and extranets. Describe wired and wireless LAN technologies and basic network design. Topics in this unit 2.1 What is a computer network? ✓ 2.2 Classification by geographic scale 1 diagram ✓ 2.3 Classification by role 1 diagram ✓ 2.4 Network topologies 1 diagram ✓ 2.5 The Internet, intranet and extranet 1 diagram ✓ 2.6 Ethernet and LAN technologies ✓ 2.7 Cabling 1 diagram ✓ 2.8 Basic network design and structured cabling 1 diagram ✓ 2.9 Network services at a glance ✓ 2.10 Hands-on lab: Build a small star network ✓ 2.11 Review: key terms and self-check 6 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"3.1 Introduction to network devices","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-1.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.1 Introduction to network devices Device OSI layer Function Network Interface Card (NIC) 1-2 Connects a host to the network; has a MAC address Repeater 1 Regenerates a signal to extend distance Hub 1 Repeats incoming bits to all ports (one collision domain); obsolete Bridge 2 Connects two LAN segments, filters by MAC address Switch 2 (some 3) Forwards frames only to the port of the destination MAC; each port is its own collision domain Router 3 Connects different networks and forwards packets by IP address; separates broadcast domains Layer 3 switch 2-3 Switch with routing ability, used between VLANs Wireless Access Point (AP) 1-2 Connects wireless clients to the wired LAN Modem 1 Converts signals for the ISP line (DSL, cable, fibre ONT) Firewall 3-7 Filters traffic by rules Gateway Any Connects networks that use different protocols; commonly the default router Load balancer 4-7 Spreads traffic across several servers Collision domain vs broadcast domain. A collision domain is the area where frames can collide (every hub port shares one; each switch port has its own). A broadcast domain is the area reached by a broadcast frame (a whole switch or VLAN; a router stops broadcasts). A router separates broadcast domains. Each switch port is its own collision domain, but all hub ports share one How a switch learns. A switch reads the source MAC of every frame and records it with the port in its MAC address table . When a frame arrives, it looks up the destination MAC: if known, it sends out that port only (forwarding); if unknown or broadcast, it floods all other ports. A switch learns which MAC address is on which port, then forwards frames only to the right port How a router decides. A router looks at the destination IP of the packet, finds the best match in its routing table , and sends the packet to the next hop. The route used when nothing else matches is the default route (0.0.0.0/0). Hub, switch and router compared These three devices are often confused, because they all have several ports and blinking lights. The difference is how clever they are about where data goes: Feature Hub Switch Router OSI layer 1 (Physical) 2 (Data link) 3 (Network) Looks at Nothing: it just repeats electrical signals MAC addresses in frames IP addresses in packets Sends data to Every port Only the port where the destination is The next router on the best path Joins Devices in one LAN Devices in one LAN Different networks (for example, your LAN and the Internet) Collisions All ports share one collision domain Each port is its own collision domain Each port is its own collision domain Broadcasts Passed to everyone Passed to everyone in the same VLAN Stopped Today Obsolete In every LAN At the edge of every LAN Analogy: a hub is like a person shouting every message across an office, so everyone hears everything. A switch is like an office messenger who learns where everyone sits and delivers each note to the right desk. A router is like the post office: it reads the address on the envelope and sends it on to the right town. Following the switch learning process Suppose PC-A (port 1) sends to PC-C (port 3), and the switch's table is empty: The frame arrives on port 1 from MAC A. The switch records \"A is on port 1\" . It does not know where C is yet, so it floods the frame out of every port except port 1. PC-C replies. The reply arrives on port 3, so the switch records \"C is on port 3\" . The reply is addressed to A, which is already known, so it goes only out of port 1. From then on, all traffic between A and C goes directly between ports 1 and 3, and the other PCs never see it. Entries that are not used for a while (typically 5 minutes) are removed, so the table stays up to date when devices move. Following a routing decision A router's routing table might contain: Destination network Send via 192.168.1.0/24 Directly connected, interface G0/0 10.0.0.0/30 Directly connected, interface G0/1 0.0.0.0/0 (default route) Next hop 10.0.0.2 (the ISP) A packet for 192.168.1.50 matches the first line, so it goes out G0/0 onto the LAN. A packet for 8.8.8.8 matches no specific network, so it follows the default route to the ISP. When several routes match, the router chooses the most specific one (the longest prefix). Routes are either typed in by the administrator ( static routes , see 3.2.3 ) or learned automatically from other routers through routing protocols such as OSPF and BGP. Other devices in more detail Access point: connects wireless devices to the wired LAN. In homes, the router, switch and access point are usually combined in one box. Modem / ONT: changes the signal on the ISP's line (telephone copper, cable or fibre) into Ethernet. A fibre connection uses an ONT (optical network terminal). Firewall: allows or blocks traffic according to rules (see 8.2 ). Load balancer: shares incoming requests among several identical servers, so a busy website stays fast, and keeps working if one server fails. Remember Hubs repeat to everyone; switches forward by MAC address; routers forward by IP address between networks. A switch learns from source MAC addresses and floods frames for unknown destinations. A router picks the most specific matching route, or the default route."},{"t":"3.2 Install and configure network devices","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-2.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.2 Install and configure network devices This section walks through setting up the devices in a small network: planning, first-time access through the console, basic switch and router configuration, VLANs, a wireless access point, and the commands that check your work. The examples use the Cisco IOS command line, which you can practise for free in Cisco Packet Tracer . The small network configured in this section: two PCs, switch SW1, router R1 and a link to the ISP Why learn the command line? Most enterprise switches and routers are configured through a text command-line interface (CLI) , not a graphical screen. The CLI is fast, it can be used over a remote connection, and a configuration can be saved as a text file, copied to another device, compared and documented. Cisco IOS is the most widely taught CLI, and the commands of many other vendors are similar. Running and startup configuration Every Cisco device holds two configurations: the running-config , held in RAM, which is the configuration in use right now. Every command you type changes it at once; the startup-config , held in NVRAM, which is loaded when the device starts. If you make changes and forget to save them with copy running-config startup-config , they are lost when the device restarts. This is one of the most common mistakes beginners make. Key words in this topic Planning : IP scheme, VLANs, passwords and backups before you touch the CLI. Switch vs router config : Layer 2 ports and VLANs versus Layer 3 interfaces and routes. VLAN / trunk : separate broadcast domains; trunks carry many VLANs between switches. Verification : show / ip commands prove the design matches reality. In this section 3.2.1 Planning checklist 3.2.2 Basic switch configuration 3.2.3 Basic router configuration 3.2.4 VLANs and trunk ports 3.2.5 Wireless access point and home router 3.2.6 Verification commands"},{"t":"3.2.1 Planning checklist","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-2-1.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.2.1 Planning checklist Draw the network diagram and list the devices and cables needed. Plan IP addressing (see Unit 4 ) and VLANs if needed. Mount equipment, label cables, connect power and uplinks. Connect to the device console (serial cable and terminal program such as PuTTY at 9600 baud) for first-time setup. Apply configuration, test, save , and document. First-time set-up uses the console port; once an IP address and SSH are configured, the device is managed over the network Planning: why it matters A network that is planned on paper first is quicker to install, has fewer faults and is much easier to troubleshoot later. For each device, write down its name, location, management IP address, the purpose of each port, and its VLANs. Draw two diagrams: a physical diagram (where devices are and how the cables run) and a logical diagram (subnets, VLANs and IP addresses). A sample addressing and device plan Device Name Management IP Notes Router R1 192.168.1.1 Default gateway for the LAN; G0/1 to the ISP Switch SW1 192.168.1.2 Ports 1 to 12 STAFF, 13 to 23 STUDENTS, port 24 uplink Server SRV1 192.168.1.10 DNS and DHCP PCs - From DHCP 192.168.1.100 to .200 A consistent naming scheme, such as building, floor and device ( B1-F2-SW1 ), lets anyone find a device from its name alone. First access through the console A new switch or router has no IP address, so you cannot reach it over the network. Instead you connect to its console port : Connect a console cable from your PC (a USB-to-serial adapter, or a USB console cable if the device has a USB console port) to the console port. Open a terminal program such as PuTTY, choose Serial , select the COM port shown in Device Manager, and set 9600 baud, 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit, no flow control. Press Enter. You should see the device's prompt, such as Switch> . After the device has an IP address and SSH is set up (see 3.2.2 ), you can manage it from anywhere on the network, and only need the console again for recovery. Installing the hardware Mount devices in a rack with enough space for air to flow, and connect them to a UPS. Use patch panels so that the fixed cabling is never plugged directly into switches. Label both ends of every cable, and record which switch port each wall socket uses. Keep power and data cables apart to reduce interference. After configuring: test, save and document Test every function you configured (ping between devices, test SSH, check VLAN membership). Save the configuration on the device and keep a backup copy of it on a server. Update the documentation with any change. Good documentation turns a two-hour fault into a ten-minute fix. Remember Plan first: diagrams, names, IP addresses and VLANs. First-time setup uses the console port at 9600 baud. Test, save, back up and document every change."},{"t":"3.2.2 Basic switch configuration","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-2-2.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.2.2 Basic switch configuration Cisco IOS CLI Copy Switch> enable Switch# configure terminal Switch(config)# hostname SW1 SW1(config)# enable secret StrongPass123 SW1(config)# service password-encryption SW1(config)# banner motd # Authorized access only # SW1(config)# interface vlan 1 SW1(config-if)# ip address 192.168.1.2 255.255.255.0 SW1(config-if)# no shutdown SW1(config-if)# exit SW1(config)# ip default-gateway 192.168.1.1 SW1(config)# line console 0 SW1(config-line)# password ConPass123 SW1(config-line)# login SW1(config-line)# exit SW1(config)# ip domain-name school.lk SW1(config)# username admin secret AdminPass123 SW1(config)# crypto key generate rsa modulus 2048 SW1(config)# ip ssh version 2 SW1(config)# line vty 0 4 SW1(config-line)# login local SW1(config-line)# transport input ssh SW1(config-line)# end SW1# copy running-config startup-config SSH needs more than transport input ssh . The switch also needs a domain name, an RSA key pair and a local user account, and the VTY lines must use login local so that SSH logins are checked against that user. A plain line password is not enough for SSH. The configuration explained line by line Command What it does enable Moves from user mode ( > ) to privileged mode ( # ), where you can view and save configurations configure terminal Enters global configuration mode, where settings are changed hostname SW1 Names the device; the name appears in the prompt and makes it easy to identify enable secret ... Sets the password needed for enable . It is stored as a strong hash, so it cannot be read from the configuration service password-encryption Hides the other passwords in the configuration with weak encryption, so they cannot be read over someone's shoulder banner motd Shows a legal warning to everyone who connects interface vlan 1 and ip address ... Gives the switch a management IP address. A Layer 2 switch needs it only so that administrators can reach it ip default-gateway Lets the switch reply to administrators on other networks line console 0 , password , login Protects the console port with a password ip domain-name , crypto key generate rsa Creates the encryption keys that SSH needs username admin secret ... Creates a local user account for SSH logins line vty 0 4 , login local , transport input ssh The VTY lines are the five remote-login sessions; they check the local user account and accept only SSH (Telnet is refused) copy running-config startup-config Saves the configuration so that it survives a restart Why SSH and not Telnet? Telnet sends everything, including the password , as plain text. Anyone capturing traffic on the network can read it. SSH encrypts the whole session. transport input ssh makes sure that Telnet cannot be used at all. Securing the switch ports Unused ports are a risk: anyone could plug a laptop into a free socket and join the network. Good practice: Cisco IOS CLI Copy SW1(config)# interface range fastEthernet 0/13 - 23 SW1(config-if-range)# shutdown SW1(config-if-range)# exit Port security can also limit each port to the MAC address of the device that should be connected, and shut the port down if a different device appears. Test before you log out. After changing VTY or SSH settings, keep your current session open and try a new SSH login. If it fails, you can still fix the problem. If you close the session first, you may need to walk to the device and use the console. Remember Name the device, set an enable secret , protect the console and allow only SSH on the VTY lines. SSH needs a domain name, RSA keys, a local user and login local . Shut down unused ports and save the configuration."},{"t":"3.2.3 Basic router configuration","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-2-3.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.2.3 Basic router configuration Cisco IOS CLI Copy Router> enable Router# configure terminal Router(config)# hostname R1 R1(config)# interface gigabitEthernet 0/0 R1(config-if)# ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0 R1(config-if)# no shutdown R1(config-if)# exit R1(config)# interface gigabitEthernet 0/1 R1(config-if)# ip address 10.0.0.1 255.255.255.252 R1(config-if)# no shutdown R1(config-if)# exit R1(config)# ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 10.0.0.2 R1(config)# end R1# copy running-config startup-config R1# show ip route R1# show ip interface brief The prompt tells you where you are: R1> user mode, R1# privileged mode, R1(config)# global configuration, R1(config-if)# interface configuration. exit goes back one level; end returns straight to R1# , where show and copy commands are run. What this configuration does Interface G0/0 connects to the LAN. Its address, 192.168.1.1, becomes the default gateway for every PC on 192.168.1.0/24. Interface G0/1 connects to the ISP using a small /30 network (10.0.0.0/30), which has exactly two usable addresses: .1 for R1 and .2 for the ISP router (see 4.4 ). no shutdown is essential. Router interfaces are shut down by default , so an interface with an address but no no shutdown stays down. ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 10.0.0.2 is a static default route : \"for any destination I do not know, send the packet to 10.0.0.2\". Without it the router only knows its own two networks, and cannot reach the Internet. Reading show ip interface brief Text Copy Interface IP-Address OK? Method Status Protocol GigabitEthernet0/0 192.168.1.1 YES manual up up GigabitEthernet0/1 10.0.0.1 YES manual up up GigabitEthernet0/2 unassigned YES unset administratively down down Status / Protocol Meaning What to check up / up Working Nothing administratively down / down The interface has been shut down Enter no shutdown down / down No physical link The cable, the device at the other end, the port up / down Physical link exists, but the link protocol is failing Settings at both ends (such as encapsulation) Reading show ip route The routing table marks each route with a code. C means a directly connected network, L is the router's own local address on that network, S is a static route, and S* marks the default route, which is shown as the gateway of last resort . If a network is missing from the table, the router cannot reach it. Static or dynamic routing? Static routes Dynamic routing protocols (OSPF, EIGRP, BGP) Typed in by the administrator Learned automatically from other routers Simple, predictable, no extra traffic Adapt automatically when a link fails Must be changed by hand when the network changes More complex to configure, and use some CPU and bandwidth Good for small networks and default routes Needed for medium and large networks Remember Give each interface an address and no shutdown ; the LAN interface is the hosts' default gateway. A default route (0.0.0.0/0) sends unknown destinations to the ISP. Check with show ip interface brief and show ip route ."},{"t":"3.2.4 VLANs and trunk ports","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-2-4.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.2.4 VLANs and trunk ports A VLAN divides one physical switch into several logical networks (for example, Staff and Students), improving security and reducing broadcast traffic. VLANs split one switch into separate logical networks; a trunk port carries several VLANs between switches with 802.1Q tags Cisco IOS CLI Copy SW1(config)# vlan 10 SW1(config-vlan)# name STAFF SW1(config-vlan)# exit SW1(config)# vlan 20 SW1(config-vlan)# name STUDENTS SW1(config-vlan)# exit SW1(config)# interface fastEthernet 0/1 SW1(config-if)# switchport mode access SW1(config-if)# switchport access vlan 10 SW1(config-if)# exit SW1(config)# interface fastEthernet 0/24 SW1(config-if)# switchport mode trunk SW1(config-if)# end Ports that carry several VLANs between switches are trunk ports (IEEE 802.1Q tagging). Why use VLANs? Without VLANs, every device connected to the switches is in one large network: one broadcast domain. That causes three problems: Security: student PCs can reach staff PCs and servers directly. Broadcast traffic: every broadcast (such as ARP and DHCP requests) reaches every device, which wastes bandwidth and CPU as the network grows. Management: grouping users by department means rewiring whenever someone moves. With VLANs, each group gets its own logical network, even on shared switches. Moving a user to another department is a one-line change on the switch port. Access ports and trunk ports An access port belongs to exactly one VLAN and connects an end device such as a PC or printer. The PC does not know that VLANs exist; it sends and receives normal frames. A trunk port carries traffic for many VLANs over one link, usually between switches, or from a switch to a router. To keep the VLANs apart, each frame is given a small 802.1Q tag containing its VLAN number. The receiving switch reads the tag, removes it, and sends the frame only to ports in that VLAN. One VLAN on a trunk, the native VLAN (VLAN 1 by default), is sent untagged . For security, many administrators change the native VLAN to an unused VLAN number. VLANs and IP subnets Each VLAN is normally given its own IP subnet , for example VLAN 10 STAFF = 192.168.10.0/24 and VLAN 20 STUDENTS = 192.168.20.0/24. Devices in different VLANs cannot talk to each other unless a Layer 3 device routes between them. This is done either by a router with one trunk link divided into sub-interfaces ( router-on-a-stick ), or by a Layer 3 switch . That router is also where you can apply rules, for example \"students may reach the web server but not the staff file server\". Checking VLANs show vlan brief lists each VLAN and its access ports. Trunk ports are not listed there; use show interfaces trunk to see which ports are trunks and which VLANs they carry. Common mistakes: forgetting switchport mode access (the port may then negotiate to become a trunk); putting a PC in the wrong VLAN, so it gets no address or the wrong one; a trunk that does not allow a VLAN, so devices on another switch cannot reach it. Remember A VLAN splits one switch into separate logical networks (separate broadcast domains). Access ports carry one VLAN; trunk ports carry many, using 802.1Q tags. Each VLAN is its own subnet, and a router or Layer 3 switch is needed between them."},{"t":"3.2.5 Wireless access point and home router","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-2-5.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.2.5 Wireless access point and home router Connect to the admin page (for example 192.168.1.1 ) and change the default admin password . Set a meaningful SSID and choose WPA2 or WPA3 with a strong passphrase. Choose the least crowded channel; enable 5 GHz where possible. Disable WPS and remote administration unless needed. Update firmware. Why each step matters Change the default admin password. Default usernames and passwords (such as admin/admin) are published online. Anyone on your network could log in and take control of the router. Use a meaningful SSID, and WPA2 or WPA3. WPA2 and WPA3 encrypt the traffic so it cannot be read over the air. Use a long passphrase (at least 12 characters, ideally a short sentence). Avoid putting your name or address in the SSID. Choose a channel. On 2.4 GHz use 1, 6 or 11, whichever is least used by your neighbours. A free Wi-Fi analyser app on a phone shows the nearby networks. Use 5 GHz where devices support it: it is faster and less crowded. Disable WPS. WPS (the \"push button\" or PIN setup) has known weaknesses, and its PIN can be guessed. Disable remote administration. Then the router's admin page cannot be reached from the Internet. Update the firmware. Updates close security holes that attackers already know about. Extra good practice Create a separate guest network for visitors. It gives them Internet access but keeps them away from your own computers, printers and cameras. Place the access point centrally and high up, away from thick walls, metal and microwave ovens. In a larger building, use several access points with the same SSID and security, on non-overlapping channels, so that devices can move between them (roaming). Businesses usually manage many access points from a central controller . Use WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise in organisations, so that each user logs in with their own account and a leaver's access can be removed without changing the password for everyone. A home router is several devices in one box Part Job Router Joins your home network to the ISP Switch The LAN ports at the back Access point The Wi-Fi DHCP server Gives addresses to your devices ( 3.3.2 ) NAT Lets all devices share one public address ( 4.3.2 ) Firewall Blocks unwanted incoming connections Modem / ONT (often) Connects to the DSL, cable or fibre line Remember Change default passwords, use WPA2/WPA3 with a long passphrase, and keep firmware updated. Disable WPS and remote administration; use a guest network for visitors."},{"t":"3.2.6 Verification commands","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-2-6.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.2.6 Verification commands Command Purpose show running-config View active configuration show mac address-table See learned MAC addresses show vlan brief List VLANs and ports show ip route View routing table show interfaces status Link state of ports What each command tells you show running-config : the full configuration in use. Check that what you typed was really accepted (a typing mistake may have been rejected without you noticing). show mac address-table : which MAC addresses the switch has learned, on which ports and in which VLANs. Use it to find which port a device is plugged into. show vlan brief : each VLAN with its name and access ports. Check that every port is in the right VLAN. show ip route : the routes a router knows. If the destination network is missing, packets for it are dropped (or follow the default route). show interfaces status : every switch port with its state (connected or notconnect), VLAN, duplex and speed. It is the quickest way to spot an unplugged cable or a duplex mismatch. More useful commands Command Purpose show ip interface brief IP address and up/down state of every interface show interfaces trunk Which ports are trunks and which VLANs they carry show version Model, software version, uptime and the reason for the last restart show cdp neighbors Directly connected Cisco devices, and the port that connects to each show startup-config The saved configuration, to compare with the running one ping and traceroute Test reachability from the device itself Verification is a habit After every change, verify it, before you move on. Change one thing, test it, then make the next change. If something breaks, you then know exactly which change caused it. Long show output can be filtered: for example, show running-config | include hostname shows only the lines that contain \"hostname\". Tip: on a router, the do keyword runs a show command without leaving configuration mode, for example R1(config)# do show ip interface brief . Remember show commands prove that the configuration works; use them after every change. show interfaces status and show ip interface brief quickly reveal ports that are down."},{"t":"3.3 Network protocols","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-3.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.3 Network protocols A protocol is a set of rules that defines the format, order and actions for communication. Protocols are grouped into a protocol suite ; the Internet uses the TCP/IP suite . Protocol Layer (TCP/IP) Purpose Port HTTP / HTTPS Application Web pages (HTTPS adds TLS encryption) 80 / 443 FTP / SFTP Application File transfer (SFTP is secure, runs over SSH) 21 / 22 SSH Application Secure remote command line 22 Telnet Application Insecure remote command line 23 SMTP Application Sending email 25 / 587 POP3 / IMAP Application Retrieving email 110 / 143 (secure: 995 / 993) DNS Application Name to IP resolution 53 DHCP Application Automatic IP configuration 67 / 68 (UDP) SNMP Application Monitoring network devices 161 (UDP) RDP Application Windows remote desktop 3389 TCP Transport Reliable, connection-oriented delivery - UDP Transport Fast, connectionless delivery - IP (IPv4/IPv6) Internet Logical addressing and routing - ICMP Internet Error and diagnostic messages (ping) - ARP Link/Internet Finds MAC address for an IP address - Ethernet, Wi-Fi Network access Frame delivery on local links - Ports: how a protocol finds the right application A server can run a web server, a mail server and SSH at the same time, all on one IP address. The port number in each segment tells the operating system which application the data is for: 443 for HTTPS, 22 for SSH, and so on. The table above lists the standard ports. They are explained further in 5.7 . Secure and insecure versions Several older protocols send everything, including passwords, as plain text: Telnet, FTP, HTTP, POP3 and IMAP without TLS. Each has a secure replacement: SSH, SFTP, HTTPS, and POP3/IMAP over TLS (ports 995 and 993). Always use the secure version, especially across the Internet or on Wi-Fi. Protocols used every day HTTP/HTTPS: fetches web pages and powers most apps. HTTPS adds TLS encryption (see 8.5 ). DNS: turns names into addresses before almost every connection (see Unit 7 ). DHCP: gives each device its address when it joins the network. SMTP, IMAP and POP3: SMTP sends mail (and passes it between mail servers); IMAP and POP3 let a mail program collect it. IMAP keeps mail on the server so that it stays in step on every device; POP3 usually downloads and removes it. ICMP: carries error and test messages. ping and traceroute use it. ARP: finds the MAC address that belongs to an IP address on the local network (see 4.1 ). Key words in this topic TCP : reliable, connection-oriented, ordered delivery. UDP : lightweight, no connection setup, no guaranteed delivery. DHCP : automatic IPv4 host configuration (DORA: Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge). In this section 3.3.1 TCP and UDP 3.3.2 DHCP and the DORA process"},{"t":"3.3.1 TCP and UDP","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-3-1.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.3.1 TCP and UDP Feature TCP UDP Connection Yes (three-way handshake: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) No Reliability Acknowledgements, retransmission, ordering None Speed / overhead Slower, larger header Faster, small header Used for Web, email, file transfer DNS queries, VoIP, video streaming, games TCP opens every connection with a three-way handshake before any data is sent TCP numbers and acknowledges every segment and resends lost ones; UDP just sends datagrams with no acknowledgements TCP in more detail TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) gives applications a reliable stream : the bytes arrive complete, without errors, and in the right order, or the application is told the connection has failed. Three-way handshake: before data is sent, the client sends SYN (\"I want to connect\"), the server replies SYN-ACK (\"agreed, and I want to connect too\"), and the client sends ACK (\"confirmed\"). Both sides now agree on the starting sequence numbers. Sequence numbers: every byte is numbered, so the receiver can put segments back in order and notice any that are missing. Acknowledgements and retransmission: the receiver acknowledges what it has received. If the sender gets no acknowledgement in time, it sends the data again. Flow control: the receiver advertises how much buffer space it has left (the window ), so a fast sender cannot overwhelm a slow receiver. Congestion control: TCP slows down when packets are lost, because loss usually means the network is overloaded, and speeds up again gradually. Closing: each side sends FIN and receives an ACK, so the connection ends tidily. UDP in more detail UDP (User Datagram Protocol) just sends each message (a datagram ) and forgets it. There is no handshake, no acknowledgement, no retransmission and no ordering. Its header is only 8 bytes, against at least 20 for TCP. That sounds worse, but it is exactly what some applications need: Live voice and video: a sound packet that arrives late is useless, so resending it would only add delay. It is better to skip it and carry on. Online games: the newest position of a player matters, not an old one. DNS queries: one short question and one short answer. If the answer does not come, the program simply asks again, which is quicker than setting up a TCP connection first. DHCP: the client has no IP address yet, so it cannot set up a TCP connection. An application that uses UDP and still needs some reliability adds it itself. For example, QUIC , the transport used by HTTP/3, runs over UDP and adds its own encryption and retransmission. Choosing between them The application needs... Use Every byte, in order (web pages, files, email, database queries) TCP Low delay, and can tolerate some loss (voice, video, games) UDP A quick single question and answer (DNS) UDP To send to many receivers at once (multicast streaming) UDP (TCP cannot multicast) Analogy: TCP is like a registered parcel: it is tracked, signed for, and sent again if it is lost. UDP is like dropping postcards in a postbox: cheap and quick, but you are never told whether they arrived. Remember TCP: connection-oriented, reliable and ordered, with a handshake, acknowledgements, flow and congestion control. UDP: connectionless and fast with a tiny header; used where speed matters more than perfect delivery."},{"t":"3.3.2 DHCP and the DORA process","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-3-2.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.3.2 DHCP and the DORA process A client with no address broadcasts D iscover; the server replies O ffer; the client sends R equest; the server sends A cknowledgement carrying the IP address, mask, gateway, DNS server and lease time. DHCP in four messages: Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge (DORA) Why DHCP? Configuring every device by hand is slow and error-prone: a single mistyped address or a duplicated address stops a device working. With DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol), a device joining the network is configured automatically in about a second, and the administrator controls all the settings from one place. The four messages in detail Discover: the new client has no IP address and does not know where the server is, so it broadcasts to the whole LAN: \"Is there a DHCP server?\" It uses source address 0.0.0.0 and destination 255.255.255.255, from UDP port 68 to port 67. Offer: a DHCP server replies with an offer: \"You can have 192.168.1.105.\" Request: the client broadcasts that it accepts that offer. It is sent as a broadcast so that, if several servers made offers, the others know they were not chosen. Acknowledge: the chosen server confirms, and sends the full settings: IP address, subnet mask, default gateway, DNS servers and lease time. Leases The address is leased , not given for ever. When half the lease time has passed, the client asks the same server to renew it. If the device leaves the network, its lease eventually expires and the address goes back into the pool for someone else. Typical lease times are 8 hours for guest Wi-Fi and 8 days for office PCs. Settings on a DHCP server Setting Meaning Example Scope (pool) The range of addresses that can be handed out 192.168.1.100 to 192.168.1.200 Exclusions Addresses in the range that must not be handed out Addresses used by printers Reservation Always give the same address to one device, identified by its MAC address The office printer always gets .50 Options The extra settings sent with the address Gateway 192.168.1.1, DNS 192.168.1.10 Lease time How long an address can be kept before renewing 8 days DHCP across routers Routers do not forward broadcasts, so a Discover never leaves its own subnet. If the DHCP server is on a different subnet (common when one central server serves many VLANs), the router on each subnet is configured as a DHCP relay (on Cisco, the ip helper-address command). It picks up the broadcast and forwards it as a normal unicast message to the server. When DHCP fails If no server answers, a Windows client gives itself an APIPA address in 169.254.0.0/16. It can only talk to other APIPA devices, not to the Internet. A 169.254 address is therefore a clear sign of a DHCP problem (see 9.5 ). Another risk is a rogue DHCP server : a home router plugged into the office network hands out wrong addresses. Managed switches can block this with DHCP snooping . Remember DHCP gives out IP address, mask, gateway and DNS automatically through Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge. Addresses are leased; reservations always give a device the same address. A relay (ip helper-address) is needed when the server is on another subnet."},{"t":"3.4 Reference models","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-4.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.4 Reference models A reference model divides communication into layers so that vendors can build interoperable products and engineers can troubleshoot one layer at a time. Analogy: sending data is like posting a letter. You write the letter (application), put it in an envelope with the address (network layer), the post office sorts it (routers) and a van carries it (physical). The receiver reverses every step. Why layers? Networking is a very big problem. Layering splits it into smaller jobs, each handled by one layer, which only needs to talk to the layer directly above and below it. This brings three benefits: Interoperability: a web browser from one company works over a Wi-Fi card from another and a router from a third, because each follows the standard for its own layer. Independent change: you can replace Wi-Fi with Ethernet (lower layers) without changing the browser (top layer). Troubleshooting: engineers can test one layer at a time. \"The cable is fine, the IP address is fine, so the problem must be DNS\" is layered thinking (see 9.1 ). Two models The OSI model (7 layers) is a reference model used for teaching and troubleshooting. Its layer numbers are used every day: people say \"Layer 2 switch\" or \"a Layer 7 firewall\". The TCP/IP model (4 layers) describes the protocols the Internet actually uses. Key words in this topic OSI 7-layer model : teaching and troubleshooting map. TCP/IP model : the model the Internet actually implements. Encapsulation : data gains headers (and sometimes trailers) as it moves down the stack. In this section 3.4.1 The OSI model 3.4.2 The TCP/IP model and OSI comparison 3.4.3 Encapsulation"},{"t":"3.4.1 The OSI model","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-4-1.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.4.1 The OSI model # Layer Function Data unit (PDU) Examples 7 Application Network services to applications Data HTTP, DNS, SMTP 6 Presentation Format, encryption, compression Data TLS, JPEG, ASCII 5 Session Opens, manages and closes sessions Data NetBIOS, RPC 4 Transport End-to-end delivery, ports, reliability Segment (TCP) / Datagram (UDP) TCP, UDP 3 Network Logical addressing, routing Packet IP, ICMP, routers 2 Data Link Framing, MAC addressing, error detection Frame Ethernet, switches 1 Physical Bits on the medium Bits Cables, hubs, radio Memory aid (top to bottom): A ll P eople S eem T o N eed D ata P rocessing. Data moves down the seven OSI layers at the sender, across the medium as bits, and back up at the receiver Each layer in plain words 7 Application: the network services that programs use: HTTP for the web, SMTP for email, DNS for names. It is not the program itself; the browser uses HTTP. 6 Presentation: makes sure both sides understand the data format. It deals with character sets, compression and encryption (TLS is often placed here). 5 Session: starts, manages and ends conversations between applications, for example keeping a login session open. 4 Transport: delivers data from one application to another. It adds port numbers , and for TCP it adds reliability: acknowledgements, retransmission and ordering (see 3.3.1 ). 3 Network: delivers packets from one host to another, across many networks, using IP addresses . Routers work here. 2 Data link: delivers frames from one device to the next device on the same link , using MAC addresses , and detects errors with a CRC. Switches work here. 1 Physical: sends the raw bits as electrical signals, light pulses or radio waves. Cables, connectors, hubs and repeaters work here. Which device works at which layer Layer Devices Addresses used 1 Physical Cables, hubs, repeaters, media converters None 2 Data link Switches, bridges, network cards, access points MAC addresses 3 Network Routers, Layer 3 switches IP addresses 4 to 7 Firewalls, load balancers, proxies Ports and application data A different memory aid The aid on the page reads from layer 7 down. To remember from layer 1 up , use: Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away (Physical, Data link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application). Using the layers to troubleshoot Symptom Probably layer No link light on the port 1 Link light on, but the PC is in the wrong VLAN 2 Wrong IP address, mask or gateway 3 The web service is not listening, or a firewall blocks port 443 4 The website shows an error, or its certificate has expired 7 Starting at the bottom and working up is the bottom-up approach described in 9.1 . Remember Seven layers: Physical, Data link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application. Switches use MAC addresses at layer 2; routers use IP addresses at layer 3; ports are at layer 4. Each layer serves the one above it and uses the one below it."},{"t":"3.4.2 The TCP/IP model and OSI comparison","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-4-2.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.4.2 The TCP/IP model and OSI comparison The TCP/IP model (4 layers) TCP/IP layer Corresponding OSI layers Application 7, 6, 5 Transport 4 Internet 3 Network Access (Link) 2, 1 Each TCP/IP layer Application: protocols that applications use directly: HTTP, DNS, SMTP, SSH, DHCP. It covers OSI layers 5, 6 and 7. Transport: TCP and UDP, with port numbers. The same as OSI layer 4. Internet: IP addressing and routing, plus ICMP for errors and testing. The same as OSI layer 3. Network access (link): whatever carries the packet on the local link (Ethernet, Wi-Fi, fibre), including MAC addresses, frames and the physical signals. It covers OSI layers 1 and 2. Some books show a five-layer version, which keeps Physical and Data link separate. It is the same model, drawn with one extra line. OSI compared with TCP/IP OSI is a theoretical teaching and troubleshooting model; TCP/IP is the practical model on which the Internet actually runs. OSI has 7 layers and separates presentation and session; TCP/IP has 4 layers and merges them into Application. The four TCP/IP layers mapped onto the seven OSI layers, with example protocols Similarities and differences Similarities Differences Both use layers, with each layer serving the one above OSI has 7 layers; TCP/IP has 4 Both have transport and network layers with the same jobs OSI separates session and presentation; TCP/IP puts them in Application Both describe how data is encapsulated OSI was designed as a general model before its protocols; TCP/IP was built from protocols that already worked TCP/IP is what the Internet runs; OSI is mostly used for teaching and troubleshooting Why both are still taught The OSI model's separate layers make it easier to describe exactly where a problem or a device sits, so engineers use OSI layer numbers in everyday conversation. The TCP/IP model describes how real protocols are actually grouped. You need both: TCP/IP to understand how the Internet works, and OSI to talk about it precisely. Remember TCP/IP has four layers: Application, Transport, Internet and Network access. Application covers OSI 5 to 7; Network access covers OSI 1 and 2. The Internet runs on TCP/IP; OSI is the common language for describing layers."},{"t":"3.4.3 Encapsulation","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-4-3.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.4.3 Encapsulation When sending, each layer adds its own header: data becomes a segment (transport header with ports), then a packet (IP header with IP addresses), then a frame (MAC header and CRC trailer), then bits. The receiver reverses the process ( de-encapsulation ). Encapsulation: each layer adds its own header (and Ethernet adds a trailer) as data moves down the stack Following one web request You type www.example.com and press Enter. On your PC: Application: the browser creates an HTTP request: \"GET / please\". Transport: TCP adds a header with the source port (a random high number, such as 51000) and the destination port (443 for HTTPS), plus sequence numbers. The data is now a segment . Internet: IP adds a header with your PC's IP address and the server's IP address. It is now a packet . Network access: Ethernet adds a header with your PC's MAC address and the MAC address of the default gateway (not the server!), plus a CRC trailer. It is now a frame , sent as bits on the cable. At every router on the way, the frame header is removed and a new frame header is added for the next link. The IP addresses stay the same from end to end, but the MAC addresses change on every hop. At the server, each layer removes its own header and passes the rest up ( de-encapsulation ) until the web server software receives the original HTTP request. Addresses at each layer Layer Address Identifies Changes on the way? Transport Port number The application on the host No Network IP address The host, anywhere in the world No (except when NAT rewrites it, see 4.3.2 ) Data link MAC address The interface on the local link Yes, at every router Overhead Every header adds bytes that are not your data. A full-size Ethernet frame carries 1,500 bytes of payload; the TCP and IP headers use at least 40 of those, and Ethernet adds its own header, trailer and framing on the wire. That is one reason why throughput is always a little lower than bandwidth (see 1.7 ). Analogy: a letter (data) goes into an envelope with the department name (port), which goes into a bigger envelope with the company's address (IP), which a courier puts in a bag labelled for the next depot (frame). At each depot the bag is changed, but the address on the envelope never changes. Remember Data, then segment, then packet, then frame, then bits. IP addresses stay the same end to end; MAC addresses change at every router. The receiver removes the headers in reverse order (de-encapsulation). Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 3.5 hands-on lab and the 3.6 review questions ."},{"t":"3.5 Hands-on lab: Devices, VLANs and packet capture","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-lab.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.5 Hands-on lab: Devices, VLANs and packet capture Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. In Packet Tracer build: two PCs on a switch (192.168.1.0/24) and a server in a second network behind a router. Configure the router interfaces and test with ping . Create two VLANs and show that hosts in different VLANs cannot ping each other without a router. Run Wireshark, open a web page, and identify an Ethernet frame, IP packet, TCP segment and HTTP request. Write which OSI layer each belongs to. Capture a DHCP exchange ( ipconfig /release then ipconfig /renew ) and identify the four DORA messages. Useful Wireshark display filters: dhcp (shows the DORA messages), http , dns , tcp.port == 80 and arp . Click a packet to see each layer's header in the middle pane."},{"t":"3.6 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-review.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.6 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. Switch router MAC table routing table default gateway VLAN trunk collision domain broadcast domain protocol suite TCP UDP handshake DHCP PDU encapsulation Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. How does a switch differ from a hub? At which OSI layer does a router operate and what address does it use? List the DORA steps of DHCP. Name the PDU at layers 4, 3 and 2. Give two applications that prefer UDP and explain why. Which TCP/IP layer corresponds to OSI layers 5 to 7? Why is Telnet considered insecure, and what replaces it? Show answers A hub repeats frames to all ports (one collision domain, half-duplex); a switch learns MAC addresses and forwards only to the destination port (separate collision domains, full-duplex). Layer 3 (Network); IP address. Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge. Layer 4: segment (TCP) or datagram (UDP); layer 3: packet; layer 2: frame. VoIP and video streaming (also DNS queries, online games): they need low delay and tolerate small loss, so retransmission would hurt. The Application layer. Telnet sends everything, including passwords, in clear text; SSH replaces it with encryption. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 3 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 3: Network Devices and Protocols","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03.html","x":"Unit 3 Network Devices and Protocols The devices that build networks and how to configure them, the protocols they use, and the OSI and TCP/IP reference models. What you will learn After this unit you can: Identify common network devices and state the function of each. Install and configure a switch, router, access point and basic firewall. Explain what a network protocol is and describe the most common protocols. Describe the OSI and TCP/IP reference models and map devices and protocols to layers. Topics in this unit 3.1 Introduction to network devices 2 diagrams ✓ 3.2 Install and configure network devices 1 diagram ✓ 3.2.1 Planning checklist 1 diagram ✓ 3.2.2 Basic switch configuration 1 command example ✓ 3.2.3 Basic router configuration 1 command example ✓ 3.2.4 VLANs and trunk ports 1 command example · 1 diagram ✓ 3.2.5 Wireless access point and home router ✓ 3.2.6 Verification commands ✓ 3.3 Network protocols ✓ 3.3.1 TCP and UDP 2 diagrams ✓ 3.3.2 DHCP and the DORA process 1 diagram ✓ 3.4 Reference models ✓ 3.4.1 The OSI model 1 diagram ✓ 3.4.2 The TCP/IP model and OSI comparison 1 diagram ✓ 3.4.3 Encapsulation 1 diagram ✓ 3.5 Hands-on lab: Devices, VLANs and packet capture ✓ 3.6 Review: key terms and self-check 7 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"4.1 MAC address","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-1.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.1 MAC address A MAC (Media Access Control) address is a 48-bit hardware address burned into a network interface and used for delivery on the local network (Layer 2). Written as 12 hexadecimal digits: 00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E or 00-1A-2B-3C-4D-5E . The first 24 bits are the OUI (Organisationally Unique Identifier) assigned to the manufacturer; the last 24 bits identify the individual card. FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF is the broadcast MAC address. ARP (Address Resolution Protocol) discovers the MAC address that belongs to a known IP address on the same LAN. A MAC address is 48 bits: the first half identifies the manufacturer (OUI), the second half the individual interface Find your MAC address: Windows ipconfig /all (Physical Address); Linux ip link . ARP: the request is broadcast to every host on the LAN; only the owner of the IP address replies with its MAC address Why MAC addresses exist An IP address can change (DHCP can give a laptop a new address tomorrow). A MAC address is tied to the network interface itself, so on a local network switches can deliver frames to the correct cable port even when IP addresses change. Layer 2 (switches, Wi-Fi access points) forwards using MACs; Layer 3 (routers) forwards using IP addresses. Both are needed, and ARP is the bridge between them. OUI and uniqueness The first three bytes (24 bits) are the OUI , assigned by the IEEE to a manufacturer (Cisco, Intel, Realtek and so on). The last three bytes are chosen by the manufacturer so that no two of their cards share the same full address. In theory every MAC in the world is unique. In practice, virtual machines and some tools can set a locally administered address, so uniqueness is not a security guarantee. Broadcast and special addresses Address Meaning FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF Broadcast: every NIC on the LAN accepts the frame 01:00:5E:... (typical) Multicast Ethernet addresses (used with IP multicast) Your own MAC Unicast: only your NIC accepts the frame How ARP works, step by step Suppose PC-A (192.168.1.10) wants to send an IP packet to PC-B (192.168.1.20) on the same subnet, but does not yet know B's MAC: A looks in its ARP cache . If B's MAC is already there and not expired, A uses it. If not, A broadcasts an ARP request : \"Who has 192.168.1.20? Tell 192.168.1.10.\" Every host on the LAN receives the request. Only B recognises its own IP and replies with an ARP reply (unicast) containing B's MAC. A stores the mapping in its ARP cache and builds the Ethernet frame with B's MAC as the destination. If B is on another subnet, A does not ARP for B. It ARPs for its default gateway and sends the frame to the router. The router then forwards the packet toward B. Getting this wrong (trying to ARP for a remote host) is a common misconception. Everyday example: a switch is like a post office that sorts by street number (MAC). A router is like a post office that sorts by city (IP network). ARP is asking your neighbours \"what is the street number for flat 20?\" Common mistake: treating a MAC as a password or as proof of identity. MACs can be spoofed. Switches may use MAC filtering as a weak extra control, but it is not real authentication. Tip: on Windows, arp -a shows the cache; on Linux, ip neigh . Clearing a stale entry ( arp -d or ip neigh flush ) can fix \"cannot reach host\" after a device's IP or NIC has changed. Remember A MAC address is a 48-bit Layer-2 hardware address; IP is Layer 3. ARP maps an IP address to a MAC address on the same LAN. To reach another subnet, hosts send frames to the gateway's MAC, not the remote host's MAC."},{"t":"4.2 Number systems (needed for IP addressing)","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-2.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.2 Number systems (needed for IP addressing) Binary uses digits 0 and 1. Each position in an octet (8 bits) has the value: 128 64 32 16 8 4 2 1 Decimal to binary: 192 = 128 + 64 = 11000000 . 172 = 128 + 32 + 8 + 4 = 10101100 . Each bit in an octet has a place value; add the values of the 1 bits to get the decimal number Binary to decimal: 00001010 = 8 + 2 = 10. Hexadecimal (base 16) uses 0-9 and A-F; each hex digit is 4 bits (A = 1010, F = 1111). Why binary matters for IP addressing Every IPv4 address and every subnet mask is really a pattern of 32 bits. Decimal (192.168.1.10) is only a human-friendly view. Subnetting, VLSM and CIDR all mean \"count bits and draw a line between the network part and the host part\". If you can convert one octet between decimal and binary, you can check any mask by hand. Place values in one octet Bit position 7 6 5 4 3 2 1 0 Place value 128 64 32 16 8 4 2 1 To convert decimal to binary , subtract the largest place value that still fits, write 1, and continue; write 0 where it does not fit. 192 = 128 + 64 → 11000000 168 = 128 + 32 + 8 → 10101000 10 = 8 + 2 → 00001010 To convert binary to decimal , add the place values of every 1 bit: 10101100 = 128+32+8+4 = 172 . Hexadecimal shortcuts Each hex digit is exactly 4 bits, so two hex digits make one byte. That is why MAC addresses are written in hex ( 00:1A:2B ) and why IPv6 uses hex groups. Useful nibble values: Hex Binary Decimal 0 0000 0 9 1001 9 A 1010 10 C 1100 12 F 1111 15 Try it: convert 255 and 128 to binary, then convert 11000000 and 00001111 to decimal. You should get 11111111 , 10000000 , 192 and 15 . Remember One octet = 8 bits; place values are 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1. Subnetting is bit arithmetic dressed up as decimal numbers. Hex is a short way to write binary; one hex digit = 4 bits."},{"t":"4.3 IPv4 addressing","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-3.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.3 IPv4 addressing An IPv4 address is 32 bits, written as four decimal octets (dotted decimal): 192.168.10.25 = 11000000.10101000.00001010.00011001 . It has a network portion and a host portion , separated by the subnet mask . An IPv4 address is 32 bits in four octets; the subnet mask marks which bits are the network part and which are the host part Key words in this topic IPv4 address : 32-bit identifier written as four decimal octets. Network portion / host portion : which bits identify the network, and which identify the device on that network. Subnet mask / prefix length : the line between those two portions ( 255.255.255.0 or /24 ). Private address : not routed on the public Internet; used inside organisations with NAT. Unicast / broadcast / multicast : one, all-on-subnet, or a group. In this section 4.3.1 Classful addressing 4.3.2 Private addresses and NAT 4.3.3 Assignment and delivery types Skills you should leave with Given an address and mask, you should be able to say whether it is public or private, estimate its classful range for recognition, and decide whether a host should get it statically or via DHCP. The next pages ( 4.4 – 4.5 ) turn the mask into exact network, broadcast and usable ranges."},{"t":"4.3.1 Classful addressing","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-3-1.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.3.1 Classful addressing Class First octet Default mask Hosts per network Use A 1-126 255.0.0.0 (/8) about 16.7 million Very large networks B 128-191 255.255.0.0 (/16) 65,534 Medium networks C 192-223 255.255.255.0 (/24) 254 Small networks D 224-239 - - Multicast E 240-255 - - Experimental 127.0.0.0/8 is loopback ( 127.0.0.1 refers to the local machine). Historic IPv4 classes by first octet. Classful addressing is no longer used for routing, but the ranges are still useful to recognise Classful addressing was replaced by CIDR in 1993. Today the prefix length (for example /24 ), not the class, decides the size of a network. What \"classful\" meant In the early Internet, the first few bits of an address fixed the mask. If the first octet was 1–126 the mask was always /8 ; if 128–191 it was /16 ; if 192–223 it was /24 . That wasted huge numbers of addresses: a company that needed 300 hosts was often given a whole Class B (65,534 usable hosts). Why you still learn the classes Modern routing uses CIDR (see 4.4 ): the prefix length can be any value, not only /8, /16 or /24. But exams and day-to-day work still use class ranges to recognise address types at a glance: 10.x.x.x looks like a Class A private range. 172.16 – 172.31 looks like Class B private. 192.168.x.x looks like Class C private. 224.x.x.x is multicast (Class D). 127.x.x.x is loopback: packets to 127.0.0.1 never leave the local machine (useful to test that the IP stack is alive). Common mistake: assuming that 192.168.1.50 must use mask 255.255.255.0 . Today the mask is whatever the network designer chose ( /25 , /26 and so on). Always look at the mask or prefix, not only the first octet. Remember Classful addressing is historic; CIDR decides size today. Class ranges are still useful for recognising private, loopback and multicast addresses. 127.0.0.1 always means \"this computer\"."},{"t":"4.3.2 Private addresses and NAT","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-3-2.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.3.2 Private addresses and NAT Range CIDR 10.0.0.0 - 10.255.255.255 10.0.0.0/8 172.16.0.0 - 172.31.255.255 172.16.0.0/12 192.168.0.0 - 192.168.255.255 192.168.0.0/16 Private addresses are not routed on the Internet; NAT (Network Address Translation) on the router translates them to a public address. APIPA addresses 169.254.x.x appear when a client fails to obtain a DHCP address. NAT: hosts with private addresses share the router's single public address on the Internet Why private addresses exist There are only about 4.3 billion IPv4 addresses. Private ranges (RFC 1918) let every home and office reuse the same addresses ( 192.168.1.1 appears in millions of networks) without clashing on the Internet, because those packets are not forwarded by Internet routers. What NAT does Network Address Translation on the edge router rewrites private source addresses to the router's public address when traffic goes out, and rewrites replies back again. From the Internet's point of view, many internal PCs share one (or a few) public IP(s). From the LAN's point of view, each PC still has its own private address. Direction What happens Outbound from PC 192.168.1.50 Router replaces source with public IP (and tracks the port mapping) Reply from a web server Router looks up the mapping and sends the packet to 192.168.1.50 Unsolicited inbound to a private IP Blocked unless you create a port-forward / firewall rule APIPA (169.254.x.x) If a client is set to use DHCP but cannot reach a DHCP server, Windows and some other systems assign themselves an address in 169.254.0.0/16 . That lets devices on the same cable talk to each other locally, but they usually cannot reach the Internet or other subnets. Seeing 169.254... in ipconfig is a strong hint that DHCP failed (cable unplugged, wrong VLAN, or DHCP server down). Everyday example: your home Wi-Fi uses 192.168.x.x . The ISP gives your router one public address. Every phone and laptop shares that public address through NAT when browsing. Remember Private addresses stay inside the organisation; NAT translates them for the Internet. 10/8 , 172.16/12 and 192.168/16 are the three private ranges. 169.254.x.x usually means \"DHCP failed\"."},{"t":"4.3.3 Assignment and delivery types","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-3-3.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.3.3 Assignment and delivery types Static and dynamic assignment Static: set manually; use for servers, routers, printers. Dynamic: leased by a DHCP server; use for clients. Choosing static or DHCP Device Usual choice Why Servers, routers, printers, switches' management IPs Static Other devices and DNS records must find them at a fixed address Laptops, phones, lab PCs DHCP Easy to manage; addresses are leased and returned automatically Static addresses must still be outside the DHCP pool (or reserved in DHCP) so two devices never get the same IP. Duplicate IPs cause intermittent \"destination unreachable\" faults that are hard to spot. Unicast, broadcast, multicast Unicast is one-to-one; broadcast is one-to-all on the subnet (sent to the subnet's broadcast address, which is the last address in the subnet); multicast is one-to-many group. Unicast goes to one host, broadcast to every host on the subnet, multicast to the members of a group Delivery types in more detail Unicast: one sender, one receiver. Normal web browsing, SSH and file transfers are unicast. Broadcast: one sender, every host on the local subnet . DHCP discover and ARP requests use broadcast. Routers do not forward broadcasts to other networks (that is one reason we subnet: to limit broadcast traffic). Multicast: one sender, only hosts that joined a group (for example IPTV or some routing protocols). Multicast addresses are in 224.0.0.0 – 239.255.255.255 . The subnet broadcast address is the last address in the subnet (all host bits set to 1). For 192.168.10.0/24 it is 192.168.10.255 . Hosts must not use the network address (all host bits 0) or the broadcast address as their own IP. Remember Static for infrastructure; DHCP for clients; never overlap pools. Broadcast stays on one subnet; that is why subnet size affects noise and performance. Unicast = one, broadcast = all on subnet, multicast = a group."},{"t":"4.4 Subnet masks and CIDR","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-4.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.4 Subnet masks and CIDR The subnet mask has 1s for the network part and 0s for the host part. CIDR notation writes the count of 1s: 255.255.255.0 = /24 . A /26 mask: 26 network bits (1s) followed by 6 host bits (0s), which is 255.255.255.192 CIDR Mask Host bits Usable hosts /24 255.255.255.0 8 254 /25 255.255.255.128 7 126 /26 255.255.255.192 6 62 /27 255.255.255.224 5 30 /28 255.255.255.240 4 14 /29 255.255.255.248 3 6 /30 255.255.255.252 2 2 Formulas: Usable hosts per subnet = 2^h - 2 (h = host bits; subtract the network address and broadcast address). Number of subnets = 2^s (s = bits borrowed from the host part). Block size = 256 - the interesting octet of the mask. Reading a mask A mask of /24 means the first 24 bits are the network identity and the remaining 8 bits identify hosts. Written in decimal that mask is 255.255.255.0 (twenty-four 1s, then eight 0s). Worked check: /26 /26 → 26 ones → binary mask ends with 11000000 in the last octet → decimal 192 → 255.255.255.192 . Host bits h = 32 − 26 = 6 Addresses in each subnet = 2⁶ = 64 Usable hosts = 64 − 2 = 62 (subtract network and broadcast) Block size shortcut In the \"interesting\" octet (the first octet that is not 255 or 0 in the mask), block size = 256 − that octet value . For 255.255.255.192 , interesting octet = 192, block size = 64. Subnets therefore start at 0, 64, 128, 192 in that octet. Borrowing bits for subnets If you start from /24 and need 4 equal subnets, you borrow 2 host bits because 2² = 4. The new prefix is /26 . If you need 8 subnets you borrow 3 bits → /27 . Always check that the remaining host bits still give enough usable hosts for the largest LAN. Common mistake: forgetting to subtract 2 for network and broadcast when counting usable hosts. A /30 has 4 addresses but only 2 usable hosts — perfect for a point-to-point router link, useless for a room of PCs. Tip: memorise the row for /24 to /30 in the table on this page. Most exam and lab designs use those sizes. Remember Prefix length = number of network 1-bits in the mask. Usable hosts = 2^h − 2; block size = 256 − interesting octet. Borrow host bits to create more subnets; check hosts still fit."},{"t":"4.5 Subnetting step by step","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-5.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.5 Subnetting step by step Subnetting is the skill of splitting one large address block into smaller networks that match real departments, VLANs or links. Work every example with paper first; then check with a calculator. Key words in this topic Network address : first address of the subnet (host bits all 0). Broadcast address : last address (host bits all 1). Usable range : everything strictly between those two. VLSM : different subnet sizes inside one parent block, largest first. Default gateway : the router's address on this subnet; hosts send remote traffic there. Worked example 1. Find the network, broadcast and host range for 192.168.10.77/26 . /26 mask = 255.255.255.192. Block size = 256 - 192 = 64 . Subnets in the last octet start at 0, 64, 128, 192. 77 lies between 64 and 128, so the network address = 192.168.10.64 . Broadcast = next network minus 1 = 192.168.10.127. Usable range = 192.168.10.65 to 192.168.10.126 (62 hosts). With a /26 mask the last octet is divided into blocks of 64; 192.168.10.77 falls in the block that starts at 64 Worked example 2 (design). Divide 192.168.1.0/24 into 4 equal subnets. 4 subnets needs 2 bits (2^2 = 4), so the new prefix is /26 (mask 255.255.255.192), 62 hosts each. Subnet Network Usable range Broadcast 1 192.168.1.0/26 .1 - .62 .63 2 192.168.1.64/26 .65 - .126 .127 3 192.168.1.128/26 .129 - .190 .191 4 192.168.1.192/26 .193 - .254 .255 Worked example 3 (requirement-driven). A department needs 50 hosts. Smallest h with 2^h - 2 >= 50 is h = 6 (62), so use /26 . Practise: make up your own addresses, work them out by hand, then check with the subnet calculator in 4.4 . In this section 4.5.1 VLSM and the default gateway"},{"t":"4.5.1 VLSM and the default gateway","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-5-1.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.5.1 VLSM and the default gateway VLSM (Variable Length Subnet Masking) VLSM lets you use different prefix lengths so that no addresses are wasted. Procedure: sort requirements from largest to smallest and allocate in that order. Example: from 192.168.5.0/24 provide LAN A (100 hosts), LAN B (50 hosts), LAN C (20 hosts), and a router link (2 hosts). LAN A: 100 hosts needs /25 → 192.168.5.0/25 (0-127) LAN B: 50 hosts needs /26 → 192.168.5.128/26 (128-191) LAN C: 20 hosts needs /27 → 192.168.5.192/27 (192-223) Link: 2 hosts needs /30 → 192.168.5.224/30 (224-227) VLSM plan for 192.168.5.0/24: each subnet is just big enough, largest first, leaving space for future growth Why \"largest first\"? If you gave the 20-host LAN a /25 first, you would waste half the parent /24 , and the 100-host LAN would no longer fit. Sorting by size packs the block tightly and leaves one contiguous free area at the end for growth. Prefix needed for N hosts Find the smallest h such that 2^h − 2 ≥ N, then prefix = 32 − h: Hosts needed Host bits Prefix 2 /30 6 /29 14 /28 30 /27 62 /26 126 /25 254 /24 Default gateway in practice Every host needs: Its own IP address inside the usable range. The correct subnet mask (same as the rest of the subnet). The default gateway = the router's interface address on this subnet (also inside the usable range, often .1 by convention). At least one DNS server address (may be the router or a public resolver). If the mask is wrong, two PCs on the \"same\" switch may think they are on different networks and send traffic to the gateway unnecessarily — or fail to reach each other. If the gateway is wrong or down, local traffic may still work ( ping to a neighbour) while Internet access fails. That pattern is a classic troubleshooting clue (see Unit 9 ). Remember VLSM: sort requirements largest → smallest; pick the smallest prefix that fits each. Gateway must be on the same subnet as the host. Wrong mask or wrong gateway are among the most common IP faults. Default gateway To reach any address outside its own subnet, a host sends traffic to its default gateway (the router interface on its subnet). A wrong gateway or mask is among the most common causes of connectivity faults."},{"t":"4.6 IPv6 addressing","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-6.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.6 IPv6 addressing IPv4 has about 4.3 billion addresses, which are exhausted. IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses written as eight groups of four hex digits: 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:ff00:0042:8329 . Shortening rules: drop leading zeros in a group; replace one run of all-zero groups with :: (only once). The address above becomes 2001:db8::ff00:42:8329 . Shortening an IPv6 address: drop leading zeros in each group, then replace one run of all-zero groups with :: Differences that matter for beginners Every IPv6 interface gets a link-local address ( fe80::/10 ) automatically. It works only on that link and is used for Neighbour Discovery and router messages even before a global address exists. There is no broadcast . One-to-many uses multicast ; the jobs ARP did in IPv4 are handled by Neighbour Discovery (ND) with ICMPv6. A LAN is usually numbered with a /64 prefix. SLAAC lets hosts build their own address from the router advertisement; DHCPv6 can still be used when administrators want tighter control. During the long migration, many networks run dual-stack (IPv4 and IPv6 together). Tunnelling and translation are fallback transition tools when one side is IPv6-only. Tip: recognise fe80:: as link-local and ::1 as loopback at a glance. If a host only has link-local addresses, it cannot reach the Internet yet — it still needs a global or unique-local address and a route. Common mistake: using :: more than once in one address. Only a single run of zero groups may be compressed, or the address is ambiguous. Remember IPv6 = 128 bits; shorten with dropped leading zeros and one :: . Link-local always; no broadcast; ND replaces ARP. /64 is the normal LAN size; dual-stack is the usual transition path. Type Prefix Note Global unicast 2000::/3 Public, routable Link-local fe80::/10 Automatically on every interface, local link only Unique local fc00::/7 Private-style addressing Multicast ff00::/8 Replaces broadcast Loopback ::1 Local host IPv6 has no broadcast and usually uses a /64 prefix for a LAN. Addresses can be assigned by SLAAC (auto-configuration) or DHCPv6. Transition methods: dual-stack, tunnelling, translation. Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 4.7 hands-on lab and the 4.8 review questions ."},{"t":"4.7 Hands-on lab: Addressing and subnetting","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-lab.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.7 Hands-on lab: Addressing and subnetting Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. Record the MAC address, IPv4 address, mask, gateway and DNS of your computer ( ipconfig /all or ip a ). Run arp -a and match entries to devices on your LAN. Subnet 172.16.0.0/16 into 8 equal subnets; list network, range and broadcast of each. In Packet Tracer, configure two subnets joined by a router and prove connectivity with ping and tracert . Convert five IPv4 addresses to binary by hand and check with a calculator. Check your work with the subnet calculator , the VLSM planner and the binary converter , but only after you have worked each answer out by hand."},{"t":"4.8 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-review.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.8 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. MAC OUI ARP octet subnet mask CIDR VLSM private address NAT APIPA default gateway IPv6 link-local SLAAC Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. How many bits are in a MAC address and an IPv4 address? Convert 203 to binary and 10010110 to decimal. Which of these are private: 172.20.5.1, 172.32.0.1, 192.168.99.5, 11.0.0.1? For 10.1.1.200/27 find network, broadcast and usable range. How many usable hosts are in a /28? What mask do you need for at least 500 hosts? Shorten fe80:0000:0000:0000:02aa:00ff:fe28:9c5a . What does ARP do? Show answers MAC 48 bits; IPv4 32 bits. 203 = 128+64+8+2+1 = 11001011 ; 10010110 = 128+16+4+2 = 150 . 172.20.5.1 and 192.168.99.5 are private. 172.32.0.1 is outside 172.16-172.31 and 11.0.0.1 is public. /27 block size 32; 200 lies in 192-223: network 10.1.1.192 , broadcast 10.1.1.223 , usable 10.1.1.193 - 10.1.1.222 . 2^4 - 2 = 14 . 2^9 - 2 = 510 >= 500, so 9 host bits, mask /23 (255.255.254.0). fe80::2aa:ff:fe28:9c5a . ARP finds the MAC address that corresponds to a known IP address on the local network. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 4 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 4: Addressing Network Devices","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04.html","x":"Unit 4 Addressing Network Devices How devices are addressed: MAC addresses, binary, IPv4 classes and private ranges, subnet masks, subnetting, VLSM and IPv6. What you will learn After this unit you can: Describe the structure and purpose of a MAC address. Explain IPv4 and IPv6 addressing, classes, public and private ranges. Convert between binary and decimal. Design and calculate subnets using subnet masks, CIDR and VLSM. Topics in this unit 4.1 MAC address 2 diagrams ✓ 4.2 Number systems (needed for IP addressing) 1 diagram · interactive lab ✓ 4.3 IPv4 addressing 1 diagram ✓ 4.3.1 Classful addressing 1 diagram ✓ 4.3.2 Private addresses and NAT 1 diagram ✓ 4.3.3 Assignment and delivery types 1 diagram ✓ 4.4 Subnet masks and CIDR 1 diagram · interactive lab ✓ 4.5 Subnetting step by step 1 diagram ✓ 4.5.1 VLSM and the default gateway 1 diagram · interactive lab ✓ 4.6 IPv6 addressing 1 diagram · interactive lab ✓ 4.7 Hands-on lab: Addressing and subnetting ✓ 4.8 Review: key terms and self-check 8 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"5.1 What is a server and why is a server OS required?","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-1.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.1 What is a server and why is a server OS required? A server is a computer (or software) that provides services to other computers (clients) over a network. A server must run continuously , serve many users at once , be secure and be manageable remotely . The server OS manages the hardware; server applications on top of it provide the services clients use Server OS compared with a desktop OS Feature Desktop OS Server OS Purpose One interactive user Many simultaneous remote users and services Hardware support Consumer hardware Multi-CPU, large RAM, RAID, hot-swap, ECC memory Roles / services Few DNS, DHCP, web, file, directory, database, mail and more Uptime and stability Moderate Designed for months or years without reboot Management Mostly local GUI Remote tools, command line, scripting, centralised policy Security Basic Fine-grained permissions, auditing, role-based access Licensing Per device Server licence plus client access licences (CALs) for Windows; free/open source or subscription for Linux Why application software is needed The operating system manages hardware; server application software delivers the actual service: a web server (Apache, Nginx, IIS), database (MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server), mail server (Postfix, Exchange), file server (Samba, SMB), DNS (BIND, Windows DNS). A server's purpose is defined by the applications installed, so the operating system and application stack must be chosen together to match the workload. What \"always on\" really means A desktop can reboot after updates whenever the user likes. A server often hosts DNS, files or a website that many people need at once. Unplanned downtime means lost lessons, lost sales or locked accounts. That is why server hardware and server operating systems favour: ECC memory and RAID (fewer silent failures). Remote management so you can fix problems without walking to the room. Role-based services that start automatically at boot and restart if they crash. Auditing and permissions fine enough for many users and shared data. Software defines the role The operating system alone is not \"a web server\". Apache , IIS or Nginx make it a web server; BIND or Windows DNS make it a name server. Choose the OS and the application stack together for the workload, the skills of your team and the budget (Windows licences and CALs versus free Linux distributions with optional support subscriptions). Everyday example: a school file server must stay available during exam week, support dozens of simultaneous logins, and let technicians patch it from another building over SSH or RDP. A normal classroom PC OS is not built for that pattern of use. Remember A server provides services to clients, continuously and for many users. The OS manages hardware; applications deliver the actual service. Uptime, remote management and strong permissions separate server OS from desktop OS."},{"t":"5.2 Types of servers (by role)","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-2.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.2 Types of servers (by role) File server, print server, web server, application server, database server, mail server, DNS server, DHCP server, proxy server, authentication/directory server (Active Directory, LDAP), backup server, and virtualization host. One computer can play many server roles; the role is defined by the server application installed Each role in plain words Role What it does Typical software File server Stores shared folders and enforces permissions Samba, Windows File Server Print server Queues and shares printers CUPS, Windows Print Server Web server Answers HTTP/HTTPS requests Apache, Nginx, IIS Database server Stores structured data for applications MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server Mail server Sends and receives email Postfix, Exchange DNS / DHCP server Names and automatic addresses BIND, Windows DNS/DHCP Directory / auth server Central usernames and policies Active Directory, LDAP Proxy server Forwards and can filter web traffic Squid, corporate proxies Backup server Stores copies of other systems Backup software + storage Virtualization host Runs many virtual machines ESXi, Hyper-V, KVM, Proxmox One physical (or virtual) machine can run several roles if the load is light — for example DNS + DHCP + file shares on a small office server. Separate busy or sensitive roles (public web versus internal AD) onto different servers or VMs for security and performance. Common mistake: putting a public website on the same server that holds all student or staff files without isolation. A web breach then reaches the files too. Prefer separate VMs and a DMZ (see Unit 8 ). Remember The role is defined by the service software, not by the box colour. Small networks combine roles; larger ones separate them."},{"t":"5.3 Server hosting and deployment options","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-3.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.3 Server hosting and deployment options Hosting options from cheapest and least work to most control and responsibility Option Description Advantages Disadvantages Typical use Dedicated server A whole physical machine for one customer Full performance, control and isolation Expensive, you manage hardware or pay for it Large databases, high-traffic sites Shared hosting Many customers share one server and its resources Very cheap, no server management Limited control, neighbours can slow you, limited security Small websites, blogs Virtual Private Server (VPS) One physical machine divided into isolated virtual machines, each with its own OS and root access Good balance of cost and control, scalable Shares hardware, performance can vary Small to medium business apps Cloud server Virtual servers in a provider data centre, billed by use (AWS, Azure, Google Cloud) Elastic scaling, high availability, pay as you go Costs can grow, depends on provider Variable or global workloads On-premises server Owned and run in your own server room Full control of data Capital cost, power, cooling, staff Sensitive data, local services Colocation Your own server hosted in a provider's data centre Provider power, cooling and bandwidth Hardware cost remains with you Companies needing own hardware Key words in this topic On-premises : servers in your own building. Colocation : your hardware in a provider's data centre. Shared hosting / VPS / cloud / dedicated : increasing control and (usually) cost. RAID : disk redundancy or speed; not a substitute for backups. In this section 5.3.1 Selecting a server"},{"t":"5.3.1 Selecting a server","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-3-1.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.3.1 Selecting a server Consider: workload (users, transactions, data volume), CPU cores, RAM, storage (SSD vs HDD, RAID level), network bandwidth , availability (redundant power supplies, clustering, backups), security and compliance , scalability , budget (purchase plus running cost), support and warranty , and skills available . Basic RAID levels: RAID 0 (striping, speed, no protection), RAID 1 (mirroring), RAID 5 (striping with parity, survives one disk failure), RAID 6 (two disk failures), RAID 10 (mirror plus stripe). RAID 0 stripes for speed only, RAID 1 mirrors, RAID 5 stripes with parity so any one disk can fail RAID is not a backup. It keeps a server running when a disk fails, but deleted files, ransomware and fire affect every disk at once. Keep separate backups. Worked example: a school needs a web server for 500 students and a file server for 50 staff. A virtual machine on one on-premises host or a small cloud VPS (2 vCPU, 4 GB RAM) is enough for the web server; the file server needs mirrored disks and daily backup. A dedicated server would be wasteful. Choosing RAID for the right reason Level Idea Survives Good for RAID 0 Stripe across disks Nothing (one disk dies → data gone) Temporary speed only; avoid for important data RAID 1 Mirror One disk in the pair OS disks, small servers RAID 5 Stripe + one parity One disk General file data (needs ≥3 disks) RAID 6 Stripe + two parity Two disks Larger arrays RAID 10 Mirror + stripe One disk per mirrored pair Databases needing speed and protection Capacity planning questions How many simultaneous users and what do they do (browse, edit big files, run a shop)? How much storage now, and in two years? What happens if this server is down for an hour? a day? Who will patch and monitor it, and with which skills? RAID is not backup. Ransomware, accidental deletion and site disasters affect every disk in the array. Keep separate, tested backups (ideally off-site). Remember Match CPU, RAM, disk and network to the workload; plan for growth. RAID keeps the server running after a disk fault; backups recover from data loss."},{"t":"5.4 Server operating systems","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-4.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.4 Server operating systems OS Key features Typical roles Windows Server (2019, 2022, 2025) GUI, Active Directory, Group Policy, IIS, PowerShell; licensed Domain services, file/print, Exchange Linux (Ubuntu Server, Debian, Red Hat Enterprise Linux, Rocky/AlmaLinux, SUSE) Free or subscription, stable, command line, low resource use Web, DNS, database, containers, cloud Unix (FreeBSD, Solaris, AIX) Very stable, specialised Legacy, appliances, enterprise Hypervisors (VMware ESXi, Hyper-V, KVM, Proxmox) Run many virtual machines Virtualization host Key words in this topic Windows Server : GUI, Active Directory, IIS; licensed + CALs. Linux Server : Ubuntu, RHEL family; strong on web, DNS, cloud and containers. Server Core / minimal install : fewer packages = smaller attack surface. Hypervisor : runs virtual machines that each have their own OS. In this section 5.4.1 Installation procedure 5.4.2 Practical: Ubuntu Server and a static IP 5.4.3 Windows Server first tasks Picking an OS for class projects For this diploma, Ubuntu Server in a VM is the fastest path to SSH, Apache, BIND and WireGuard practice at zero licence cost. Use Windows Server evaluation ISOs when you must practise Active Directory, GUI roles or PowerShell remoting. Hypervisors let you run both side by side without buying extra PCs."},{"t":"5.4.1 Installation procedure","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-4-1.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.4.1 Installation procedure 1 Plan → 2 Back up → 3 Prepare media → 4 Boot → 5 Partition disks → 6 Admin account → 7 Network → 8 First boot tasks → 9 Document Plan: check hardware requirements, choose edition, note licence key, plan hostname, static IP, disk layout. Back up any existing data. Prepare media: download the ISO, verify its checksum , write to USB or attach to the virtual machine. Boot from media ; choose language, keyboard and installation type (use Server Core / minimal where a GUI is not needed, which reduces attack surface). Partition disks (for example separate / , /var , /home on Linux; system and data volumes on Windows). Consider RAID. Set administrator account and a strong password. Configure network: static IP, mask, gateway, DNS. First boot tasks: set hostname and time zone/NTP, apply all updates, enable firewall, remove unneeded services, create a standard user for daily tasks. Back up the clean configuration and document it. Why the order matters Skipping \"plan hostname and IP\" leads to renaming and certificate pain later. Skipping \"verify ISO checksum\" risks installing from a corrupted or tampered image. Skipping \"apply updates before exposing services\" leaves known holes open on day one. Minimal installs On Windows, Server Core (no full desktop) and on Linux a minimal package set reduce what attackers can exploit and what you must patch. Add only the roles you need. You can still manage Windows Core with PowerShell and remote tools, and Linux with SSH. Tip: write the hostname, static IP, mask, gateway, DNS and admin account in a build sheet before you start the installer. Tick each item after first boot. Remember Plan → media → install → network → harden → document. Prefer minimal GUI/packages on servers facing the network."},{"t":"5.4.2 Practical: Ubuntu Server and a static IP","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-4-2.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.4.2 Practical: Ubuntu Server and a static IP Practical: install Ubuntu Server in VirtualBox Create a VM: 2 CPUs, 2 GB RAM, 20 GB disk, network in Bridged or NAT with port forwarding. Mount the Ubuntu Server ISO and start the VM. Follow the installer; tick Install OpenSSH server . After reboot log in and run: Terminal Copy sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y hostnamectl set-hostname server1 ip a Setting a static IP on Ubuntu (Netplan) Edit the YAML file in /etc/netplan/ (for example 00-installer-config.yaml or 50-cloud-init.yaml ; the name depends on the Ubuntu version): YAML Copy network: version: 2 ethernets: enp0s3: dhcp4: false addresses: [192.168.1.10/24] routes: - to: default via: 192.168.1.1 nameservers: addresses: [8.8.8.8, 1.1.1.1] Test the change with sudo netplan try (it rolls back automatically if you lose the connection), then make it permanent with sudo netplan apply . YAML indentation matters: use spaces, never tabs. The interface name ( enp0s3 here) must match the name shown by ip a . Netplan pitfalls Ubuntu Server uses Netplan YAML files under /etc/netplan/ . Small mistakes break networking completely: Use spaces , never tabs, for indentation. Interface name must match ip a ( enp0s3 , eth0 , ens33 …). Prefix length is part of the address: 192.168.1.10/24 , not a separate old-style mask only. Prefer sudo netplan try first: it applies the config and rolls back if you lose connectivity. After a working static IP, set the hostname ( hostnamectl ), install updates, and confirm ip a , ip route and ping to the gateway. Everyday example: in VirtualBox, Bridged mode gives the VM an address on your real LAN so other PCs can open http://vm-ip . NAT alone usually cannot be reached from other computers without port forwarding. Remember Static IP + gateway + DNS belong on every server. netplan try is safer than apply while you are learning."},{"t":"5.4.3 Windows Server first tasks","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-4-3.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.4.3 Windows Server first tasks Use Server Manager to rename the computer, set a static IP (Network Connections), run Windows Update, and add roles with Add Roles and Features (or PowerShell: Install-WindowsFeature DNS -IncludeManagementTools ). PowerShell Copy Rename-Computer -NewName SRV1 -Restart New-NetIPAddress -InterfaceAlias \"Ethernet\" -IPAddress 192.168.1.20 -PrefixLength 24 -DefaultGateway 192.168.1.1 Set-DnsClientServerAddress -InterfaceAlias \"Ethernet\" -ServerAddresses 192.168.1.10 Install-WindowsFeature DNS, DHCP -IncludeManagementTools Get-WindowsFeature | Where-Object Installed These PowerShell commands do the same jobs as Server Manager. Change the interface name ( Get-NetAdapter lists them) and the addresses to suit your network. Why these first tasks come before roles Adding DNS or DHCP on a machine that still has a random hostname, a DHCP-assigned address and unpatched services creates moving targets: clients cache the wrong IP, certificates disagree with the name, and attackers find known bugs. Finish identity (name), addressing (static IP), and updates first; then add roles; then harden the firewall to match those roles only. Everyday example: a lab installs DHCP before setting a static IP on the server itself. After a reboot the server gets a new address from another DHCP box, and every client that was pointed at the old address stops resolving names. First hour on Windows Server Rename the computer and reboot ( Rename-Computer ). Set a static IP , mask, gateway and DNS ( New-NetIPAddress , Set-DnsClientServerAddress ). Check the interface name with Get-NetAdapter . Run Windows Update before adding public roles. Add only needed roles (DNS, DHCP, AD DS, File Server…) with Server Manager or Install-WindowsFeature . Enable the firewall appropriately and create a standard admin workflow (separate accounts, not shared passwords). Common mistake: leaving a server on DHCP. If its address changes, every client, DNS record and firewall rule that pointed at the old IP breaks. Remember Same goals as Linux: identity, addressing, updates, roles, firewall, documentation. PowerShell can do everything Server Manager can, and is easier to repeat."},{"t":"5.5 Server access methodologies","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-5.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.5 Server access methodologies Method Description Port Notes Local console Keyboard, monitor, mouse on the server - Needs physical presence SSH Encrypted remote command line (Linux, network devices) 22 Use key-based login; disable root login RDP Remote graphical desktop for Windows 3389 Use VPN, Network Level Authentication, strong passwords PowerShell Remoting / WinRM Remote scripting for Windows 5985/5986 Good for automation Telnet Unencrypted command line 23 Do not use Web-based panels (cPanel, Webmin, Cockpit) Manage through a browser various Protect with HTTPS and restricted access Out-of-band (iLO, iDRAC, IPMI, KVM over IP) Hardware management even when the OS is down - Place on a separate management network File transfer (SFTP, SCP, SMB) Move files 22 / 445 Prefer SFTP/SCP SSH example: Terminal Copy ssh user@192.168.1.10 ssh-keygen -t ed25519 # create a key pair on your PC ssh-copy-id user@192.168.1.10 # install your public key on the server scp report.pdf user@192.168.1.10:/home/user/ Key-based SSH: the public key is copied to the server, and the private key never leaves your computer Good practice: disable password login once keys work ( PasswordAuthentication no in /etc/ssh/sshd_config ), use least-privilege accounts, and log all administrative sessions. Remote access compared Method Encrypted? Typical use Risk if exposed SSH Yes Linux and network devices Brute force if passwords allowed RDP Yes (with NLA) Windows desktop admin Very frequently attacked on the open Internet WinRM Yes (HTTPS mode) Windows automation Must be locked to admin networks Telnet No Legacy only Passwords visible on the wire — never use iLO / iDRAC / IPMI Varies Hardware even if OS is dead Keep on a separate management VLAN SSH keys in one paragraph You create a key pair on your PC ( ssh-keygen ). The public key is copied to the server ( authorized_keys ). The private key stays on your PC and never travels. Later logins prove you hold the private key. Once that works, disable password authentication so attackers cannot guess passwords on port 22. Tip: do not publish RDP (3389) or SSH (22) straight to the whole Internet. Use a VPN ( Unit 6 ), restrict source IPs, or jump through a bastion host. Remember Prefer encrypted, key-based, least-privilege remote access. Out-of-band management saves you when the OS network stack is broken. Telnet is obsolete for administration."},{"t":"5.6 Server applications and tools","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-6.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.6 Server applications and tools Common server applications Service Linux Windows Web Apache, Nginx IIS Database MySQL/MariaDB, PostgreSQL SQL Server File sharing Samba, NFS File Server role (SMB) DHCP isc-dhcp-server, dnsmasq DHCP Server role DNS BIND9 DNS Server role Mail Postfix, Dovecot Exchange Directory OpenLDAP, FreeIPA Active Directory Domain Services Install and test a web server (Ubuntu): Terminal Copy sudo apt install apache2 -y systemctl status apache2 curl http://localhost Open a browser on another computer to http://server-ip ; the default Apache page confirms success. Content lives in /var/www/html . Administration tools Task Linux Windows Manage services systemctl start/stop/enable Services console, Get-Service View processes top , htop , ps Task Manager, Get-Process Disk usage df -h , du -sh Disk Management, Storage Spaces Users and groups adduser , usermod , chmod , chown Active Directory Users and Computers, Local Users Logs /var/log , journalctl Event Viewer Packages and updates apt , dnf Windows Update, WSUS Scheduling cron Task Scheduler Monitoring Nagios, Zabbix, Prometheus Performance Monitor, SCOM Backup rsync , tar , Bacula Windows Server Backup Linux permissions: chmod 750 file gives owner rwx (7), group r-x (5), others none (0). Services and permissions you will use daily On Linux, a background service is often called a daemon . systemctl status apache2 shows whether it is running; enable makes it start at boot; restart reloads after config changes. Logs live under /var/log/ or in the journal ( journalctl -u apache2 ). Reading chmod 750 Permissions are owner / group / others, each with r(4), w(2), x(1): 7 = 4+2+1 → owner read+write+execute 5 = 4+1 → group read+execute 0 → others nothing So chmod 750 script.sh lets only the owner fully control the file and the group run it. Web content is often 644 for files and 755 for directories — group-writable web roots are a common vulnerability. Remember Install the application that matches the role, then manage it with the OS tools ( systemctl , Services, Event Viewer). Permissions and logging are part of administration, not extras."},{"t":"5.7 Ports","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-7.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.7 Ports An IP address identifies a host; a port number (16 bits, 0-65535) identifies the application or service on that host. A socket is IP address plus port (for example 192.168.1.10:443 ). Ports can be TCP or UDP . A socket is an IP address plus a port. The server listens on one port per service; the firewall blocks ports that should not be public Range Name Use 0-1023 Well-known ports Standard services 1024-49151 Registered ports Applications (MySQL 3306, RDP 3389) 49152-65535 Dynamic / ephemeral Temporary client-side ports Port Service 20/21 FTP data / control 22 SSH, SFTP 23 Telnet 25 SMTP 53 DNS 67/68 DHCP 80 HTTP 110 POP3 143 IMAP 389 / 636 LDAP / LDAPS 443 HTTPS 445 SMB 3306 MySQL 3389 RDP Ports complete the address An IP address finds the computer; a port finds the program. Together they form a socket ( 192.168.1.10:443 ). Servers listen on well-known ports; clients pick temporary ephemeral ports for their side of the conversation. Security rule of thumb List what is listening ( ss -tulpn or netstat -ano ). For each port ask: \"Do we need this?\" If not, stop the service or block it on the firewall. Attackers scan for unexpected open ports (Telnet, old database ports, remote desktop) as easy entry points. If you see Think Port 22 open to world Restrict or put behind VPN Port 3306 on public IP Database should usually be internal only Many unknown listeners Inventory services; disable unused ones Remember Well-known 0–1023, registered 1024–49151, ephemeral above that. Open only needed ports; verify with ss / netstat / nmap on systems you own. Checking and securing ports Terminal Copy sudo ss -tulpn # Linux: list listening ports and their programs netstat -ano # Windows: connections with process IDs nmap -sT 192.168.1.10 # scan a server from another machine (only your own!) Security rule: open only the ports a server needs ; close or firewall all others. Unused open ports are attack entry points. Port forwarding on a router maps a public port to an internal server port (for example public 8080 to 192.168.1.10:80). Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 5.8 hands-on lab and the 5.9 review questions ."},{"t":"5.8 Hands-on lab: Build a Linux server","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-lab.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.8 Hands-on lab: Build a Linux server Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. Install Ubuntu Server in a VM, set a static IP, install SSH and connect from your host. Install Apache, change the default page, and view it from another computer. Create a user, set file permissions and test access. List listening ports with ss -tulpn and justify each one. Install a Windows Server evaluation VM and add the DHCP and DNS roles. Take a VM snapshot after each working step (installed, static IP, SSH, Apache). If a later change breaks the server, roll back in seconds instead of reinstalling."},{"t":"5.9 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-review.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.9 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. Server client dedicated shared VPS cloud hypervisor RAID Server Core SSH RDP port socket service daemon role package manager Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. Give three differences between a desktop OS and a server OS. Compare shared hosting and a VPS. A company needs a server for a busy online shop with traffic spikes. Which option would you suggest and why? Which port and protocol are used by SSH, HTTPS and DNS? Why should password login be replaced by key-based login on SSH? What does the command ss -tulpn show? State two post-installation tasks you must do on every new server. Show answers A server OS supports multiple simultaneous remote users and services, offers roles/services such as DNS and AD, has stronger remote management and security, supports server-grade hardware and is built for continuous uptime. (Any three.) Shared: many sites on one OS, no root access, cheapest and limited control. VPS: isolated virtual machine with its own OS and root access, more resources and flexibility, somewhat higher cost. A cloud server or auto-scaling VPS cluster, because it can scale up for traffic spikes and be billed by use; add load balancing and backups. SSH 22 (TCP); HTTPS 443 (TCP); DNS 53 (UDP, TCP for large replies/zone transfers). Keys are far harder to guess or brute-force than passwords and cannot be captured by typing; password login can then be disabled. It lists listening TCP/UDP ports and the programs using them. Apply updates, set a static IP/hostname, enable firewall, create a non-admin account, disable unneeded services, take a backup (any two). Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 5 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 5: Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05.html","x":"Unit 5 Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration Why servers need a server OS, hosting options and server selection, installing Linux and Windows servers, remote access, server applications and ports. What you will learn After this unit you can: Explain what a server is and why server operating systems and application software are required. Compare dedicated, shared, VPS, cloud and virtual servers and select a suitable one. Install and configure a server operating system. Describe and use server access methods. Install common server applications and tools. Explain ports and manage them securely. Topics in this unit 5.1 What is a server and why is a server OS required? 1 diagram ✓ 5.2 Types of servers (by role) 1 diagram ✓ 5.3 Server hosting and deployment options 1 diagram ✓ 5.3.1 Selecting a server 1 diagram ✓ 5.4 Server operating systems ✓ 5.4.1 Installation procedure ✓ 5.4.2 Practical: Ubuntu Server and a static IP 2 command examples ✓ 5.4.3 Windows Server first tasks 1 command example ✓ 5.5 Server access methodologies 1 command example · 1 diagram ✓ 5.6 Server applications and tools 1 command example ✓ 5.7 Ports 1 command example · 1 diagram · interactive lab ✓ 5.8 Hands-on lab: Build a Linux server ✓ 5.9 Review: key terms and self-check 7 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"6.1 Concept","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-1.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.1 Concept A Virtual Private Network creates an encrypted tunnel across an untrusted network (the Internet) so that remote users and sites can communicate as if on one private network. It provides: Confidentiality: data is encrypted. Integrity: data cannot be altered unnoticed. Authentication: only authorised users or devices connect. A VPN builds an encrypted tunnel across the untrusted Internet, so the remote user works as if inside the office network What a VPN does not do It does not remove viruses, stop a user pasting a password into a fake site, or fix a misconfigured firewall behind the gateway. It protects the path . Combine it with patching, MFA and least privilege from Unit 8 . VPN and the CIA triad A Virtual Private Network builds a protected path across a network you do not trust (usually the Internet): Confidentiality: encryption stops eavesdroppers on café Wi-Fi or ISP links from reading the data. Integrity: tampering with packets in transit is detected. Authentication: only users or sites with valid credentials/keys join the tunnel. After the tunnel is up, applications behave much as they would on the office LAN: you can open internal web apps, file shares or SSH hosts by their private addresses (depending on how the VPN is routed). Everyday example: a teacher at home starts the school VPN client, authenticates, and then opens an internal marks system at 10.0.0.50 as if sitting in the staff room. Neighbours on the same home Wi-Fi see only encrypted VPN packets. Remember A VPN is an encrypted tunnel across an untrusted network. It protects data in transit , not a malware-infected endpoint."},{"t":"6.2 Types of VPN","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-2.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.2 Types of VPN Type Description Example Remote-access VPN A single user connects to the organisation network from outside Staff working from home using a VPN client Site-to-site VPN Two networks (branch and head office) joined permanently by VPN gateways or routers Bank branch to head office Client-based vs clientless (SSL/browser) Software client or browser-only access SSL VPN portal Commercial / personal VPN Service used to protect traffic on public Wi-Fi or change apparent location Consumer VPN apps Remote-access VPNs connect individual users; site-to-site VPNs permanently join whole networks Choosing a VPN type Remote-access A laptop or phone runs a VPN client (or uses a browser SSL VPN portal). Best for staff who move around. Each session is per user; you can require MFA and limit which internal subnets they may reach. Site-to-site Two gateways (routers/firewalls) keep a permanent tunnel so whole LANs can route to each other. Users need no special software. Typical for branch offices. If the gateway is down, the whole site loses the path. Personal / commercial VPN apps These encrypt traffic between you and the provider and can hide your traffic from a local Wi-Fi owner. They do not by themselves give you access to your company LAN unless the company runs that service. Tip: \"Clientless\" SSL VPN is useful when you cannot install software (locked-down kiosk), but feature depth is often lower than a full tunnel client. Remember Remote-access = user to network; site-to-site = network to network. Pick the type that matches who moves: people or whole offices."},{"t":"6.3 Tunnelling and encapsulation","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-3.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.3 Tunnelling and encapsulation In tunnelling, the original packet is encrypted and wrapped inside another packet with new outer IP addresses (the VPN endpoints). Routers on the Internet see only the outer header. At the far end the packet is decrypted and delivered to the private network. Tunnelling: the original packet is encrypted and wrapped in a new packet addressed between the two VPN endpoints Encapsulation step by step Your PC creates a normal private packet (for example to 10.0.0.50 ). The VPN software encrypts that packet. It wraps the result in a new outer packet addressed from your public IP (or VPN client address) to the VPN gateway's public IP. Internet routers forward using only the outer header. They cannot read the inner addresses or payload. The gateway decrypts, unwraps, and forwards the original packet onto the private LAN. This wrapping is encapsulation (and the reverse is decapsulation). Protocols differ (IPsec ESP, WireGuard UDP payloads, OpenVPN TLS), but the idea is the same. Common mistake: assuming VPN traffic is invisible. Observers still see that you are talking to a VPN server and how much data you send; they should not see the inner contents. Remember Inner packet = real conversation; outer packet = tunnel between VPN endpoints. Encryption happens before the data crosses the untrusted network."},{"t":"6.4 VPN protocols","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-4.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.4 VPN protocols Protocol Notes IPsec (IKE + ESP/AH) Network-layer standard; common for site-to-site; modes: transport and tunnel SSL/TLS VPN (e.g. OpenVPN) Uses TLS, works over TCP/UDP 443 or 1194, passes through firewalls easily WireGuard Modern, small and fast, uses UDP (default 51820) L2TP/IPsec Tunnel with IPsec encryption PPTP Obsolete and insecure; avoid How to choose quickly Site-to-site between firewalls: IKEv2/IPsec is the usual enterprise choice. Bring-your-own-client remote access with simple config: WireGuard. Need to look like HTTPS to pass strict networks: OpenVPN on TCP 443 (or a TLS VPN portal). Old PPTP profiles in documentation: replace them; do not rebuild them. Ports must be open on the path between client and gateway. If UDP is blocked, prefer a TCP-based option or fix the middle firewall rather than disabling encryption. Protocol cheat sheet Protocol Typical ports Strengths Avoid when IPsec (IKEv2) UDP 500, 4500 Standard on firewalls; great site-to-site You need a tiny home lab and want simplest setup OpenVPN UDP/TCP 1194 or 443 Flexible; works through many firewalls You need maximum performance on weak hardware WireGuard UDP 51820 (default) Simple config, modern crypto, fast Very old devices without support L2TP/IPsec UDP 500/4500 + L2TP Built into some OS clients Complexity without benefit over IKEv2/WireGuard PPTP TCP 1723 — Always — broken crypto Remember Prefer WireGuard, OpenVPN or modern IPsec (IKEv2). PPTP is obsolete; do not deploy it."},{"t":"6.5 Benefits and limits","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-5.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.5 Benefits and limits Benefits: secure remote work, lower cost than leased lines, secure branch connectivity, protection on public Wi-Fi. Limits: extra latency and processing, requires correct configuration and key management, a compromised VPN client can bring a threat inside the network, and a VPN does not protect against malware or phishing. Best practice: use strong authentication (certificates plus multi-factor), keep VPN software updated, apply least-privilege access and log connections. Security boundary reminder Treat every VPN user as a possible entry path into the LAN. Combine VPN auth with: up-to-date endpoints, MFA, group-based routes (finance users need different internal nets than guests), logging of connect/disconnect times. A VPN without access control is only a longer network cable for attackers. Benefits, limits and split tunnelling Benefits: secure remote work, cheaper than private leased lines for branches, safer admin access than exposing RDP/SSH to the world, encryption on hostile networks. Limits: encryption adds some latency and CPU use; keys and certificates must be managed; a compromised laptop can attack the internal network through the VPN; phishing and malware are not stopped by a tunnel. Split tunnelling sends only company-destined traffic through the VPN and sends general Internet browsing straight out of the home ISP. That can improve speed but may bypass company filtering. Full tunnel sends everything through the organisation (stronger control, more load on the gateway). Remember VPN protects the path; endpoint security still matters. Design authentication (MFA), routing (full vs split) and logging deliberately."},{"t":"6.6 Quick set-up outline (WireGuard on Ubuntu)","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-6.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.6 Quick set-up outline (WireGuard on Ubuntu) Terminal Copy sudo apt install wireguard -y umask 077 # new files readable only by you wg genkey | tee server.key | wg pubkey > server.pub sudo nano /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf # add the [Interface] and [Peer] sections shown below sudo wg-quick up wg0 sudo wg show The [Interface] section holds the server's private key, tunnel address (for example 10.8.0.1/24 ) and listening port; each [Peer] holds a client's public key and allowed IP. Open the UDP port on the firewall ( sudo ufw allow 51820/udp ) and, if clients must reach the LAN behind the server, enable IP forwarding ( net.ipv4.ip_forward=1 in /etc/sysctl.conf ). Configuration file Copy [Interface] Address = 10.8.0.1/24 ListenPort = 51820 PrivateKey = <contents of server.key> [Peer] PublicKey = <the client's public key> AllowedIPs = 10.8.0.2/32 A WireGuard server on UDP 51820 gives each peer a tunnel address in 10.8.0.0/24 and forwards its traffic to the private LAN Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 6.7 hands-on lab and the 6.8 review questions . Reading the WireGuard config [Interface] on the server: its private key, tunnel IP ( 10.8.0.1/24 ), and ListenPort . [Peer] : each client’s public key and the tunnel IP that client is allowed to use ( AllowedIPs = 10.8.0.2/32 ). Clients have the reverse view: their private key, and a Peer entry for the server’s public key with Endpoint = public-ip:51820 and AllowedIPs set to the networks they should reach through the tunnel. Why IP forwarding? If clients must talk to the LAN behind the server (not only to 10.8.0.1 ), the server must route packets between wg0 and the LAN interface. That requires net.ipv4.ip_forward=1 and matching firewall rules (NAT or routing + allow). Tip: protect private keys with tight file permissions ( umask 077 ). Anyone with the server private key can impersonate the VPN. Remember WireGuard peers authenticate with public keys; configure AllowedIPs carefully. Open UDP 51820 (or your chosen port) on the firewall."},{"t":"6.7 Hands-on lab: Build a VPN","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-lab.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.7 Hands-on lab: Build a VPN Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. Build a WireGuard or OpenVPN server in a VM and connect a second VM or phone as a client. Confirm the client receives a tunnel IP and can reach a private server ( ping 10.8.0.1 ). Use Wireshark to show that traffic on the physical interface is encrypted. In Wireshark, capture on the physical network adapter while you ping through the tunnel: you should see only UDP packets to port 51820 (WireGuard) with unreadable contents, not the ICMP pings."},{"t":"6.8 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-review.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.8 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. Tunnel IPsec TLS remote-access VPN site-to-site VPN encapsulation authentication key Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. Define a VPN and give its three security benefits. Differentiate remote-access and site-to-site VPNs. Why is PPTP no longer recommended? Does a VPN make a malware-infected computer safe? Explain. Show answers A VPN is an encrypted tunnel across an untrusted network; benefits are confidentiality, integrity and authentication. Remote-access connects individual users to a network; site-to-site joins two networks through gateways. PPTP's authentication (MS-CHAPv2) and encryption (MPPE, based on RC4) have well-known weaknesses: a captured login can be cracked quickly and the traffic then decrypted. It is obsolete and should be replaced by WireGuard, OpenVPN or IPsec (IKEv2). No. A VPN protects data in transit only; the infected computer can still spread malware through the tunnel. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 6 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 6: Virtual Private Network (VPN)","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06.html","x":"Unit 6 Virtual Private Network (VPN) Virtual private networks: why they are used, remote-access and site-to-site VPNs, tunnelling, VPN protocols and a WireGuard set-up. What you will learn After this unit you can: Explain what a VPN is and why it is used. Compare site-to-site and remote-access VPNs. Describe tunnelling and common VPN protocols. Set up a simple VPN. Topics in this unit 6.1 Concept 1 diagram ✓ 6.2 Types of VPN 1 diagram ✓ 6.3 Tunnelling and encapsulation 1 diagram ✓ 6.4 VPN protocols ✓ 6.5 Benefits and limits ✓ 6.6 Quick set-up outline (WireGuard on Ubuntu) 2 command examples · 1 diagram ✓ 6.7 Hands-on lab: Build a VPN ✓ 6.8 Review: key terms and self-check 4 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"7.1 Why DNS?","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-1.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.1 Why DNS? People remember names ( www.example.com ), computers route by IP addresses ( 93.184.216.34 ). DNS is a distributed, hierarchical database that translates names to IP addresses (and back), and it also stores mail routing and other information. DNS translates the name people type into the IP address computers use, before the real connection starts DNS failure patterns students should recognise What works What fails Likely cause Ping to 8.8.8.8 Browser names fail DNS servers wrong or unreachable Nothing by IP or name — Addressing, cable or gateway Internal names only Internet names Forwarders missing on internal DNS One name only Others OK Bad or stale record / wrong TTL cache Always test with both a name and a raw IP before blaming \"the Internet\". Life without DNS Imagine typing 93.184.216.34 instead of a website name, and updating every bookmark whenever a server moves to a new IP. DNS stores the mapping in a distributed database so people use stable names while networks still route with IP addresses . It also answers questions like \"which host accepts mail for this domain?\" (MX records). A lookup usually happens in milliseconds and is cached. When DNS fails, it often looks like \"the Internet is down\" even though ping to an IP still works — a classic troubleshooting pattern (see Unit 9 ). Everyday example: you type www.school.lk . Your resolver finds an A record such as 203.0.113.10 . Your browser then opens a TCP connection to that IP on port 443. DNS finished before the web traffic started. Remember DNS translates names ↔ IP addresses (and stores other data such as MX). Users depend on DNS even when they never configure it manually."},{"t":"7.2 DNS architecture","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-2.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.2 DNS architecture The namespace is an inverted tree : Root (.) : 13 logical root server addresses (A to M), operated by many organisations with many mirrored instances. Top-Level Domains (TLD): generic ( .com , .org , .net ), country-code ( .lk , .uk , .in ) and newer generic TLDs such as .app and .shop . Second-level domain: the registered name ( example in example.com ). Subdomain / host: www , mail and so on. The DNS namespace is an inverted tree: the root, top-level domains, second-level domains, then hosts and subdomains A Fully Qualified Domain Name (FQDN) names a host completely: www.example.com. (the trailing dot is the root). A zone is the portion of the namespace administered by one set of name servers; a zone file holds its records. DNS server roles Role Function Authoritative server Holds the official records for a zone (primary/master and secondary/slave, kept in sync by zone transfer ) Recursive resolver Asks other servers on the client's behalf and caches answers (your ISP, 8.8.8.8, 1.1.1.1) Caching server Stores answers for the record's TTL to speed up repeated lookups Forwarder Passes queries it cannot answer to another resolver Root and TLD servers Direct resolvers down the tree Hierarchy keeps the system scalable No single server holds every name on Earth. Authority is delegated down the tree: root → TLD ( .lk , .com ) → organisation ( school.lk ) → hosts ( www.school.lk ). Each zone is a slice of that tree managed by a set of authoritative name servers. Roles you must separate mentally Authoritative server: answers for zones it owns (\"I am the official source for school.lk\"). Recursive resolver: does the walking of the tree for the client and caches answers (ISP DNS, 1.1.1.1 , a local BIND resolver). Forwarder: a resolver that hands hard work to another resolver (common on small internal DNS servers). Primary (master) holds the writable zone data; secondaries (slaves) copy it with zone transfers so the domain still resolves if one server dies. Remember DNS is hierarchical: root, TLD, domain, host. Authoritative ≠ recursive; many problems come from mixing those roles carelessly (open resolvers)."},{"t":"7.3 Name resolution step by step","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-3.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.3 Name resolution step by step A client wants www.example.com : The client checks its own cache and the hosts file. It asks its configured recursive resolver . If not cached, the resolver asks a root server , which refers it to the .com TLD servers. The TLD server refers it to the authoritative server for example.com . The authoritative server returns the IP address. The resolver caches the answer (for the TTL) and returns it to the client. Resolving www.example.com with an empty cache: the resolver is referred from the root to the TLD to the authoritative server Queries use UDP port 53 (TCP 53 for large responses and zone transfers). Recursive queries ask the server to find the complete answer; iterative queries accept a referral. Recursive vs iterative Recursive query (client → its resolver): \"Give me the final answer.\" The resolver does the work. Iterative query (resolver → other servers): \"Tell me the best next hop.\" Root and TLD servers normally answer with referrals, not the final A record. Caching and TTL Every record has a TTL (time to live) in seconds. Resolvers cache answers that long. Lower TTL means changes propagate faster but produce more queries; higher TTL reduces load but slows updates. After you change an A record, old cached values can linger until TTLs expire — that is propagation delay . Queries normally use UDP/53 . TCP/53 is used for large responses and for zone transfers between name servers. Tip: when testing a change, query the authoritative server directly ( dig www.school.lk @ns1.school.lk ) so a public cache cannot hide the update. Remember Resolver asks root → TLD → authoritative, then caches. UDP/53 common; TCP/53 for big answers and zone transfers."},{"t":"7.4 DNS record types","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-4.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.4 DNS record types Record Purpose Example A Name to IPv4 address www IN A 192.0.2.10 AAAA Name to IPv6 address www IN AAAA 2001:db8::10 CNAME Alias to another name web IN CNAME www.example.com. MX Mail server for the domain (with priority) @ IN MX 10 mail.example.com. NS Name servers for the zone @ IN NS ns1.example.com. PTR IP address to name (reverse lookup) 10 IN PTR www.example.com. SOA Start of Authority: primary server, serial, refresh timers one per zone TXT Free text; used for SPF, DKIM, domain verification \"v=spf1 mx -all\" SRV Location of a service used by Active Directory, SIP The trailing dot matters. In a zone file mail.example.com. is a complete name. Without the final dot BIND adds the zone name, so mail.example.com becomes mail.example.com.example.com. When to use each record Record Use it for A / AAAA Point a name at an IPv4 / IPv6 address CNAME Alias one name to another (do not place CNAMEs where other record types must exist, such as on the zone apex in many setups) MX Mail servers; lower preference number = higher priority NS Delegate or publish the name servers for a zone PTR Reverse DNS (IP → name); needed for many mail servers TXT SPF, DKIM, domain proof for certificates or Google/Microsoft SOA Zone identity and timers; one per zone; serial must increase on edits The trailing-dot rule In zone files, a name ending with a dot is absolute ( www.school.lk. ). Without the dot, the zone origin is appended. Writing www.school.lk inside zone school.lk can accidentally become www.school.lk.school.lk . Common mistake: forgetting to bump the SOA serial after an edit. Secondaries may never fetch the new data. Remember A/AAAA for hosts, MX for mail, NS for delegation, PTR for reverse. Absolute names end with a dot in zone files."},{"t":"7.5 Configuring a DNS server","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-5.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.5 Configuring a DNS server A DNS server can be built on Linux with BIND9 or on Windows Server with the DNS Server role. The pages below configure the same example zone, school.lk , on both, then add a reverse zone, test the server and protect it. Key words in this topic Forward zone : name → IP (e.g. school.lk ). Reverse zone : IP → name ( 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa ). Primary / secondary : where the data is edited vs copied. Forwarder : where your server sends queries it cannot answer authoritatively. In this section 7.5.1 BIND9 on Ubuntu 7.5.2 Windows Server DNS 7.5.3 Reverse zones, management and testing 7.5.4 DNS attacks and protection"},{"t":"7.5.1 BIND9 on Ubuntu","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-5-1.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.5.1 BIND9 on Ubuntu Terminal Copy sudo apt install bind9 bind9utils -y In /etc/bind/named.conf.local declare the zone: Configuration file Copy zone \"school.lk\" { type master; file \"/etc/bind/db.school.lk\"; }; Create /etc/bind/db.school.lk : Newer BIND versions also accept type primary; , the modern name for type master; . DNS zone file Copy $TTL 3600 @ IN SOA ns1.school.lk. admin.school.lk. ( 2025010101 ; serial 3600 ; refresh 900 ; retry 604800 ; expire 3600 ) ; negative cache TTL @ IN NS ns1.school.lk. ns1 IN A 192.168.1.10 www IN A 192.168.1.20 mail IN A 192.168.1.25 @ IN MX 10 mail.school.lk. Check and apply: Terminal Copy sudo named-checkconf sudo named-checkzone school.lk /etc/bind/db.school.lk sudo systemctl restart bind9 Remember to increase the serial number whenever you edit the zone. Also allow DNS in the firewall ( sudo ufw allow 53 ). Minimal zone mental model SOA (zone meta + serial) → NS (who is authoritative) → A/AAAA (hosts) → MX/TXT as required. If dig against 127.0.0.1 works but LAN clients fail, check listen addresses, firewall UDP/TCP 53, and whether clients really use this server as their resolver. BIND workflow Declare the zone in named.conf.local (zone name, type primary/master, file path). Create the zone file with SOA, NS, A/AAAA and other records. Run named-checkconf and named-checkzone school.lk /etc/bind/db.school.lk . Reload ( systemctl reload bind9 ) and test with dig . Allow UDP/TCP 53 on the firewall only as required (authoritative to the world, or recursive only to your LAN). Increase the serial (often YYYYMMDDnn ) on every change so secondaries notice updates. Remember Check config → check zone → reload → dig. Serial number changes are mandatory for transfers to see updates."},{"t":"7.5.2 Windows Server DNS","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-5-2.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.5.2 Windows Server DNS Server Manager > Add Roles and Features > DNS Server . Open DNS Manager , right-click Forward Lookup Zones > New Zone > Primary zone > name school.lk . Right-click the zone > New Host (A) to add records; tick Create associated PTR record if a reverse zone exists. Configure Forwarders (for example 8.8.8.8) on the server properties. Point clients' DNS setting to this server (usually through DHCP). Checklist after the zone exists Create A records for servers students must reach. Create PTR records if mail or policy requires reverse DNS. Set forwarders for Internet lookups. On DHCP, push your DNS server IP to clients. From a client: nslookup www.school.lk and confirm the answering server is yours. If clients still use 8.8.8.8 directly, they will never see your internal-only names. Windows DNS in AD environments On Windows Server, the DNS role often integrates with Active Directory . Creating a primary forward lookup zone for school.lk , then adding New Host (A) records (with optional PTR creation), gives names to servers and clients. Set forwarders (for example 8.8.8.8 or your ISP) so internal DNS can resolve Internet names. Point DHCP at your DNS server so clients learn it automatically. Tip: dynamic updates can register client names automatically in AD-integrated zones — useful, but restrict who may update. Remember DNS Manager: zones, records, forwarders. Clients must use your DNS IP (via DHCP) or they never see internal names."},{"t":"7.5.3 Reverse zones, management and testing","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-5-3.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.5.3 Reverse zones, management and testing Reverse lookup zones For the 192.168.1.0/24 network the reverse zone is 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa ; PTR records map the last octet to a name. A reverse lookup writes the network octets backwards under in-addr.arpa; a PTR record maps the address back to a name Configuration file Copy zone \"1.168.192.in-addr.arpa\" { type master; file \"/etc/bind/db.192.168.1\"; }; DNS zone file Copy $TTL 3600 @ IN SOA ns1.school.lk. admin.school.lk. ( 2025010101 3600 900 604800 3600 ) @ IN NS ns1.school.lk. 10 IN PTR ns1.school.lk. 20 IN PTR www.school.lk. 25 IN PTR mail.school.lk. Managing and testing DNS Terminal Copy nslookup www.school.lk 192.168.1.10 dig www.school.lk @192.168.1.10 dig MX school.lk ipconfig /flushdns # Windows: clear client cache resolvectl flush-caches # Linux Management tasks: add, change and delete records, set suitable TTLs, keep secondary servers in sync, secure zone transfers (allow only listed secondaries), enable logging, back up zone files, and consider DNSSEC (signs records so answers can be verified). Reverse DNS idea IPv4 reverse lookups use the in-addr.arpa domain with octets reversed. For 192.168.1.10 , the PTR name is 10.1.168.192.in-addr.arpa . Mail servers often reject or flag mail from hosts without matching PTR records. Testing commands Shell Copy dig www.school.lk +short dig @127.0.0.1 school.lk NS nslookup 192.168.1.10 Compare answers from your server versus a public resolver to see caching effects. Remember Reverse zone name = reversed network octets + .in-addr.arpa . Test with dig/nslookup against a specific server using @ ."},{"t":"7.5.4 DNS attacks and protection","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-5-4.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.5.4 DNS attacks and protection Cache poisoning/spoofing, DNS amplification DDoS, tunnelling and hijacking. Protect with DNSSEC, restricted recursion (do not run an open resolver), rate limiting, patching, firewall rules and registrar account security (strong password and multi-factor authentication). Hardening one-liners Recursion: allow only LAN nets, not any . Zone transfers: allow only listed secondary IPs. Keep BIND/Windows DNS patched. Monitor for sudden spikes in query rate (amplification abuse). Registrar: MFA + lock so NS records cannot be hijacked quietly. Attacks and simple defences Attack Idea Defence Cache poisoning Feed a resolver a fake answer DNSSEC validation; keep software patched; limit who may query Amplification DDoS Small spoofed query → large reply to victim Disable open recursion; rate-limit; filter spoofing Domain hijacking Steal registrar login, change NS MFA on registrar; registrar lock DNS tunnelling Hide data inside DNS queries Monitor odd DNS patterns; restrict outbound DNS An open resolver (anyone on the Internet may recurse through you) is a gift to attackers. Authoritative servers should answer for their zones; recursion should be limited to trusted client networks. Remember Patch, restrict recursion, protect registrar accounts, consider DNSSEC. DNS security is both server configuration and account hygiene."},{"t":"7.6 Obtaining and registering a domain","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-6.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.6 Obtaining and registering a domain Who is involved: ICANN coordinates the namespace; a registry runs each TLD (for example Verisign for .com ; LK Domain Registry for .lk ); a registrar is the company you buy from; the registrant is you. Who is involved in a domain name: ICANN coordinates, a registry runs each TLD, a registrar sells names, and you are the registrant Steps to register: Choose a name: short, memorable, relevant, easy to spell; check trademarks. Check availability with the registrar's search or a whois lookup. Select the TLD ( .com , .org , .lk and so on; some zones have eligibility rules and require documents, for example .ac.lk for academic institutions). Pick an accredited registrar and compare price, renewal cost and support. Provide registrant details (name, address, email, phone) and pay; registration is for 1 to 10 years. Enable protections: WHOIS privacy, auto-renew, registrar lock and two-factor authentication. Set name servers: use the registrar's DNS, a hosting provider's, or your own (create glue records if your name servers are inside the domain, for example ns1.example.com ). Create records: A/AAAA for the website, MX for email, TXT for SPF/DKIM and verification. Wait for propagation: changes may take minutes to 48 hours depending on TTL; verify with dig or an online checker. Renew on time or the domain may expire and be lost. Subdomains (for example portal.example.com ) are free to create once you own the domain. Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 7.7 hands-on lab and the 7.8 review questions . Who does what ICANN coordinates global names and numbers at a high level. A registry operates a TLD (for example the organisation that runs .lk ). A registrar sells you a name under that TLD. You are the registrant . After purchase Set name servers, create A/MX/TXT records, enable auto-renew and account lock, and document the expiry date. If your name servers are inside the domain ( ns1.example.com ), the parent needs glue A/AAAA records so resolvers can find them without a chicken-and-egg problem. Remember Registration is a contract with renewals; missed renewal can lose the name. DNS records at the DNS host are separate from paying the registrar — both must be correct."},{"t":"7.7 Hands-on lab: DNS lookups and a DNS server","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-lab.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.7 Hands-on lab: DNS lookups and a DNS server Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. Use nslookup and dig to find the A, MX and NS records of three websites. Run dig +trace www.example.com and identify the root, TLD and authoritative stages. Build a BIND9 or Windows DNS server for school.lk (or another name you control; avoid .local , which is reserved for multicast DNS) with A, CNAME, MX and PTR records; point a client at it and test. Make a mistake on purpose (wrong serial or missing dot) and fix it using named-checkzone . Look up the registrar and expiry date of a domain with whois ."},{"t":"7.8 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-review.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.8 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. DNS FQDN root TLD zone authoritative recursive resolver TTL A/AAAA/CNAME/MX/NS/PTR/SOA/TXT zone transfer DNSSEC registrar registry registrant glue record Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. What problem does DNS solve? List the components of the FQDN mail.dept.school.lk. Explain the difference between an authoritative and a recursive server. Which record type would you create for an email server and for an alias? What must you change in a zone file after every edit and why? State the steps to register a domain and make a website available on it. Which port and protocol does DNS use, and when does it use TCP? Show answers It translates human-readable names to IP addresses (and other data) so people need not remember numbers. Host mail ; subdomain dept ; second-level domain school ; TLD lk ; root ( . ). An authoritative server holds the official records for a zone; a recursive resolver finds answers by querying other servers and caches them. MX (mail server, together with an A record for the mail host); CNAME (alias). Increase the SOA serial number so secondary servers know the zone changed and transfer it. Choose a name and check that it is available (registrar search or whois ). Select the TLD and an accredited registrar. Register with your details and pay; enable WHOIS privacy, auto-renew, registrar lock and two-factor authentication. Set the name servers (registrar, hosting provider or your own). Create the records: A/AAAA for the website, MX for email, TXT for SPF and verification. Wait for propagation (depends on TTL) and test with dig . Upload the site to the server the A record points to, and renew the domain on time. UDP 53; TCP 53 for large responses and zone transfers. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 7 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 7: Domain Name System (DNS)","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07.html","x":"Unit 7 Domain Name System (DNS) The Domain Name System: its hierarchy, how names are resolved, record types, configuring BIND9 and Windows DNS, and registering a domain. What you will learn After this unit you can: Explain the purpose of DNS and describe its hierarchical architecture. Describe the name-resolution process and record types. Install, configure and manage a DNS server. Explain how to obtain and register a domain name. Topics in this unit 7.1 Why DNS? 1 diagram ✓ 7.2 DNS architecture 1 diagram ✓ 7.3 Name resolution step by step 1 diagram ✓ 7.4 DNS record types ✓ 7.5 Configuring a DNS server ✓ 7.5.1 BIND9 on Ubuntu 4 command examples ✓ 7.5.2 Windows Server DNS ✓ 7.5.3 Reverse zones, management and testing 3 command examples · 1 diagram ✓ 7.5.4 DNS attacks and protection ✓ 7.6 Obtaining and registering a domain 1 diagram ✓ 7.7 Hands-on lab: DNS lookups and a DNS server ✓ 7.8 Review: key terms and self-check 7 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"8.1 Why security settings matter","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-1.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.1 Why security settings matter Servers hold valuable data and run critical services, and they are attacked continuously. Security settings protect the CIA triad : Confidentiality: only authorised people can read data. Integrity: data is not changed without detection. Availability: services are usable when needed. The CIA triad: every security setting protects confidentiality, integrity or availability Common threats: malware and ransomware, phishing, brute-force password attacks, unpatched vulnerabilities, denial of service (DoS/DDoS), man-in-the-middle attacks, misconfiguration and insider misuse. Essential security settings checklist (server hardening) Keep the OS and applications patched ; enable automatic security updates. Remove unneeded software and disable unused services and ports . Use strong passwords and multi-factor authentication; lock out repeated failures. Apply least privilege : normal accounts for daily work, administrator rights only when required; disable direct root/Administrator login over the network. Use SSH keys , change default accounts and passwords on all devices. Enable the host firewall and a network firewall. Use encryption : HTTPS/TLS for services, disk encryption, encrypted backups. Enable logging and monitoring and review logs; synchronise time (NTP) so logs agree. Use antimalware and, where suitable, intrusion detection (IDS/IPS). Keep tested backups (the 3-2-1 rule: 3 copies, 2 media types, 1 off-site). Separate networks (VLANs, DMZ) and restrict management access. Defence in depth: several independent layers of protection, so one failure does not expose the data Threats in everyday language Threat What happens Simple defence Malware / ransomware Software damages or encrypts data Patching, least privilege, backups, antimalware Phishing User is tricked into giving secrets Training, MFA, email filtering Brute force Guess passwords on SSH/RDP Keys, MFA, lockouts, block public exposure Unpatched bug Known hole left open Automatic security updates DoS / DDoS Service flooded until unusable Capacity, provider protection, rate limits Man-in-the-middle Attacker sits on the path and reads/alters TLS/HTTPS, VPNs, avoid hostile Wi-Fi Misconfiguration Open database, default password Hardening checklists, reviews Insider misuse Authorised person abuses access Least privilege, logging, separation of duties Defence in depth One control fails eventually. Layer firewalls, patched hosts, encryption, backups and trained users so that a single miss does not equal a full breach. The checklist on this page is the practical hardening list for student servers — apply it on every new build. Remember CIA: confidentiality, integrity, availability. Hardening is continuous (patch, reduce services, monitor), not a one-time install option."},{"t":"8.2 Firewall","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-2.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.2 Firewall A firewall is hardware or software that permits or blocks network traffic according to security rules, acting as a barrier between networks of different trust levels (for example the Internet and the LAN). A firewall sits between networks of different trust and allows or blocks each connection according to its rules A firewall allows or blocks traffic using rules. It is usually the first technical control between an untrusted network and your servers, and it also runs as a host firewall on each machine. Key words in this topic Packet filter / stateful / proxy / NGFW / WAF : increasing awareness of connections and applications. Default deny : block everything not explicitly allowed. DMZ : network segment for public-facing servers. ACL : access control list of permit/deny entries. In this section 8.2.1 Types of firewall 8.2.2 Rule design, DMZ and ACLs 8.2.3 Configuring firewalls: UFW, Windows and iptables"},{"t":"8.2.1 Types of firewall","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-2-1.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.2.1 Types of firewall Type How it works Notes Packet-filtering Checks source/destination IP, port and protocol of each packet Fast, simple; no knowledge of connection state Stateful inspection Tracks connection state and allows replies to permitted outbound traffic Standard in routers and servers Proxy / application gateway Acts as intermediary and inspects application data Deep control, slower Next-Generation Firewall (NGFW) Adds application awareness, intrusion prevention, user identity, TLS inspection Enterprise networks Web Application Firewall (WAF) Protects web apps from SQL injection, XSS In front of web servers Host-based vs network-based Runs on one computer vs protects a whole network Use both (defence in depth) Placement Border NGFW/firewall: between ISP and your LAN/DMZ. Host firewall: on each server (UFW, firewalld, Windows Firewall). WAF: only in front of web applications that need HTTP-aware protection. Rules at the border do not replace host firewalls: an infected laptop inside the LAN can still attack a server unless the host filters that traffic too. Which firewall type fits? Packet filters are fast but cannot tell whether a packet belongs to a real conversation. Stateful firewalls track connections: if you allow outbound HTTPS, the return packets are allowed automatically. Home routers and ufw / firewalld work this way. Proxies terminate the session and rebuild it, inspecting content deeply (good for policy, costlier in latency). NGFW add application identity (\"block Facebook but allow HTTPS to the bank\") and often IDS/IPS features. WAF sit in front of web apps to catch SQL injection and XSS that network firewalls miss. Use network and host firewalls together: the perimeter blocks the Internet; the host still protects itself if something bypasses the perimeter. Remember Stateful inspection is the baseline for modern networks. Host + network firewalls = defence in depth."},{"t":"8.2.2 Rule design, DMZ and ACLs","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-2-2.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.2.2 Rule design, DMZ and ACLs Firewall rule design Rules are checked top to bottom ; the first match wins. Apply default deny : block everything not explicitly allowed. Allow only required services (for example inbound 80, 443; SSH only from the admin network). Document each rule and review regularly. DMZ (demilitarised zone): a separate network segment for public-facing servers (web, mail) so that if one is compromised the internal LAN is still protected by a second firewall. A DMZ holds public-facing servers between two firewalls, so a compromised web server does not open the internal LAN Access control lists (ACLs) Routers and switches use ACLs to permit or deny packets; for example, a standard ACL filters by source IP only, an extended ACL by source, destination, protocol and port. Writing rules that stay understandable Good policy starts with default deny inbound on servers: only open the ports each role needs (80/443 for web, 53 for DNS, and so on). Outbound rules may be looser on clients but tighter on hardened servers. Order matters on many platforms: first match wins. Put specific denies/allows before general ones, and document why each rule exists. DMZ idea Public web servers belong in a DMZ VLAN/segment that can talk to the Internet on limited ports, and may talk to internal databases only on the exact port required — not full LAN access. If the web server is compromised, the blast radius is smaller. Everyday example: allow Internet → DMZ:443; allow DMZ → internal DB:3306 from web server IP only; deny DMZ → internal LAN otherwise. Remember Default deny; least ports; document rules. DMZ separates public servers from internal trust."},{"t":"8.2.3 Configuring firewalls: UFW, Windows and iptables","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-2-3.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.2.3 Configuring firewalls: UFW, Windows and iptables On this page UFW on Ubuntu Windows Defender Firewall iptables on Linux UFW on Ubuntu Terminal Copy sudo ufw default deny incoming sudo ufw default allow outgoing sudo ufw allow 22/tcp # SSH sudo ufw allow 80/tcp # HTTP sudo ufw allow 443/tcp # HTTPS sudo ufw allow from 192.168.1.0/24 to any port 3306 # database only from LAN sudo ufw enable sudo ufw status verbose Windows Defender Firewall Open Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security; create an Inbound Rule > Port > TCP 443 > Allow the connection > choose profiles (Domain/Private/Public) > name the rule. Use New-NetFirewallRule in PowerShell for automation. iptables on Linux Terminal Copy sudo iptables -A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT # local traffic sudo iptables -A INPUT -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT # replies sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT # SSH sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT # HTTP sudo iptables -A INPUT -j DROP # everything else sudo iptables -L -n -v --line-numbers Do not lock yourself out. A rule set that only allows port 80 and then drops everything also blocks SSH, local (loopback) traffic and the replies to the server's own connections, so a remote server becomes unreachable. Allow loopback, established connections and SSH first. iptables rules are lost at reboot unless saved (for example with the iptables-persistent package). Commands mean policy On Ubuntu UFW : Shell Copy sudo ufw default deny incoming sudo ufw default allow outgoing sudo ufw allow 22/tcp sudo ufw allow 80,443/tcp sudo ufw enable sudo ufw status verbose This denies unsolicited inbound traffic, keeps outbound open, and permits SSH and web. Always allow your admin access method before enabling a firewall on a remote VM, or you can lock yourself out. Windows Defender Firewall uses inbound/outbound rules per profile (Domain/Private/Public). iptables / nftables are the lower-level Linux tools UFW wraps. Common mistake: enabling UFW with no allow rule for SSH while connected only over SSH — you lose access until console recovery. Remember Set defaults, allow needed ports, then enable. Test from another machine; keep a console/out-of-band path."},{"t":"8.3 Cryptography basics","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-3.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.3 Cryptography basics Encryption converts readable plaintext into unreadable ciphertext with an algorithm and a key ; decryption reverses it. Security depends on the secrecy of the key, not the algorithm. Cryptography protects data mathematically: secrecy (encryption), integrity (hashing) and strong authentication (public-key signatures). TLS on HTTPS combines these ideas. Key words in this topic Symmetric : same key encrypts and decrypts. Asymmetric : public encrypts or verifies; private decrypts or signs. Hash : one-way fingerprint of data. Hybrid cryptography : asymmetric to share a symmetric session key (TLS). In this section 8.3.1 Symmetric encryption 8.3.2 Asymmetric (public-key) encryption 8.3.3 Comparison and the hybrid approach 8.3.4 Hashing Read the four short topics in order: symmetric, asymmetric, how TLS combines them, then hashing. Together they explain HTTPS, VPN payloads, password storage and digital signatures in 8.4 – 8.5 ."},{"t":"8.3.1 Symmetric encryption","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-3-1.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.3.1 Symmetric encryption The same secret key encrypts and decrypts. Symmetric encryption: the same secret key both encrypts and decrypts, so it must be shared safely in advance Algorithms: AES (128/192/256-bit, the current standard), ChaCha20; older DES and 3DES are obsolete. Advantages: very fast, efficient for large data (files, disks, VPN traffic). Disadvantage: the key distribution problem : how do two parties share the key securely? With n users who each need private channels, n(n-1)/2 keys are required. Key length and secrecy Use modern algorithms and lengths recommended by current standards (for example AES-128 or AES-256 in a proper mode). Never invent your own cipher. Rotate keys when people leave or devices are lost. VPN and disk-encryption tools manage these keys for you — your job is to protect passphrases and key files. Symmetric encryption Algorithms such as AES use one shared secret. They are fast and suit bulk data (disk encryption, VPN payloads, TLS session data). The hard part is key distribution : both sides must receive the same secret without exposing it. If the key leaks, past and present ciphertext may be readable. Remember Same key both ways; fast; protect the key carefully."},{"t":"8.3.2 Asymmetric (public-key) encryption","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-3-2.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.3.2 Asymmetric (public-key) encryption A key pair is used: a public key (shared with everyone) and a private key (kept secret). What one key encrypts, only the other can decrypt. Asymmetric encryption: anyone can encrypt with Bob's public key, but only Bob's private key can decrypt Algorithms: RSA (2048-bit or more), ECC (elliptic curves), Diffie-Hellman for key exchange. Encryption for confidentiality: the sender encrypts with the receiver's public key; only the receiver's private key can decrypt. Advantage: solves key distribution; supports digital signatures. Disadvantage: much slower than symmetric encryption. What each key may do (summary) Action Key used Encrypt a message to Alice Alice's public key Decrypt that message Alice's private key Sign a message as Alice Alice's private key Verify Alice's signature Alice's public key Sharing the private key destroys the model. Certificates exist so you can trust that a public key really belongs to Alice's server name. Public and private keys In asymmetric crypto (RSA, elliptic curves): The public key can be shared widely. The private key must stay secret. Typically: encrypt with the recipient's public key so only their private key decrypts; or sign with your private key so anyone with your public key can verify it was you. Everyday example: your browser uses the web server's public key (inside its certificate) at the start of HTTPS to help establish secrecy with that server. Remember Public ≠ secret; private stays private. Encryption for secrecy and signing for authenticity are different operations."},{"t":"8.3.3 Comparison and the hybrid approach","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-3-3.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.3.3 Comparison and the hybrid approach Comparison Feature Symmetric Asymmetric Keys One shared secret key Public and private key pair Speed Fast Slow Key distribution Difficult Easy (public key can be shared openly) Typical use Bulk data, disk and VPN encryption Key exchange, digital signatures, certificates Examples AES, ChaCha20 RSA, ECC, Diffie-Hellman Hybrid approach (how HTTPS works) Real systems combine both: asymmetric cryptography (and certificates) authenticate the server and securely agree on a session key ; then fast symmetric encryption protects the data. HTTPS combines both: slow asymmetric cryptography agrees a session key, then fast symmetric encryption protects all the data Why TLS is hybrid Asymmetric crypto is too slow to encrypt every byte of a video stream. Symmetric crypto is fast but needs a shared key. TLS uses asymmetric cryptography (and certificates) so client and server can agree on a fresh session key , then uses symmetric encryption for the bulk transfer. You get both safety of key exchange and speed of AES. Remember Hybrid = asymmetric handshake + symmetric session. This is the pattern behind HTTPS and many VPNs."},{"t":"8.3.4 Hashing","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-3-4.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.3.4 Hashing A hash function (SHA-256, SHA-3) turns any data into a fixed-length digest . It is one-way (cannot be reversed), and any tiny change in the data gives a completely different digest. Uses: file integrity checks, password storage (with a unique salt and a deliberately slow algorithm such as bcrypt or Argon2), and digital signatures. MD5 and SHA-1 are broken and should not be used for security. Real SHA-256 digests: a one-letter change gives a completely different digest, and every digest has the same length Terminal Copy sha256sum file.iso # Linux Get-FileHash file.iso # Windows PowerShell Hashing is not encryption A hash (SHA-256, etc.) maps data to a short fingerprint. You cannot reverse a hash to get the original data. Change one bit of input → completely different hash. Uses: Verify downloads (compare published checksum). Digital signatures (sign the hash of a message). Store password verifiers (with salt) instead of plain passwords. Encryption is designed to be reversed with a key. Hashing is designed not to be reversed. Do not call a hash \"encrypted password\". Remember Hash = integrity / fingerprint; encryption = secrecy. Password storage uses slow hashes + salts, not reversible encryption."},{"t":"8.4 Digital signature","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-4.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.4 Digital signature A digital signature proves who created a message (authentication), that it was not altered (integrity) and that the signer cannot deny it (non-repudiation). Signing: the sender (1) computes a hash of the message, (2) encrypts the hash with the sender's private key ; the result is the signature, sent with the message. Verifying: the receiver (1) hashes the received message, (2) decrypts the signature with the sender's public key , and (3) compares the two hashes. If they match, the message is authentic and unchanged. Signing uses the sender's private key; anyone can verify with the sender's public key. Matching digests prove origin and integrity Note the reversal: encryption for secrecy uses the receiver's public key; a signature uses the sender's private key. Task Key used Encrypt a message for secrecy receiver's public key Decrypt the message receiver's private key Sign a message sender's private key Verify a signature sender's public key \"Encrypting the hash with the private key\" describes RSA signatures. Other algorithms, such as ECDSA and Ed25519, calculate the signature differently, but the rule is the same: sign with the private key, verify with the public key. Digital signatures To sign: hash the document, encrypt/sign that hash with your private key, attach the signature. To verify: hash the document again, and check the signature with the sender's public key. Success proves (1) the document was not altered and (2) someone holding that private key signed it ( non-repudiation , in practical terms). Certificates bind public keys to identities so verifiers know whose public key they are using (see 8.5 ). Remember Private key signs; public key verifies. Signature ≠ encryption of the whole file for secrecy; it proves origin and integrity."},{"t":"8.5 Digital certificate","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-5.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.5 Digital certificate How do you know a public key really belongs to the claimed owner? A digital certificate (standard X.509 ) binds a public key to an identity (a person, organisation or domain), and is signed by a Certificate Authority (CA) . A certificate contains: subject name (for example www.example.com ), the subject's public key , issuer (the CA), validity dates, serial number, signature algorithm and the CA's digital signature . The main fields of an X.509 certificate. The CA's signature binds the public key to the domain name A digital certificate is a signed statement that a public key belongs to a name (such as www.example.com ). Browsers trust certificates issued by known Certificate Authorities (CAs) . Key words in this topic PKI : CAs, certificates, revocation, trust stores. CSR : certificate signing request from your server. TLS/HTTPS : HTTP over an encrypted, authenticated channel. In this section 8.5.1 PKI and certificate types 8.5.2 How a browser trusts an HTTPS site 8.5.3 Practical: HTTPS certificates"},{"t":"8.5.1 PKI and certificate types","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-5-1.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.5.1 PKI and certificate types Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) PKI is the system of CAs, registration authorities, certificates, revocation lists ( CRL /OCSP) and policies that issues and manages certificates. Browsers and operating systems contain a list of trusted root CAs . A chain of trust runs from the server certificate to an intermediate CA to a trusted root CA. Chain of trust: the browser follows the signatures from the server certificate up to a root CA it already trusts Certificate types Domain Validated (DV): proves control of a domain; quick and cheap (for example Let's Encrypt , which is free). Organisation Validated (OV) / Extended Validation (EV): CA also verifies the organisation. Self-signed: signed by itself; fine for labs and internal testing, but browsers show warnings. Wildcard ( *.example.com ) and multi-domain (SAN) certificates. PKI pieces You generate a key pair and a CSR containing your public key and the domain name. A CA validates that you control the domain and signs a certificate. Clients trust the CA (built into the OS/browser). Intermediate CAs form a chain to a root. Types you will meet: Domain Validated (DV) for normal HTTPS, wildcard ( *.example.com ), and organisation-validated certificates for higher assurance. Remember Certificate = CA-signed binding of identity ↔ public key. You prove control of the domain to obtain one."},{"t":"8.5.2 How a browser trusts an HTTPS site","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-5-2.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.5.2 How a browser trusts an HTTPS site The browser connects to the server on port 443 and says which TLS versions and ciphers it supports. The server sends its certificate . The browser checks the CA signature and chain, the validity dates , and that the certificate name matches the domain . The browser and server agree on a session key using asymmetric key exchange. Both switch to symmetric encryption (for example AES) for the rest of the session. Simplified TLS handshake: the server proves its identity with a certificate, both sides agree a session key, then all data is encrypted Lab vs production trust Browsers trust public CAs automatically. A lab self-signed certificate is fine for practice if you accept the warning or import the cert into the trust store. Never tell end users to ignore certificate warnings on real systems — that trains them to approve attacks. What the browser checks Is the certificate chain rooted in a trusted CA? Is the certificate past its validity dates? Does the name match the site you typed ( CN / SAN )? Has it been revoked (CRL/OCSP), when checked? Then TLS finishes and the padlock appears. If any check fails, users see a warning. Self-signed certificates fail the CA trust check unless you manually install them (labs only). Remember Trust flows from root CA → intermediate → server certificate. Name mismatch and expiry are common lab mistakes."},{"t":"8.5.3 Practical: HTTPS certificates","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-5-3.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.5.3 Practical: HTTPS certificates Practical: enable HTTPS with Let's Encrypt (Ubuntu + Apache) Terminal Copy sudo apt install certbot python3-certbot-apache -y sudo certbot --apache -d www.example.com sudo certbot renew --dry-run This requires a real domain pointing to your server and ports 80/443 open. Practical: create and inspect a self-signed certificate Terminal Copy openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout key.pem -out cert.pem -days 365 openssl x509 -in cert.pem -text -noout Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 8.6 hands-on lab and the 8.7 review questions . After HTTPS works Redirect HTTP to HTTPS. Check the certificate chain in a browser from another machine. Calendar a renewal test; short-lived certs fail suddenly when automation breaks. Lock down admin panels so they are not public on port 443 without auth. Let's Encrypt in practice Certbot (or similar) proves you own the domain (HTTP or DNS challenge), obtains a short-lived DV certificate, installs it in the web server, and can renew automatically. You still must open ports 80/443 and point DNS A/AAAA records at the server. Tip: certificates expire (Let's Encrypt in ~90 days). Always test renewals ( certbot renew --dry-run ) or HTTPS will suddenly fail. Remember Automated CAs make HTTPS free and routine; automation of renewal is part of the job."},{"t":"8.6 Hands-on lab: Firewalls and cryptography","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-lab.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.6 Hands-on lab: Firewalls and cryptography Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. Ethics: scan, test and attack only machines in your own lab. Scanning other people's systems without written permission is illegal in many countries. Enable UFW on an Ubuntu VM with default deny; allow only SSH and HTTP; test from another machine with nmap (own lab only). Create a firewall rule on Windows to block ping (ICMP), test, then remove it. Encrypt a file with a password using openssl enc -aes-256-cbc -salt -pbkdf2 -in a.txt -out a.enc , then decrypt it. Generate an RSA key pair, sign a file with the private key, and verify with the public key ( openssl dgst -sha256 -sign and -verify ). Open a HTTPS site in a browser, view the certificate and write down issuer, validity and the chain. Terminal Copy # encrypt and decrypt a file with a password (AES-256) openssl enc -aes-256-cbc -salt -pbkdf2 -in a.txt -out a.enc openssl enc -d -aes-256-cbc -pbkdf2 -in a.enc -out a-decrypted.txt # create a key pair, sign a file and verify the signature openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 -out private.pem openssl pkey -in private.pem -pubout -out public.pem openssl dgst -sha256 -sign private.pem -out a.sig a.txt openssl dgst -sha256 -verify public.pem -signature a.sig a.txt"},{"t":"8.7 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-review.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.7 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. CIA triad hardening least privilege firewall stateful DMZ ACL plaintext ciphertext symmetric asymmetric public key private key AES RSA hash digital signature certificate CA PKI TLS Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. List four security settings you would apply to a new server and explain why. What does \"default deny\" mean in a firewall? Compare symmetric and asymmetric encryption (any three points). Which key does a sender use to create a digital signature, and which key does the receiver use to verify it? What problem does a digital certificate solve, and who signs it? Explain why HTTPS uses both asymmetric and symmetric cryptography. What is the purpose of a DMZ? Show answers Any four, with reasons, for example: Apply all updates: fixes known vulnerabilities that attackers scan for. Disable unused services and ports: reduces the attack surface. Strong passwords or SSH keys with MFA, no direct root login: stops brute-force and stolen-password attacks. Host firewall with default deny: only required services are reachable. Logging and monitoring with NTP time: incidents are detected and can be investigated. Tested backups (3-2-1): data can be recovered after ransomware or failure. Everything is blocked unless a rule explicitly allows it. Symmetric uses one shared key, is fast and is used for bulk data; asymmetric uses a public/private key pair, is slower and is used for key exchange and signatures; symmetric has a key-distribution problem which asymmetric solves. The sender's private key signs; the receiver uses the sender's public key to verify. It proves that a public key belongs to a named owner, preventing impersonation; it is signed by a trusted Certificate Authority. Asymmetric cryptography authenticates the server and securely agrees a key, then faster symmetric encryption protects the bulk data. A DMZ isolates public-facing servers from the internal LAN so a compromised server does not give direct access to internal systems. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 8 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 8: Network Security","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08.html","x":"Unit 8 Network Security Protecting servers and networks: hardening, firewalls and DMZs, symmetric and asymmetric encryption, hashing, digital signatures and certificates. What you will learn After this unit you can: Explain the significance of security settings on servers and networks. Describe the types of firewall and configure basic firewall rules. Explain symmetric and asymmetric encryption and where each is used. Explain hashing, digital signatures and digital certificates. Topics in this unit 8.1 Why security settings matter 2 diagrams ✓ 8.2 Firewall 1 diagram ✓ 8.2.1 Types of firewall ✓ 8.2.2 Rule design, DMZ and ACLs 1 diagram ✓ 8.2.3 Configuring firewalls: UFW, Windows and iptables 2 command examples ✓ 8.3 Cryptography basics ✓ 8.3.1 Symmetric encryption 1 diagram ✓ 8.3.2 Asymmetric (public-key) encryption 1 diagram ✓ 8.3.3 Comparison and the hybrid approach 1 diagram ✓ 8.3.4 Hashing 1 command example · 1 diagram ✓ 8.4 Digital signature 1 diagram ✓ 8.5 Digital certificate 1 diagram ✓ 8.5.1 PKI and certificate types 1 diagram ✓ 8.5.2 How a browser trusts an HTTPS site 1 diagram ✓ 8.5.3 Practical: HTTPS certificates 2 command examples ✓ 8.6 Hands-on lab: Firewalls and cryptography 1 command example ✓ 8.7 Review: key terms and self-check 7 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"9.1 Troubleshooting methodology","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-1.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.1 Troubleshooting methodology 1 Identify the problem → 2 Theory of cause → 3 Test the theory → 4 Plan of action → 5 Implement → 6 Verify → 7 Document Identify the problem: gather information, ask questions (who, what, when, what changed?), reproduce the fault, define the scope (one user, one subnet, everyone). Establish a theory of probable cause (start with the simple and common). Test the theory to confirm; if wrong, form a new theory or escalate. Establish a plan of action and consider the impact (get approval for production changes). Implement the solution (change only one thing at a time). Verify full system functionality and apply preventive measures. Document findings, actions and outcomes. Approaches Top-down: start at the application layer and move down. Bottom-up: start at the physical layer and move up (best when nothing works). Divide and conquer: start at a middle layer (usually ping at layer 3) and move up or down depending on the result. Follow the path and compare with a working system ; substitute known-good parts. Three ways to work through the layers: top-down, bottom-up, or divide and conquer starting at layer 3 with ping Making the seven steps concrete Identify: \"Who is affected — one PC, one VLAN, or everyone? Did anything change (new switch, new DHCP, Windows update)?\" Reproduce the fault yourself. Theory: start with likely causes (unplugged cable, wrong gateway) before exotic ones. Test theory: swap the cable, ping the gateway, check ipconfig . One experiment at a time. Plan: for production, note downtime risk and get approval if needed. Implement: apply the fix; avoid changing five settings at once. Verify: confirm the original symptom is gone and related services still work. Document: what you saw, what fixed it, how to prevent it — future you will thank you. Which approach when? Bottom-up when the link lights are off or nothing pings. Top-down when the network seems fine but one application fails. Divide and conquer: ping gateway and a public IP first; branch up (DNS/app) or down (cable/NIC) from the result. Remember Method beats random clicking. Change one variable at a time; always verify and document."},{"t":"9.2 Layer-by-layer symptoms","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-2.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.2 Layer-by-layer symptoms Layer Typical faults Checks 1 Physical Unplugged or faulty cable, dead port, no power, damaged fibre Link lights, swap cable, cable tester, check port 2 Data link Wrong VLAN, duplex mismatch, MAC filtering, loop show interfaces , VLAN membership, switch MAC table 3 Network Wrong IP, mask or gateway, duplicate IP, routing fault, APIPA address ipconfig , ping , tracert , routing table 4 Transport Port blocked by firewall, service not listening netstat / ss , telnet host port , firewall rules 5-7 Application Service stopped, misconfiguration, certificate expired, DNS error Service status, application logs, browser errors Quick story \"Email fails.\" Ping to the mail server's IP works → not Physical/Network. nslookup mail.school.lk fails → DNS (application helper) before you rebuild SMTP. Always separate path problems from name problems from service problems . Symptoms by layer (practical view) Layer Typical symptoms First checks Physical No link light, intermittent disconnects Cable, port, power, Wi-Fi radio Data link Linked but no MAC learning / VLAN mismatch Correct VLAN, switchport mode, MAC table Network Cannot ping gateway or remote nets IP, mask, gateway, routing Transport Ping works, app port fails Firewall ports, listening service ( ss ) Application Port open but app errors URL, DNS name, app logs, certificates A fault at a lower layer breaks everything above it. That is why \"blinky lights first\" saves time when the whole PC seems dead on the network. Remember Map the symptom to a layer, then test at that layer. Connectivity (ping) succeeding does not prove the application layer is healthy."},{"t":"9.3 Command-line tools","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-3.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.3 Command-line tools Tool Windows Linux Use Show IP settings ipconfig /all ip a , ip route Check IP, mask, gateway, DNS, MAC Renew address ipconfig /release and /renew sudo dhclient -r; sudo dhclient (or sudo networkctl renew <interface> ) DHCP faults Test reachability ping ping Layer 3 connectivity and latency Trace path tracert traceroute Find where packets stop Path quality pathping mtr Loss at each hop DNS lookup nslookup dig , nslookup Test name resolution Connections and ports netstat -ano ss -tulpn See open ports, processes ARP table arp -a ip neigh IP-to-MAC mapping, duplicate IP Port test Test-NetConnection host -Port 443 nc -zv host 443 Is the service reachable? Capture traffic Wireshark tcpdump , Wireshark Deep analysis Web test browser, curl curl -I http://site Check HTTP response Service status Services, Get-Service systemctl status name Is the application running? Logs Event Viewer journalctl -xe , /var/log/* Find the error message Resources Task Manager, Get-Process top , free -h , df -h CPU, RAM, disk full Interpreting ping results Result Meaning Reply from address Layer 3 path is working Request timed out Host down, ICMP blocked, or no route back Destination host unreachable No route or ARP failure; check IP, mask and gateway General failure / transmit failed Local interface or driver problem High or variable time Congestion, wireless interference, overloaded device Tools and when to reach for them Tool Shows you Use it when ping ICMP reachability and round-trip time Basic \"can I reach X?\" tracert / traceroute Each hop on the path Where packets stop ipconfig / ip a Address, mask, gateway, DNS Suspect wrong IP config ipconfig /all DHCP, MAC, DNS details Windows deep check nslookup / dig DNS answers Names fail but IPs work netstat / ss Sockets and listeners Port or service questions arp -a / ip neigh IP↔MAC cache Local delivery / duplicate IP clues Tip: ping the loopback ( 127.0.0.1 ), then the host's own IP, then the gateway, then a public IP, then a name. Each step isolates a layer of problems. Remember Ping tests reachability, not \"the website is fine\". DNS tools diagnose name failures; address tools diagnose IP failures."},{"t":"9.4 The \"cannot reach the Internet\" sequence","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-4.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.4 The \"cannot reach the Internet\" sequence The standard sequence works outwards from your own computer, so the first test that fails shows where the fault is ping 127.0.0.1 - is the TCP/IP software working on this computer? ipconfig /all - is the IP address valid (not 169.254.x.x)? Is the gateway correct? ping the default gateway - is the local network working? ping 8.8.8.8 - is the Internet reachable by IP? ping www.google.com - does DNS work? If this fails while step 4 works, the fault is DNS . tracert 8.8.8.8 - where does the path stop? Write the evidence At each step record the command and the result ( ping 192.168.1.1 → destination host unreachable). That log becomes your theory test and your ticket notes. Guessing without recording leads to looping through the same checks. \"Cannot reach the Internet\" decision path Can you ping 127.0.0.1 ? No → OS/network stack problem. Do you have an IP address? 169.254.x.x → DHCP failed. No address → NIC/driver/disabled interface. Ping the default gateway . Fail → wrong VLAN/cable/gateway IP or gateway down. Ping a public IP (for example 1.1.1.1 ). Fail → routing/NAT/ISP/firewall outbound issue. Ping a name ( ping www.google.com ) or use nslookup . Fail while IP works → DNS problem. Browser still fails while ping works → proxy settings, firewall port 80/443, or application issue. Write down which step first failed; that is your theory for 9.1 . Remember Local → gateway → public IP → name → application. APIPA means DHCP trouble; names failing alone means DNS trouble."},{"t":"9.5 Common faults and fixes","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-5.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.5 Common faults and fixes Symptom Likely cause Fix 169.254.x.x address DHCP server unreachable Check cable/Wi-Fi, DHCP service and scope; renew lease Can ping IP but not name DNS problem Check client DNS settings, DNS server status, nslookup , flush cache Can reach local hosts, not Internet Wrong or missing gateway, ISP fault Correct gateway, check router and WAN link Intermittent connection Bad cable, Wi-Fi interference, duplex mismatch Replace cable, change channel, set speed/duplex IP conflict message Two devices with same IP Use DHCP reservations, find the device with arp -a Website down, server pings fine Web service stopped or port blocked systemctl status , check firewall and port 80/443 Cannot SSH or RDP Service off, firewall, wrong port or credentials Check service, ss -tulpn , firewall rules, logs Certificate warning Expired or mismatched certificate, wrong date on client Renew certificate, check name and system clock Server slow CPU, RAM or disk exhausted, too many connections top , df -h , find the process, add resources Server will not boot Bad disk, corrupt update, wrong boot order Boot recovery media, check disk health, restore from backup Application crashes after update Incompatible version, missing dependency Read logs, roll back, reinstall dependency Cannot send email Wrong MX/SMTP settings, port 25 blocked, SPF failure Check DNS records, mail logs, firewall Frequent faults and fast checks Fault Clue Fix direction Bad/wrong cable or port No link light Replace cable, other port Wrong VLAN Link up, no DHCP, cannot ping gateway Correct access VLAN Duplicate IP Intermittent reachability, ARP warnings Find clash; reserve DHCP Wrong mask/gateway Local OK or not; Internet fails Correct IP plan DNS wrong IPs ping, names fail Fix DNS servers / records DHCP down APIPA addresses Repair DHCP service/relay Firewall blocking Ping OK, app port closed Adjust host/network rules Expired VPN/cert Only remote users fail Renew cert; check VPN logs Remember Match clue → likely layer → single corrective action → retest."},{"t":"9.6 Operating system and application troubleshooting","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-6.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.6 Operating system and application troubleshooting 1 Read the logs → 2 Check config syntax → 3 Check resources → 4 Check permissions → 5 Check recent changes Check the logs first: the exact error message usually points to the cause. On Linux use sudo journalctl -u apache2 --since \"1 hour ago\" and tail -f /var/log/syslog ; on Windows open Event Viewer (System and Application). Check the configuration syntax before restarting a service ( apache2ctl configtest , named-checkconf , nginx -t ). Check resources: disk full ( df -h ) is a very common cause of strange failures. Check permissions and ownership of files and users. Check recent changes: updates, new software, configuration edits. Roll back if needed. Boot problems: use Safe Mode or Recovery Environment (Windows), rescue mode or GRUB recovery (Linux); restore from backup if the system is damaged. Keep baseline records of normal performance so abnormal behaviour is easy to recognise. When the network is fine but the service is not Is the service running ? ( systemctl status … , Services.msc) Is it listening on the expected port and interface? ( ss -tulpn ) Does the host firewall allow it? Do app logs show bind errors, bad config, or full disks? For web: correct vhost , document root, and certificate name? For clients: proxy settings, stale DNS cache ( ipconfig /flushdns ), wrong hosts file entry? Compare with a working PC (same VLAN) to see whether the fault is machine-specific or network-wide. Remember Application troubleshooting starts after basic IP connectivity succeeds. Service up ≠ port open ≠ reachable through firewall — check each."},{"t":"9.7 Documentation","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-7.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.7 Documentation Write a trouble ticket : date, reporter, symptom, affected systems, tests performed, root cause, fix, time to resolve and follow-up action. A knowledge base of past fixes saves time later. Sample trouble ticket Field Example Ticket / date #0142 · 14 March 2026, 10:20 Reported by Accounts office, PC ACC-03 Symptom Cannot open websites; email in Outlook still works Affected systems One PC only Tests performed ping 8.8.8.8 succeeds; ping www.google.com fails; ipconfig /all shows DNS server 192.168.1.100 Root cause DNS server address typed incorrectly (should be 192.168.1.10) Fix Set the adapter back to obtain DNS automatically (DHCP); ipconfig /flushdns Time to resolve 15 minutes Follow-up Remove local administrator rights so users cannot change network settings Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 9.8 hands-on lab and the 9.9 review questions . What to write in a ticket Good documentation turns a one-off save into institutional knowledge: Reported problem (user words) and actual impact Environment (who, where, IP, VLAN, time) Steps to reproduce and tests you ran (commands + results) Root cause (not only the symptom) Fix applied and verification Prevention (monitoring, config standard, user guidance) Even a short lab notebook with timestamps helps assessments and real jobs. Templates beat blank pages when you are busy. Sample line: \"10:40 – PC Lab2-05 has APIPA. Cable OK. DHCP scope exhausted. Freed unused leases; PC received 10.20.1.45. Documented scope capacity warning.\" Remember If it is not written down, the organisation will solve it again from scratch. Record cause and prevention, not only \"fixed\"."},{"t":"9.8 Hands-on lab: Fault-finding exercise","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-lab.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.8 Hands-on lab: Fault-finding exercise Your teacher or a partner breaks one thing on a working lab network. Find and fix each fault using the method above and write a ticket for each: Wrong subnet mask on a PC. Wrong default gateway. DNS server address typed incorrectly. Web service stopped on the server. Firewall rule blocking port 80. Disconnected or wrong-VLAN port. Disk full or stopped service on a Linux server. Use the method: follow the \"cannot reach the Internet\" sequence for each fault and record it in a ticket like the sample in 9.7 ."},{"t":"9.9 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-review.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.9 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. Top-down bottom-up divide and conquer ping traceroute nslookup netstat log baseline root cause ticket Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. List the steps of the CompTIA-style troubleshooting method in order. A user can ping 8.8.8.8 but cannot open websites by name. What is the likely fault and how do you confirm it? Which tool shows where along a path packets stop? A Linux web server is not responding. Name five checks you would do. What does an IP address of 169.254.12.5 on a client indicate? Why should you change only one thing at a time? Show answers Identify the problem (gather information, reproduce, define the scope). Establish a theory of probable cause. Test the theory to determine the cause. Establish a plan of action and identify its impact. Implement the solution (or escalate). Verify full system functionality and apply preventive measures. Document findings, actions and outcomes. DNS failure; confirm with nslookup www.example.com and by changing the DNS server (for example to 8.8.8.8). tracert (Windows) or traceroute (Linux). Any five, for example: ping the server and check its IP settings ( ip a ). systemctl status apache2 to see whether the web service is running. ss -tulpn to check it is listening on port 80/443. sudo ufw status to check the firewall allows the port. journalctl -u apache2 and /var/log/apache2/error.log for error messages; apache2ctl configtest for syntax. df -h , free -h and top for a full disk, low memory or high CPU. nslookup / dig to check the name resolves to the right address. The client failed to get a DHCP address and self-assigned an APIPA address. So you can tell which change fixed the problem or caused a new one and can easily undo it. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 9 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 9: Network Troubleshooting","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09.html","x":"Unit 9 Network Troubleshooting A systematic way to find and fix faults: the troubleshooting method, layer-by-layer symptoms, command-line tools, common faults and documenting the fix. What you will learn After this unit you can: Apply a systematic troubleshooting method. Use command-line tools to diagnose network, DNS and server problems. Troubleshoot operating system, application and network issues using logs and tools. Document faults and solutions. Topics in this unit 9.1 Troubleshooting methodology 1 diagram ✓ 9.2 Layer-by-layer symptoms ✓ 9.3 Command-line tools ✓ 9.4 The \"cannot reach the Internet\" sequence 1 diagram ✓ 9.5 Common faults and fixes ✓ 9.6 Operating system and application troubleshooting ✓ 9.7 Documentation ✓ 9.8 Hands-on lab: Fault-finding exercise ✓ 9.9 Review: key terms and self-check 6 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"Final Revision and Self-Assessment","c":"Final Revision","u":"revision.html","x":"Final Revision Final Revision and Self-Assessment Check your understanding of the whole module with 100 multiple-choice questions, 100 structured questions and a topic checklist. How to use this section Work through the questions after finishing all the units. Every answer links back to the topic it tests, so use your wrong answers as a revision list. Topics in this section R.1 Multiple-Choice Questions ✓ R.2 Structured Questions ✓ R.3 Topic Checklist ✓"},{"t":"R.1 Multiple-Choice Questions","c":"Final Revision","u":"revision-mcq.html","x":"practice questions answers quiz exam revision"},{"t":"R.2 Structured Questions","c":"Final Revision","u":"revision-structured.html","x":"practice questions answers quiz exam revision"},{"t":"R.3 Topic Checklist","c":"Final Revision","u":"revision-checklist.html","x":"Final Revision and Self-Assessment R.3 Topic Checklist Tick each outcome when you can explain it and do it without looking at your notes. Your ticks are saved in this browser. Each link opens the unit. Unit 1: Data Communication Fundamentals Define data communication and list its five components. Unit 1 Distinguish transmission modes, data flow directions and signal types. Unit 1 Compare transmission media. Unit 1 Explain bandwidth, throughput, latency and the basic ideas of multiplexing and switching. Unit 1 Unit 2: Computer Network Fundamentals Define a computer network and describe its purposes. Unit 2 Classify networks by size (PAN, LAN, MAN, WAN) and by role (peer-to-peer, client-server). Unit 2 Compare physical and logical topologies. Unit 2 Explain the Internet, intranets and extranets. Unit 2 Describe wired and wireless LAN technologies and basic network design. Unit 2 Unit 3: Network Devices and Protocols Identify common network devices and state the function of each. Unit 3 Install and configure a switch, router, access point and basic firewall. Unit 3 Explain what a network protocol is and describe the most common protocols. Unit 3 Describe the OSI and TCP/IP reference models and map devices and protocols to layers. Unit 3 Unit 4: Addressing Network Devices Describe the structure and purpose of a MAC address. Unit 4 Explain IPv4 and IPv6 addressing, classes, public and private ranges. Unit 4 Convert between binary and decimal. Unit 4 Design and calculate subnets using subnet masks, CIDR and VLSM. Unit 4 Unit 5: Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration Explain what a server is and why server operating systems and application software are required. Unit 5 Compare dedicated, shared, VPS, cloud and virtual servers and select a suitable one. Unit 5 Install and configure a server operating system. Unit 5 Describe and use server access methods. Unit 5 Install common server applications and tools. Unit 5 Explain ports and manage them securely. Unit 5 Unit 6: Virtual Private Network (VPN) Explain what a VPN is and why it is used. Unit 6 Compare site-to-site and remote-access VPNs. Unit 6 Describe tunnelling and common VPN protocols. Unit 6 Set up a simple VPN. Unit 6 Unit 7: Domain Name System (DNS) Explain the purpose of DNS and describe its hierarchical architecture. Unit 7 Describe the name-resolution process and record types. Unit 7 Install, configure and manage a DNS server. Unit 7 Explain how to obtain and register a domain name. Unit 7 Unit 8: Network Security Explain the significance of security settings on servers and networks. Unit 8 Describe the types of firewall and configure basic firewall rules. Unit 8 Explain symmetric and asymmetric encryption and where each is used. Unit 8 Explain hashing, digital signatures and digital certificates. Unit 8 Unit 9: Network Troubleshooting Apply a systematic troubleshooting method. Unit 9 Use command-line tools to diagnose network, DNS and server problems. Unit 9 Troubleshoot operating system, application and network issues using logs and tools. Unit 9 Document faults and solutions. Unit 9"},{"t":"Appendix A Glossary","c":"Appendices","u":"appendix-a.html","x":"Appendices A Glossary Key terms used in this module, in alphabetical order. Term Meaning ACL Access Control List: ordered permit/deny rules on a router or switch AES Advanced Encryption Standard; the common symmetric cipher APIPA Automatic Private IP Addressing: a 169.254.x.x address a client gives itself when DHCP fails ARP Address Resolution Protocol; maps IP to MAC on a LAN Bandwidth Maximum data capacity of a link Broadcast domain Part of a network reached by a broadcast frame; routers separate broadcast domains CA Certificate Authority; trusted body that signs certificates CIDR Classless Inter-Domain Routing; prefix notation such as /24 Collision domain Part of a network where frames can collide; each switch port is its own collision domain CSMA/CA Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Avoidance, used by Wi-Fi CSMA/CD Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Detection, used by early half-duplex Ethernet Default gateway The router interface a host sends traffic to for any destination outside its own subnet DHCP Protocol that automatically assigns IP settings Digital certificate An X.509 document, signed by a CA, that binds a public key to an identity Digital signature A value created with the sender's private key that proves origin and integrity DMZ Network zone for public-facing servers DNS System that translates names to IP addresses DNSSEC DNS Security Extensions: signs DNS records so answers can be verified DORA Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge: the four DHCP messages Encapsulation Adding a header (and trailer) at each layer as data moves down the stack Firewall Device or software filtering traffic by rules FQDN Fully Qualified Domain Name Gateway Router that links a network to others Hash Fixed-length one-way digest of data Hypervisor Software that runs virtual machines IPsec Network-layer VPN and security protocol suite Latency The time data takes to travel from source to destination MAC address 48-bit hardware address of a network interface NAT Translates private addresses to public ones OSI model Seven-layer reference model for communication OUI Organisationally Unique Identifier: the first 24 bits of a MAC address, identifying the manufacturer Packet Layer 3 data unit PDU Protocol Data Unit: the data unit at a layer (segment, packet, frame, bits) PKI Public Key Infrastructure; system managing certificates Port 16-bit number identifying a service on a host RAID Combining disks for speed and/or redundancy Recursive resolver DNS server that finds an answer for a client by querying other servers, then caches it Registrar Company that sells domain names RSA Widely used asymmetric algorithm SLAAC Stateless Address Autoconfiguration: IPv6 hosts build their own address from the router's prefix Socket An IP address plus a port number, for example 192.168.1.10:443 SSH Secure Shell; encrypted remote access Subnet mask Separates network and host parts of an IP address TCP / UDP Reliable / unreliable transport protocols Throughput The data rate actually achieved on a link, always at or below the bandwidth TLS Transport Layer Security; encrypts HTTPS and many other protocols Topology The physical or logical arrangement of devices and links in a network Trunk A switch link that carries several VLANs, with 802.1Q tags TTL Time To Live: how long a DNS answer may be cached VLAN Logical network on a switch VLSM Variable Length Subnet Masking VPN Virtual Private Network VPS Virtual Private Server Zone Part of the DNS namespace managed by one set of servers"},{"t":"Appendix B Quick Reference Cards","c":"Appendices","u":"appendix-b.html","x":"Appendices B Quick Reference Cards On this page Subnetting cheat sheet Private and special IPv4 addresses Common port numbers Which key for which job Troubleshooting order Everyday commands Subnetting cheat sheet Prefix Mask Block size Usable hosts /8 255.0.0.0 - 16,777,214 /16 255.255.0.0 - 65,534 /23 255.255.254.0 2 (third octet) 510 /24 255.255.255.0 1 (third octet) 254 /25 255.255.255.128 128 126 /26 255.255.255.192 64 62 /27 255.255.255.224 32 30 /28 255.255.255.240 16 14 /29 255.255.255.248 8 6 /30 255.255.255.252 4 2 Formulas: usable hosts = 2^h − 2 · subnets = 2^s · block size = 256 − mask octet. Powers of two: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256. Practise with the subnet calculator and the VLSM planner . Private and special IPv4 addresses Range Use 10.0.0.0/8 Private (RFC 1918) 172.16.0.0/12 Private (172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255) 192.168.0.0/16 Private 127.0.0.0/8 Loopback (127.0.0.1) 169.254.0.0/16 APIPA: DHCP failed 224.0.0.0/4 Multicast Common port numbers Port Service Port Service 20/21 FTP 143 IMAP 22 SSH, SFTP, SCP 161 SNMP (UDP) 23 Telnet (insecure) 389 / 636 LDAP / LDAPS 25 / 587 SMTP 443 HTTPS 53 DNS (UDP, TCP) 445 SMB 67/68 DHCP (UDP) 3306 MySQL 80 HTTP 3389 RDP 110 POP3 51820 WireGuard (UDP) Which key for which job Task Key used Encrypt for secrecy receiver's public key Decrypt receiver's private key Sign sender's private key Verify a signature sender's public key Troubleshooting order 1 ping 127.0.0.1 → 2 ipconfig /all → 3 ping gateway → 4 ping 8.8.8.8 → 5 ping a name → 6 tracert Everyday commands Task Windows Linux IP settings ipconfig /all ip a , ip route Reachability ping ping Path tracert traceroute , mtr DNS lookup nslookup dig , nslookup Listening ports netstat -ano ss -tulpn ARP table arp -a ip neigh Port test Test-NetConnection host -Port 443 nc -zv host 443 Services Get-Service systemctl status name Logs Event Viewer journalctl -xe , /var/log"},{"t":"Appendix C Lab Set-up and Study Plan","c":"Appendices","u":"appendix-c.html","x":"Appendices C Lab Set-up and Study Plan On this page Recommended lab set-up Self-study roadmap A routine for each unit Recommended lab set-up Everything in this module can be practised with free software on one computer: A computer with at least 8 GB RAM and virtualization enabled. VirtualBox or VMware Player to run virtual machines. Ubuntu Server LTS ISO and a Windows Server evaluation ISO. Cisco Packet Tracer (free from Cisco Networking Academy) for simulating routers and switches. Wireshark for packet capture. PuTTY or the built-in ssh client. Study tip: do the labs. Networking and servers are practical skills; reading alone is not enough. Each unit ends with a hands-on lab page. Self-study roadmap A seven-week plan for working through the module on your own: Week Study Lab to complete 1 Units 1 and 2 Labs 1 and 2 2 Unit 3 Lab 3 3 Unit 4 (practise 20 subnetting problems daily) Lab 4 4 Unit 5 Lab 5 5 Units 6 and 7 Labs 6 and 7 6 Unit 8 Lab 8 7 Unit 9 and revision Lab 9, full mock test A student who reads each unit, completes its lab and answers the self-check questions has covered every point of the module. A routine for each unit Read the unit overview and its learning outcomes. Work through each short topic page, trying every command and interactive lab. Complete the hands-on lab and keep notes or screenshots. Answer the review questions before opening the answers. Tick the outcomes in the topic checklist ."},{"t":"Appendix D References and Free Resources","c":"Appendices","u":"appendix-d.html","x":"Appendices D References and Free Resources On this page Free online resources Books Software used in the labs Free online resources Cisco Networking Academy: free courses (Networking Basics) and Packet Tracer Ubuntu Server documentation Microsoft Learn: Windows Server documentation BIND 9 Administrator Reference Manual Wireshark User's Guide and sample captures Let's Encrypt documentation ICANN (domain name system) IANA root zone and port number registry WireGuard quick start The Linux Command Line by William Shotts (free online book) Books Kurose, J. and Ross, K. Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach . Pearson. Tanenbaum, A. and Wetherall, D. Computer Networks . Pearson. CompTIA Network+ and CompTIA Server+ study guides. Shotts, W. The Linux Command Line . No Starch Press (free online edition). Software used in the labs Software Used for VirtualBox or VMware Player Running server virtual machines Ubuntu Server LTS Linux server labs (SSH, Apache, BIND9, UFW, WireGuard) Windows Server evaluation Server Manager, DNS and DHCP roles, Windows Firewall Cisco Packet Tracer Simulating switches, routers and VLANs Wireshark Capturing and analysing packets PuTTY or the built-in ssh client Remote command-line access OpenSSL Encryption, signatures and certificates (included with Linux and Git for Windows)"},{"t":"Appendices","c":"Appendices","u":"appendices.html","x":"Appendices Appendices Glossary, quick reference cards, your lab set-up and study plan, and free resources for further study. Topics in this section Appendix A Glossary ✓ Appendix B Quick Reference Cards ✓ Appendix C Lab Set-up and Study Plan ✓ Appendix D References and Free Resources ✓"}];
